$$\\color{lightgrey}12$$v_2$$10$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}7$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$7$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}15$$\\color{lightgrey}6$$v_4$$3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$-$$8$$15$$-$$-$$2$$v_6$$\\color{lightgrey}12$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$6$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}2$$\\color{lightgrey}-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_4$$3$$v_1 - v_2$$10$$v_2 - v_3$$7$$v_3 - v_6$$6$ |\n| **Step 6.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $2$ located at the intersection of row $v_5$ and column $v_6$. So, we mark column $v_5$, cancel out row $v_5$, and add edge $v_6-v_5$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$3$$4$$2$$6$$5$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_6$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}10$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}12$$v_2$$10$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}7$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$7$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}15$$\\color{lightgrey}6$$v_4$$3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}15$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$2$$v_6$$\\color{lightgrey}12$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$6$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}2$$\\color{lightgrey}-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_4$$3$$v_1 - v_2$$10$$v_2 - v_3$$7$$v_3 - v_6$$6$$v_6 - v_5$$2$ |\n\nThe total weight of the resulting minimum spanning tree is\n\n$$\n3 + 10 + 7 + 6 + 2 = 28\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110374 1069 "e-17212" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110374 1071 "Consider the equation\n$(5s + 4)x = 9x$\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110374 1072 #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110374 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = bx$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b$, then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx$. In this case,\n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5s &= 5 \\\\\ns &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110375 1069 "e-1802" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110375 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=3(2^x)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110375 1072 #uuid "e5680653-d529-58df-b243-7447db0a750a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110375 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y=2^{x}$:\n\n![](images/f2/f2200a327b3c85d190638befc03655296be954d668d96a55d060d4d9b3791148.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of $3$. The $y$-intercept becomes\n\n$$\n\\left(0,3 \\right)\n$$\n\nand the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/ab/abcd2227ca143b42f5dc09523c4a0e944af2316e048fdea35c507bcbd1bb7310.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110376 1069 "e-1803" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110376 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110376 1072 #uuid "edf79f97-9325-55a0-a78b-133bba524177" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110376 1074 "The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nhas a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$, grows rapidly for $x<0$, and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0$.\n\nWhen $x=-1$, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-1} =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, another point on the graph is\n\n$$\n\\left(-1, 4\\right)\n$$\n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/4c/4cbb5007cf380afe964bea8b5970e4a37924f38401ec24cb856b02c0510676eb.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110377 1069 "e-18852" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110377 1071 "Consider the equation\n$(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12$\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110377 1072 #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110377 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c$, and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x$, and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x$, we have\n$k + 4 = 6$.\nSolving this equation for $k$, we get\n$k|= 2$.\n- Equating the constants, we have\n$3r = 12$.\nSolving this equation for $r$, we get\n$r|= 4$.\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4$, we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110378 1069 "e-18854" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110378 1071 "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n1. $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n2. $-3x = -4x + 6$\n3. $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110378 1072 #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110378 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n$4x + 1|= 4x + 1; 4x + 1 - 4x|= 4x + 1 - 4x; 1|= 1$.\nThis equation is true for all values of $x$. Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n- For the second equation, we have\n$-3x|=-4x + 6; - 3x + 4x|=-4x + 6 + 4x; x|= 6$.\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6$.\n- For the third equation, we have\n$2x + 7|= 2x - 1; 2x + 7 - 2x|= 2x - 1 - 2x; 7|=-1$.\nThis equation is false for all values of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110379 1069 "e-19740" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110379 1071 "| **$x$** | $4$ | $12$ | $20$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$f(x)$** | $9$ | $25$ | $9$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y = f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110379 1072 #uuid "04877936-271e-5fcb-a1ea-aa39c52482b6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110379 1074 "Notice that the values of $f(x)$ at the points $x=4$ and $x=20$ are equal.\n\n$$\nf(4) = f(20) = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola's axis of symmetry must be equidistant from the lines $x=4$ and $x=20$. We can find this by taking the average of these two values.\n\nIf the parabolas vertex is $(h,k)$, then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{4 + 20}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{2} \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110380 1069 "e-2553" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110380 1071 "![](images/8b/8bc5a70179a245c26f766e7c0efb36b508d259746a6a92b02c4d423d39940d5a.png)\n\nGiven the number line above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $AC=BD$\n2. $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{CD}$\n3. $\\overline{AB}$ is congruent to $\\overline{BD}$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110380 1072 #uuid "8d23b33d-46ad-56af-853c-353c306c7af0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110380 1074 "Let's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that $AC= BD=5$ units.\n- Statement II is true. Since $AB=CD=3$ units, we have that $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{CD}$.\n- Statement III is false since $AB=3$, while $BD = 5$. So, $\\overline{AB}\\ncong \\overline{BD}$.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110381 1069 "e-2554" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110381 1071 "Which of the line segments shown below are congruent?\n\n![](images/fe/fea89643ee848c5cce4ea9844da71fae26f4799c4dbbe5795086a5365fbf3ec9.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110381 1072 #uuid "a53bc0ac-2deb-53f9-a4cf-2ac02b903cd2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110381 1074 "For two segments to be congruent, they must have the same length.\n\nSo, let's determine the length of each segment in the diagram. We see that:\n\n- $AB = 3$ units\n- $CD=EF = 4$ units\n- $GH = 2$ units\n\nTherefore, the only pair of congruent line segments is $\\overline{CD}\\cong\\overline{EF}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110382 1069 "e-2556" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110382 1071 "For the diagram shown below, which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](images/79/793dacde520b1be4aa4a1e023760a06d1db5fe23266ec1277efe948e8ca04ab1.png)\n\n1. The segments $\\overline{BC}$ and $\\overline{BD}$ are congruent\n2. $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{BE}$\n3. $BE = 4\\,\\textrm{cm}$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110382 1072 #uuid "e5cdb5e0-0ea7-5a96-81d4-0b53e2ad2f9e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110382 1074 "Let's look at the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. In the picture, both $\\overline{BC}$ and $\\overline{BD}$ have the same double hash marks, so\n$\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{BD}$.\n- Statement II is false. From the picture, we can see that $\\overline{BE}$ and $\\overline{BC}$ have different hash marks, so\n$\\overline{BC} \\not\\cong \\overline{BE}$.\n- Statement III is false. According to the picture, we have that $\\overline{BE}$ and $\\overline{BD}$ have different hash marks, so\n$\\overline{BE} \\not\\cong \\overline{BD}$.\nHence, ${BE} \\neq {BD}$, and since ${BD} = 4\\,\\textrm{cm}$, we have $BE \\neq 4\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110383 1069 "e-2557" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110383 1071 "Which number corresponds to the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ shown below?\n\n![](images/88/8850f207b51c6789073a0fb83b221a93290ade40b3f148248cac803fc1486452.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110383 1072 #uuid "f46e0c53-c110-5990-b3ea-5dd27991cdc9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110383 1074 "The point $A$ corresponds to the number $x_1=-1$ while $B$ corresponds to $x_2=5$.\n\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to the midpoint $M$, we use the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1 + 5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=2$.\n\n![](images/07/07d9c940fbe2e5a14c5dc3fc1b46add8947f9ad0df7151f8693793cf70359688.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110384 1069 "e-2558" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110384 1071 "Given that $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, what is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/bf/bf08731ef8b88b55c1b3df41e58443beefb5e1a69b46757fbfb18c22d6c0b6b1.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110384 1072 #uuid "7c337d65-54d7-5264-b750-1a7f7bcd07f4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110384 1074 "Since $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overline{PM}$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2PM &= PQ \\\\\n2(4x - 3) &= 5x + 9 \\\\\n8x - 6 &= 5x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x - 6 - 5x &= 5x + 9 - 5x \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $6$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 6 + 6 &= 9 + 6 \\\\\n3x &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110385 1069 "e-2559" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110385 1071 "The point $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{LN}$. What is the value of $x$ if $MN=4x+1$ centimeters and $LM=3x+4$ centimeters?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110385 1072 #uuid "6e321a78-087f-5962-a88c-e61bdc10de9f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110385 1074 "We have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/60/60e50f27aef61d5a97f9165dab922fa25cd80c114a0b11bbfbcdfe4c0fdf90e9.png)\n\nSince $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{LN}$, the two segments $\\overline{LM}$ and $\\overline{MN}$ have the same length. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLM &= MN \\\\\n3x + 4 &= 4x + 1 \\\\\n3x + 4 - 3x &= 4x + 1 - 3x \\\\\n4 &= x + 1 \\\\\n4 - 1 &= x + 1 - 1 \\\\\n3 &= x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 3\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\n." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110386 1069 "e-2560" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110386 1071 "For the diagram depicted below, which number corresponds to the point $R$ if $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{RS}$?\n\n![](images/d4/d4b49c7832633378a281295850be72f2ff664475b79f9b692591411c2d9ed6c4.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110386 1072 #uuid "c79d8054-b4f5-57e3-b816-5ac07ccfa90d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110386 1074 "From the picture, we can see that the point $S$ corresponds to the number $x_2=3$ while the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=1$.\n\nTo find the number $x_1$ corresponding to the point $R$, we first set up an equation using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{x_{1} + 3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= \\frac{x_{1} + 3}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 1 &= x_{1} + 3 \\\\\n2 &= x_{1} + 3 \\\\\n2 - 3 &= x_{1} \\\\\n- 1 &= x_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $R$ corresponds to $x_1=-1$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4897cf174e575e4999ba9605c9ddd03b3820c63fd77a1d7f87fdcaac171e35b.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110387 1069 "e-2577" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110387 1071 "In the figure below, determine $m\\angle{POR}$.\n\n![](images/0b/0bb38727216cc66882b7ed55fa59c3c700243ad18eb035a3e19a73eeffa81a2a.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110387 1072 #uuid "361d828c-ec57-5baa-81c6-321e0dc723e2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110387 1074 "![](images/70/70bb672243a7689bd08c2d03ebe19570fdb148182dd7b5137da5be4b91724f03.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\angle{POQ}$ and $\\angle{QOR}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overrightarrow{OQ}$.\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠POR &= m∠POQ + m∠QOR \\\\\n&= 68^{∘} + 37^{∘} \\\\\n&= 105^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110388 1069 "e-2578" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110388 1071 "In the figure below, $m \\angle BAE = {110}^{\\circ}$. Find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/24/245dbaa430eb99aee193110fcd6df2844cfd7cffd2363c561337ac74d18a1fb5.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110388 1072 #uuid "4ad9432d-82fc-58e6-b586-f16474fa18f3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110388 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $\\angle CAD$.\n\nThe measure of $\\angle CAD$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(\\angle BAE)$ and the two smaller known angles $(\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle DAE)$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 110^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 20^\\circ = 50^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110389 1069 "e-2579" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110389 1071 "Compute the measures of the shaded angles in the figure below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d39f6b553856204eb1958c84fb06dc2756edb4e44beee4c55550aad8d3592f4.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110389 1072 #uuid "d8d0babe-ca51-5d80-ba9c-5289697cb968" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110389 1074 "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 15^{∘} &= 2x + 10^{∘} \\\\\nx - 15^{∘} &= 10^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 25^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=25^\\circ$, we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $2x + 10^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n2x + 10^\\circ = 2 \\cdot 25^\\circ + 10^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of $60^\\circ$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110390 1069 "e-2583" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110390 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle AOB$ is a straight angle. Determine $m \\angle AOD$.\n\n![](images/6e/6eac6c11dcc513cdf896cdfaa9650689af1bf58afd147783d0bb1ca61dd3a324.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110390 1072 #uuid "02660015-d4fd-53f3-b7a5-4a4118e406b3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110390 1074 "Since $\\angle AOB$ is straight,\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC + m\\angle BOC = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5x + 100^{∘}) + 11x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n16x + 100^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n16x &= 80^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 5^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=5^\\circ$, we can calculate $m \\angle COB$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm \\angle COB = 11(5^\\circ) = 55^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $\\angle AOD$ and $\\angle COB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOD = m \\angle COB = 55^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110391 1069 "e-2587" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110391 1071 "In the diagram shown below, it is known that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ$. Determine the value of $m \\angle 2 + m \\angle 4$.\n\n![](images/1b/1bc662904a52724fac28dc180f70088592ff4da181930c6607b1bb590b3ec188.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110391 1072 #uuid "a5dae4ad-b516-5ece-8d12-5805c5073fe9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110391 1074 "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\nm∠1 = m∠2,; m∠3 = m∠4\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\nm \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\n60^{∘} = m∠2,; 120^{∘} = m∠4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠2 + m∠4 &= 60^{∘} + 120^{∘} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110392 1069 "e-2588" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110392 1071 "Which of the following shows an obtuse angle $\\angle ABC$?\n\n![](images/6d/6d346356c4f36a4361cf386890522191c50bb1d237ba4cde609ae395446aa641.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110392 1072 #uuid "52e6d36c-9609-5093-b3ee-0f0f2df6dc35" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110392 1074 "Remember that an obtuse angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$. Therefore, the correct diagram is the following:\n\n![](images/ea/eacc0abc69ac416e36a4743dbb2c51e212fbde62e2e84150b5bfb94cf9e77640.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110393 1069 "e-2590" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110393 1071 "In the figure shown below, determine the measure of $\\angle EOA$.\n\n![](images/80/80f47745a289bf021a4566a58d1961c93bb216e2df900b8effae8f2dda3f1606.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110393 1072 #uuid "826ef015-53ba-5889-8749-170a656c35f9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110393 1074 "From the diagram, $\\angle COD$ is a right angle, so\n\n$$\nm\\angle COD = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\angle COD$ and $\\angle EOB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB=m\\angle COD=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since we know that\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB=40^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe can use the angle addition postulate to solve for $m\\angle EOA$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠EOA + m∠AOB &= m∠EOB \\\\\nm∠EOA + 40^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠EOA &= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110394 1069 "e-2593" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110394 1071 "Which of the following shows an acute angle $\\angle CDE$?\n\n![](images/e1/e144e6e17d324552c8c580685228ae70299f86318ac7e2c23eab934aad682315.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110394 1072 #uuid "6d89b301-bf06-5bcd-8299-c08e5a55151a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110394 1074 "Remember that an acute angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$. Therefore, the correct diagram is the following:\n\n![](images/1f/1f99b6f90638737c70d07021ea291c722aea3237537d8b806a371768138ae643.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110395 1069 "e-2597" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110395 1071 "Which of the following shows a reflex angle $\\angle CDE$?\n\n![](images/9b/9bfba73c3b5c3c1eea3c68e41b939fad6fb354ef4aa97e2dd8268633904b634e.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110395 1072 #uuid "506a1576-d7b3-577d-99a8-9188129d74f5" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110395 1074 "Remember that a reflex angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$. Therefore, the correct diagram is the following:\n\n![](images/8a/8a2a0b69e0f53e31a81180a9c28a024a74e662e92c0301cf15a0e7d12234d7b5.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110396 1069 "e-2599" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110396 1071 "![](images/80/800f11f555f0c2be0e89aa26602cfe4a35a62f4fe2309dfe2a188f47138fb98b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $\\angle A$ is obtuse\n2. $\\angle B$ is a right angle\n3. $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are vertical angles" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110396 1072 #uuid "6438b232-f40a-54b0-a599-a1d6aa4151d2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110396 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the diagram, $\\angle A$ and the angle with measure $130^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore,\n$m\\angle A = 130^\\circ > 90^\\circ$.\nConsequently, $\\angle A$ is an obtuse angle.\n- Statement II is false. From the diagram, $\\angle B$ and the angle with measure $50^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore,\n$m\\angle B = 50^\\circ \\neq 90^\\circ$.\nConsequently, $\\angle B$ is not a right angle.\n- Statement III is false. Angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are not vertical since they have a common side.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110397 1069 "e-2601" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110397 1071 "Let $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ be two adjacent angles. Given that $\\angle S$ is a straight angle and $\\angle T$ is right, what type of angle is their sum?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110397 1072 #uuid "15470df7-8d18-5427-9488-f3f10df13952" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110397 1074 "Since $\\angle S$ is a straight angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle S=180^\\circ\n$$\n\nand since $\\angle T$ is a right angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle T=90^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSo, their sum is\n\n$$\nm\\angle S + m\\angle T=180^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ}=270^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ is a reflex angle." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110398 1069 "e-2604" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110398 1071 "![](images/86/867b3664e93f42c55a2f97dc28bf66794484e226981632c85f725926cec77def.png)\n\nWhat type of angle is $\\angle RST$, shown in the figure above?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110398 1072 #uuid "154c65b2-4d51-5da0-b00c-293f3fba3bd0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110398 1074 "Notice that the vertex of $\\angle RST$ is point $S$, not point $T$. So $\\angle RST$ is *not* a straight angle.\n\nAs a result, the two rays $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{SR}$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{ST}$ of our angle coincide. So, we can think of $\\angle RST$ as:\n\n- A *null* angle, assuming it doesn't turn at all.\n\n![](images/a8/a8916a724d4463bf2047c919b73770160d3124838b61af0dcdc2b9e04322381a.png)\n\n- A *full* angle, assuming it makes one full turn.\n\n![](images/9c/9cd768b4361e95a18ffa52d6315fb5ea6cd0cf6784bc4c9e12b1bae1f6566755.png)\n\nSo, the answer is \"Null\" or \"Full.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110399 1069 "e-2612" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110399 1071 "![](images/21/21926302f77e4322b1901e0fa5bf7941d91f008c6ed7289d6957ec040bff5792.png)\n\nGiven the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB},\\, \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PS}$ shown above, for which value of $x$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110399 1072 #uuid "f319bdf3-8333-571a-94ac-3bb3fcb71af5" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110399 1074 "First, notice that $\\angle PQA$ and $\\angle PQB$ are supplementary angles. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PQB + m∠PQA &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠PQB + 61^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠PQB &= 119^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PS}$.\n\nThe angles $\\angle PQB$ and $\\angle QRD$ are corresponding angles. To have parallel lines, the corresponding angles must be congruent.\n\nTo have $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, we need $\\angle PQB\\cong\\angle QRD$. So, these two angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PQB &= m∠QRD \\\\\n119^{∘} &= 11x - 2^{∘} \\\\\n121^{∘} &= 11x \\\\\n11^{∘} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 11^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110400 1069 "e-2614" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110400 1071 "Given the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ shown below, which value of $x$ means that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$?\n\n![](images/02/023e80720451cbf8234c3e1a77093886a97775e3b90ab488ca06e37fc8812620.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110400 1072 #uuid "8806cd8a-2fae-596b-8e47-6218d2df2199" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110400 1074 "The lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$.\n\nThe angles $\\angle BJI$ and $\\angle JID$ are consecutive interior angles. To have parallel lines, the consecutive interior angles must be supplementary.\n\nTo have $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, we need\n\n$$\nm\\angle BJI + m\\angle JID = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting into this equation, we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BJI + m∠JID &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + 50^{∘} + 5x + 10^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n6x + 60^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n6x &= 120^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110401 1069 "e-2615" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110401 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle DAB$ and $m\\angle DAB = 79^\\circ$. What is the measure of $\\angle DAC$?\n\n![](images/8d/8d02999cf6ae7ec69fee2cdac99b8dc214c59f1ed50bbf85bec6bec01f2212ef.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110401 1072 #uuid "9612e5aa-af83-5ea5-8bf2-302834e1926a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110401 1074 "An angle bisector divides the angle into two congruent angles with equal measures, so\n\n$$\nm\\angle BAC = m\\angle DAC\n$$\n\n![](images/6e/6e7c8ce2713441e633d7ecc3548c504d2522b4771eb6355217d6ab766a647b67.png)\n\nEach of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DAC &= \\frac{m∠DAB}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{79^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= 39.5^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110402 1069 "e-2619" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110402 1071 "For which value of $x$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}\\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$?\n\n![](images/5b/5b8160ff68aba3f2fcb5445647f7d2cf31bf8a4b359f71c2ac2607652fd4d888.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110402 1072 #uuid "1198e4e7-2e3c-58d7-a5fe-77d8232cf0d2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110402 1074 "The lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PS}$.\n\nThe angles $\\angle ARS$ and $\\angle PQD$ are alternate exterior angles. To have parallel lines, the alternate exterior angles must be congruent.\n\nTo have $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, we need\n\n$$\nm\\angle ARS = m\\angle PQD\n$$\n\n.\n\nSubstituting into this equation, we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ARS &= m∠PQD \\\\\n2x + 20^{∘} &= 110^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 45^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110403 1069 "e-2621" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110403 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$ and $m\\angle BAC = 40^\\circ$. What is the measure of $\\angle GAD$?\n\n![](images/51/51e4b2db4e0715ce53b605fb9e803d86613c3b4a128dcdd9255a9d183a2de941.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110403 1072 #uuid "ec3e6d87-e38e-5aa6-b064-f3c15a51a836" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110403 1074 "Since the angles $\\angle CAD$ and $\\angle BAC$ are supplementary, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠CAD &= 180^{∘} - m∠BAC \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 40^{∘} \\\\\n&= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠GAD &= \\frac{m∠CAD}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{140^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110404 1069 "e-2624" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110404 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overrightarrow{OC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle AOB$. If $m \\angle AOD = 90^\\circ$ and $m \\angle COD = 35^\\circ$, find the measure of $\\angle BOD$.\n\n![](images/0b/0b76c6ee6e576fa2701993e941c27f1168c213d9f5f26b1aa8a50759c3711404.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110404 1072 #uuid "f4304d42-1b4c-5ade-86d2-8ed44a952fc8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110404 1074 "![](images/5a/5ae4873418247e8d825233f903ed359ebee4d2c51ad73e6c7763102a23acded6.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOC &= m∠AOD - m∠COD \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} - 35^{∘} \\\\\n&= 55^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOB =2 m \\angle AOC = 110^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, the angle addition postulate gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BOD &= m∠AOB - m∠AOD \\\\\n&= 110^{∘} - 90^{∘} \\\\\n&= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110405 1069 "e-2628" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110405 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$. What is the measure of $\\angle GAF$?\n\n![](images/45/458893f38204421326fd7dcb0693b1688eeb61fb3988e00b2d36763b47adc2d2.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110405 1072 #uuid "b3dbb627-0262-5fdd-ae6b-ba669e4fac6f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110405 1074 "![](images/3b/3b0fddd3229947725b0f4f07e58ce9bfac3a9e7e348c25757162f5bc8e03d699.png)\n\nLet\n\n$$\nm \\angle FAC=x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAG=y\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=m\\angle FAC + m \\angle CAG=x+y\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BAC=2x\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAD=2y\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAC + m∠CAD &= m∠BAD \\\\\n2x + 2y &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + y &= 90^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=x+y=90^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110406 1069 "e-2711" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110406 1071 "Find the value of $x$ in the following figure.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d124c971c55844381cf9cedaa0e392e634e5ceda31d6af7a136426678f4db.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110406 1072 #uuid "1642f15d-32c8-5a19-b20b-95f501dc0a97" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110406 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B$. We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n73^{∘} + m∠B &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 180^{∘} - 73^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 107^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n41^{∘} + 107^{∘} + 2x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n148^{∘} + 2x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 32^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 16^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\n180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]) = m∠A + m∠C\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]) &= m∠A + m∠C \\\\\n73^{∘} &= 41^{∘} + 2x \\\\\n32^{∘} &= 2x \\\\\n16^{∘} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110407 1069 "e-2712" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110407 1071 "Find the value of $x$ in the figure below.\n\n![](images/6e/6eb087290fa6ea66325d35fa626ef385bb4d4e477e6e1c40057b9f9d7e43d9d4.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110407 1072 #uuid "d9bf29fd-5151-591f-b6af-8f5e53b3d231" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110407 1074 "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n145^{∘} + x + (2x - 25^{∘}) &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n120^{∘} + 3x &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 240^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110408 1069 "e-2714" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110408 1071 "Given a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, let the exterior angles at ${A}$ and ${B}$ be equal $120^\\circ$ and $100^\\circ$, respectively. What is the measure of the angle $\\angle C$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110408 1072 #uuid "e97d1ab9-f0b2-5e27-afff-a84da69c7487" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110408 1074 "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](images/3e/3e2698fe8e2e886127669b1e04f45499706750312b447ff0c73524f2de4ff2ce.png)\n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at ${C}$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n120^{∘} + 100^{∘} + z &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n220^{∘} + z &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nz &= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠C &= 180^{∘} - z \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 140^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = 40^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110409 1069 "e-2831" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110409 1071 "![](images/6f/6fe7a5993d47d66915c9ec7918c938d5d2d1af4bd1479a7d73a96bca4e47cdea.png)\n\nIn the trapezoid $ABCD$ above, determine $m \\angle ADC + m \\angle ABC$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110409 1072 #uuid "822a4e42-8ac2-54df-8897-d02d9ac28f47" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110409 1074 "Note that\n\n$$\nm \\angle{BCD} = 180^{\\circ} - 55^{\\circ} = 125^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nBecause the angles adjacent to the legs of the trapezoid are supplementary, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ADC &= 180^{∘} - m∠BAD \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 70^{∘} \\\\\n&= 110^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ABC &= 180^{∘} - m∠BCD \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 125^{∘} \\\\\n&= 55^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nm∠ADC + m∠ABC = 110^{∘} + 55^{∘} = 165^{∘}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110410 1069 "e-2832" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110410 1071 "![](images/c8/c853975681e4a2a6de71c685b39085494889fe839dd83473ff48d27884b390f8.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ given the trapezoid $PQRS$ above?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110410 1072 #uuid "c8c513e4-773c-5314-8479-e0d7c4b942e9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110410 1074 "The angles $\\angle{P}$ and $\\angle{S}$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠P + m∠S &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n(x + 25^{∘}) + (3x - 17^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n4x + 8^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n4x &= 172^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 43^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110411 1069 "e-2833" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110411 1071 "Which of the following terms describe the shape below?\n\n1. Parallelogram\n2. Rectangle\n3. Square\n\n![](images/d5/d578ca7af1f030dabb8e44a5a8fab90ce4bd21d002054fccdf2be2413a855a5c.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110411 1072 #uuid "515ab620-8513-55cc-8c88-61c0ad27aff2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110411 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of opposite congruent angles, so it is a parallelogram.\n- Since all the angles in this parallelogram are right, it is a rectangle.\n- Not all the sides of this rectangle are congruent, so it is not a square.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (parallelogram) and II (rectangle)." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110412 1069 "e-2834" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110412 1071 "Which of the following shapes is a trapezoid?\n\n![](images/6a/6a9f79e60fb5fca0ffee8fed594c19e946d3d6f02544f65ebd7e040e5cbf1ca0.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110412 1072 #uuid "ee3a028c-93df-5e5a-bc06-4c286a5e1839" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110412 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a trapezoid is a quadrilateral with exactly one pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{P}$ is a trapezoid since it has one pair of opposite sides that are parallel.\n- The shape $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rhombus. It has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to one another, not one pair. Therefore, it is not a trapezoid.\n- The shape $\\mathcal{R}$ is a kite. Its opposite sides are not parallel, therefore it is not a trapezoid.\n- The shape $\\mathcal{S}$ is a rectangle. It has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to one another, not one pair. Therefore, it is not a trapezoid.\n\nTherefore, only shape $\\mathcal{P}$ is a trapezoid." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110413 1069 "e-2836" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110413 1071 "Given the trapezoid below, find $CD$ if $AH = 2$, $FB = 8$, and $AB = 15$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f583d94182a302c4ef9ce23daa9a090b0210a6aa87c4ae4116b11004d7c58c9.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110413 1072 #uuid "f1fd06af-7097-5518-a8fb-8196c2040fc6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110413 1074 "Notice that $\\overline{AB}$ is the largest base of a trapezoid and\n\n$$\nAB=AH+HF+FB\n$$\n\nAlso, $CD = HF$.\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= AH + HF + FB \\\\\n15 &= 2 + HF + 8 \\\\\n15 &= HF + 10 \\\\\nHF &= 15 - 10 \\\\\nHF &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nCD = HF = 5\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110414 1069 "e-2839" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110414 1071 "Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. A rhombus is always a kite.\n2. A rhombus is always a parallelogram.\n3. A kite is always a parallelogram." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110414 1072 #uuid "a7b82dde-36fc-582e-9ed6-fbfcfbf8b47c" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110414 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A rhombus fits this description, because all the sides in a rhombus are congruent.\n- Statement II is true. In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel. The same is true for a rhombus. A rhombus is a specific type of parallelogram, where all the sides are congruent.\n- Statement III is false. In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel. However, in a kite, it's only necessary that two pairs of congruent sides are adjacent to each other. Therefore, a kite is not always a parallelogram.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110415 1069 "e-2850" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110415 1071 "For which value of $t$ is the rectangle below also a square?\n\n![](images/e2/e22aacc6b9d558be7809c53244b3021c5855040452ca910bb3d560ac6e5e7c87.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110415 1072 #uuid "b5149f89-fb8c-5a67-9cd6-08742f659b57" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110415 1074 "In order for the rectangle to be a square, all of its sides must be congruent.\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= CD \\\\\n2t + 6 &= 4t \\\\\n2t &= 6 \\\\\nt &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110416 1069 "e-2852" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110416 1071 "The perimeter of the rectangle $PQRS$ below equals $25\\, \\mathrm{cm}$. What is the length of $\\overline{PQ}$?\n\n![](images/3e/3ef40de2efedd6982d306052c67ad69953460fcfeb8cd0db7ee04e06512218ba.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110416 1072 #uuid "2e99f745-a0e5-5242-8767-2f26ab39108d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110416 1074 "The opposite sides of a rectangle are congruent, so we have\n\n$$\nPS=QR=5\\, \\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nFor the same reason, we also have\n\n$$\nPQ = RS\n$$\n\nNow, since the perimeter is\n\n$$\np=25 \\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= PQ + RS + PS + QR \\\\\n25 &= PQ + PQ + 5 + 5 \\\\\n25 &= 2PQ + 10 \\\\\n15 &= 2PQ \\\\\n7.5 &= PQ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nPQ = 7.5 \\, \\text{cm}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110417 1069 "e-2853" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110417 1071 "In the square below, $NO=3\\,\\mathrm{mm}$. Find $KM$.\n\n![](images/fa/fae7652884bf6056cfc4da35fe9fbbfa888d89c21fb0b1e7b30e075c5877ce30.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110417 1072 #uuid "8b9285e6-2091-51ba-baf6-2d1b32cceacd" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110417 1074 "The diagonals of a square are equal and bisect each other. So we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nKM &= LN \\\\\n&= 2NO \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3mm \\\\\n&= 6mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110418 1069 "e-2855" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110418 1071 "For which value of $x$ is the parallelogram below also a rectangle?\n\n![](images/cc/cc6b91de2e5fc0a9c0ffba6e287066354297b2c0de5b087350cf1c564b770eae.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110418 1072 #uuid "e03d3b05-2333-5a65-ab82-b6cb839e9fba" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110418 1074 "If $ABCD$ is a rectangle, then\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since the interior angles of $\\triangle ABD$ must sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ADB + m∠ABD + m∠BAD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x^{∘} + (x + 15)^{∘} + 90^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x + 15 &= 90 \\\\\n3x &= 75 \\\\\nx &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110419 1069 "e-2871" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110419 1071 "The triangle $\\mathcal{P}$ (shown below) is shifted $5$ units to the right and $4$ units downward to give the triangle $\\mathcal{Q}$. Plot the resulting triangle.\n\n![](images/b8/b897f65bd0b439a71d5c9c15b6b7f19735462df71a6e5af42e659c8f70af6f5d.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110419 1072 #uuid "b4966b87-5391-5958-b617-5b6100d525f6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110419 1074 "First, we must translate each point of the triangle $5$ units to the right. To accomplish this, we can translate each vertex $5$ units to the right and then draw in the line segments between the new vertices.\n\n![](images/2d/2d657e6a8b8edb3ccb9cd928b95a16321c23d6089303832a629a394526575c62.png)\n\nNext, we must translate each point of the resulting triangle $4$ units downward. Again, we translate the vertices and then draw in the line segments between the new vertices. The diagram below shows the resulting triangle.\n\n![](images/85/85ae92528bc635d4b0703fd97d6af8adaa7153697d704c0835c26b756c8d2f1b.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110420 1069 "e-2872" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110420 1071 "![](images/71/711da8bb589f4b3d004cfa4419b83cfc8920b346d37017f6803258394f6f426b.png)\n\nA translation $f$ maps the polygon $\\mathcal P$ to the polygon $\\mathcal Q$, as shown above. What is the functional representation of $f$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110420 1072 #uuid "9c7d6297-07d2-56d7-9853-91827cd15ad9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110420 1074 "Note that if we translate the polygon $\\mathcal{P}$ by $5$ units to the right and $3$ units upward, then we get the polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$.\n\nTherefore, the functional representation of the transformation is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= (x+5,y+3)\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110421 1069 "e-2873" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110421 1071 "![](images/06/066a5f6bdfefffa6557a1f9e3ce4ae3bc00f2861a1402c7270029033a2796d09.png)\n\nThe translation $(x,y) \\mapsto(x+p, y+q)$ maps the polygon $\\mathcal{P}$ to the polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ as shown above. What are the values of $p$ and $q$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110421 1072 #uuid "e1371c06-443a-5bbc-9a00-4c6b5e4352ad" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110421 1074 "Every point belonging to $\\mathcal{P}$ is translated in the same way. Therefore to determine the values of $p$ and $q$, we can consider any point of the polygon.\n\nLet's consider the vertex $(-2,2)$. Under the translation, it moves to $(8,1)$. So, we have\n\n$$\n(-2, 2) \\mapsto (-2+p, 2+q)= (8,1)\n$$\n\nWe establish the values of $p$ and $q$ by considering the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of the above transformation.\n\n- Looking at the $x$-coordinates, we must have $-2+p = 8$, so $p=10$.\n- Looking at the $y$-coordinates, we must have $2+q=1$, so $q=-1$.\n\nTherefore, $p=10$ and $q=-1$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110422 1069 "e-2896" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110422 1071 "The point $(2, 5)$ is reflected across the $y$-axis. What are the coordinates of the resulting point?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110422 1072 #uuid "5b956104-d936-5df3-82f3-b385f6d94412" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110422 1074 "Reflection across the $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\mapsto(-x,y)\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(2,5)$, we have\n\n$$\n(2,5) \\mapsto (-2,5)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-2,5)$.\n\n![](images/20/203495d322bc101b918df53a81eedeb2bf98c33cab0ba21c32a142a60d17b11f.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110423 1069 "e-2897" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110423 1071 "The segment shown below is reflected across the $y$-axis. Draw the resulting segment.\n\n![](images/11/1177ae0295e8276b49d89641f499b7fad8135ccdd494e0139773713474271db4.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110423 1072 #uuid "b474463d-68f4-5b1e-9f7d-1d628686bc06" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110423 1074 "To reflect a segment across a line, we\n\n- reflect the endpoints of the segment, and then\n- draw the segment connecting the reflected endpoints.\n\nReflection across the $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\mapsto (-x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, our two endpoints $(2,2)$ and $(6, 4)$ are mapped to the following points by the reflection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2, 2) & ↦ (-2, 2) \\\\ (6, 4) & ↦ (-6, 4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, reflecting our segment across the $y$-axis, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/09/093ce3dd61cf4176a61e46bedfa7ffca7053a484ebc77ae535687e720717270d.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110424 1069 "e-2900" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110424 1071 "The triangle shown below is reflected across the $x$-axis. Draw the resulting triangle.\n\n![](images/c0/c07a7277524f3b7826db4ba98848c4b4e7d20f74e7055451d5e2ed23fc9dfe05.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110424 1072 #uuid "3eba107a-5628-5832-a95f-b3269409c9f2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110424 1074 "To reflect a polygon across a line we\n\n- reflect the vertices of the polygon, and then\n- draw the edges connecting the reflected vertices.\n\nTherefore, reflecting our polygon across the $x$-axis, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/bb/bb4160b6f193ef767c7f76f6f36d889f8d368641a7107b32b5f6a06670f09da1.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110425 1069 "e-2904" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110425 1071 "Are the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below congruent?\n\n![](images/75/75684fc94e43e8854403d543019f28f971b8b6adc0ca3f40cb673dc9dec6dcb9.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110425 1072 #uuid "aedb6e30-d1e6-5d89-aa83-56c1d6946b50" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110425 1074 "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\n$\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110426 1069 "e-2908" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110426 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the polygons $\\mathcal{A}$, $\\mathcal{B}$, and $\\mathcal{C}$ shown below?\n\n1. $\\mathcal{A}\\cong\\mathcal{B}$\n2. $\\mathcal{B}\\cong\\mathcal{C}$\n3. $\\mathcal{C}\\cong\\mathcal{A}$\n\n![](images/a1/a13f283489521fc43d73a5a128b79ff937dca760c8cea330706efe6f39ac26f6.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110426 1072 #uuid "fd18dfda-f385-5e24-a200-882a71d3b063" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110426 1074 "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the polygons $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $\\mathcal{A}\\cong\\mathcal{B}$.\n- Statement II is false. Notice that the polygons $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent angles ($\\mathcal{B}$ does not have right angles, while $\\mathcal{C}$ does).\n- Statement III is false. By the same reasoning as above, we can see that the polygons $\\mathcal{C}$ and $\\mathcal{A}$ are not congruent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110427 1069 "e-2910" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110427 1071 "![](images/de/de9be6ab7fe0608003c7a0493b8aab19df0dd4353b085330fc2a8f8aea84e527.png)\n\nWrite down a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110427 1072 #uuid "b3ec8468-a64a-5f55-b392-8f0072fbdd78" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110427 1074 "First, notice that $\\angle A \\cong \\angle C$.\n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second quadrilateral that correspond to $A$. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= XY \\\\\nAD &= XW \\\\\n&= ∠A \\mid ≅∠X\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $X$, and\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $Z$ (since $C$ is opposite $A$ and $Z$ is opposite $X$).\n\nAs for the other two vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∠B & ≅∠Y \\\\ ∠D & ≅∠W\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCD &= ZW \\\\\nBC &= YZ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to $Y$, and\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to $W$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nABCD \\cong XYZW\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110428 1069 "e-2911" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110428 1071 "Given that $ABCDE \\cong PQRST$, find $x+y$.\n\n![](images/f1/f133fecf2229bc78933ad0e73bb707dd17cad45ae087037b3a327e3ef4dbbc73.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110428 1072 #uuid "f6e8ab4f-f21a-5c5e-9682-9f9b00e4fdc1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110428 1074 "The statement $ABCDE \\cong PQRST$ means that $\\mathcal R$ and $\\mathcal Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $P$,\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to vertex $Q$,\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $R$,\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to vertex $S$,\n- vertex $E$ corresponds to vertex $T$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCD &= RS \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= m∠P \\\\\n82^{∘} &= (11y + 5)^{∘} \\\\\n77 &= 11y \\\\\n7 &= y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx+y = 2+7=9\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110429 1069 "e-2915" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110429 1071 "According to the SAS criterion only, which of the following triangles are congruent to $\\mathcal{T}$?\n\n![](images/77/77ef6e5be57ee130e6887c5154f7f92024466a40077ede36cf32e2b8b82efaee.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110429 1072 #uuid "861abbc1-eb15-59fb-bf43-655c5eb5347e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110429 1074 "The SAS (side-angle-side) congruence criterion states the following:\n\n> Two triangles are congruent if and only if two sides and the included angle of one triangle are congruent to two sides and the included angle of the other triangle.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given triangles.\n\n- $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{T}$ by SAS since two sides and the included angle of one triangle are congruent to two sides and the included angle of the other triangle.\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* congruent to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SAS since we don't have a pair of congruent angles.\n- $\\mathcal{R} \\cong \\mathcal{T}$ by SAS too, since two sides and the included angle of one triangle are congruent to two sides and the included angle of the other triangle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{R}$ only.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110430 1069 "e-2917" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110430 1071 "Given the diagram below, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle DEF$ by SAS\n2. $\\angle{B} \\cong \\angle{F}$\n3. $x=2$\n\n![](images/ae/aeaaf4f3a88af20878b7dea50511173ea6c6761a57f3f641cd34194be9abd889.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110430 1072 #uuid "7bcf98ef-a087-5547-a4b2-d9f6d267fcaf" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110430 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by the SAS criterion since we have the following pairs of congruent sides and included angles:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}BA^(―) & ≅ED^(―) \\\\ AC^(―) & ≅DF^(―) \\\\ ∠A & ≅∠D\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement II is false. Since the two triangles are congruent, the corresponding angles must be congruent too. Therefore, we obtain\n$\\angle{B} \\cong \\angle{E}$.\n- Statement III is true. Since the two triangles are congruent, the corresponding sides must be congruent too. Hence, we obtain\n$BC|= EF; 2x|= x + 2; x|= 2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110431 1069 "e-2920" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110431 1071 "Find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/f8/f826a6e63a9b4d9daa4af51041adde91f08f1d73a60ef57c8ec4a4db48fb84be.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110431 1072 #uuid "95d34b20-ac65-58c7-8528-1b824ddad9f0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110431 1074 "First, notice that $\\angle AMB$ and $\\angle QMP$ are vertical angles.\n\nNow, $\\triangle AMB$ and $\\triangle QMP$ are congruent by the SAS criterion since we have the following pairs of congruent sides and included angles:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AM^(―) & ≅MQ^(―) \\\\ BM^(―) & ≅MP^(―) \\\\ ∠AMB & ≅∠QMP\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, all the corresponding angles of the triangles must be congruent. In particular, $\\angle A \\cong \\angle Q$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠Q &= m∠A \\\\\n3x - 7^{∘} &= 65^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 72^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 24^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110432 1069 "e-2923" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110432 1071 "Given the figure below, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/e5/e5a8ff34261e947c50de5e06b6f3007dc8260d6b5697eeb75d2ee60b0c1d5e95.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110432 1072 #uuid "8391bc28-9897-59ff-812d-67ef22a1876a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110432 1074 "Notice that $\\triangle PSQ$ and $\\triangle PSR$ are congruent by SAS (side-angle-side) since $\\overline{PS}$ is their common side and we have the following pairs of congruent sides and included angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}SQ^(―) & ≅SR^(―) \\\\ ∠PSQ & ≅∠PSR\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding sides of the triangles must be congruent. In particular, $\\overline{PQ} \\cong \\overline{PR}$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPR &= PQ \\\\\n5x - 2 &= 13 \\\\\n5x &= 15 \\\\\nx &= 3in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110433 1069 "e-2928" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110433 1071 "![](images/94/945a206a1cd479dc2571d147bd53bdc3b9cfd8675de3ba8cb6e991742e4897bb.png)\n\nAccording to the HL criterion only, which of the triangles above are congruent to $\\mathcal{P}$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110433 1072 #uuid "a9715467-ef05-5895-80e7-2e38b8ab9803" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110433 1074 "The HL (hypotenuse-leg) congruence criterion states the following:\n\n> Two right triangles are congruent if and only if they have a pair of congruent hypotenuses and a pair of congruent legs.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given triangles.\n\n- $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{T}_1$ by HL since we have a pair of right angles, a pair of congruent legs, and a pair of congruent hypotenuses.\n- $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{T}_2$ by HL since we have a pair of right angles, a pair of congruent legs, and a pair of congruent hypotenuses.\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is *not* congruent to $\\mathcal{T}_3$ by HL since we don't have a pair of congruent legs and congruent hypotenuses.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{T}_1$ and $\\mathcal{T}_2$ only.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110434 1069 "e-2931" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110434 1071 "![](images/4d/4d0000d1c9573a3382495305fffe0642bb9bf085b21f292acbd786e533265dee.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle DEF$ by HL\n2. $\\angle C \\cong \\angle F$\n3. $y=4$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110434 1072 #uuid "2807f657-d487-5e85-8103-5d2608ef1c2d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110434 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by HL (hypotenuse-leg) since we have the following congruent legs, hypotenuses, and right angles:$m\\angle{B}=m\\angle{E}=90^\\circ$$\\overline{AB}\\cong \\overline{ED}$$\\overline{AC}\\cong \\overline{DF}$\n- Statement II is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding angles must be congruent. In particular,\n$\\angle C \\cong \\angle F$\n- Statement III is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{EF}$. Thus,\n$BC|= EF; 3y - 8|= 2y - 4; y|= 4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110435 1069 "e-2932" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110435 1071 "The point $A$ is located on the base $\\overline{PR}$ of an isosceles triangle $\\triangle PQR$ such that $\\angle QAR$ is a right angle. If $m \\angle RQA = 70^\\circ$ and $m \\angle{PQA}=3x+13^\\circ$, what is the value of $x$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110435 1072 #uuid "f21078b3-6637-521d-819b-f58855338ea8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110435 1074 "First, let's draw a sketch using the given information.\n\n![](images/9d/9dd1fbc85c738d49f865f25ceede8c88e563f17638e0da6a6d3411918babb250.png)\n\nSince $\\angle RAQ$ and $\\angle PAQ$ form a linear pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠RAQ + m∠PAQ &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n90^{∘} + m∠PAQ &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠PAQ &= 90^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\triangle QAR$ and $\\triangle QAP$ are congruent by HL (hypotenuse-leg) since we have the following congruent legs, hypotenuses, and right angles:\n\n- $m\\angle{QAR}=m\\angle{QAP}=90^\\circ$\n- A common leg $\\overline{AQ}$\n- $\\overline{QR}\\cong \\overline{QP}$\n\nAs a consequence, all the corresponding angles must be congruent. In particular, $\\angle AQR \\cong \\angle AQP$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PQA &= m∠RQA \\\\\n3x + 13^{∘} &= 70^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 57^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 19^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110436 1069 "e-2957" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110436 1071 "![](images/7f/7f9a244e8fac92bf63c80ff1a4a1a748d926677af138005efafb4b0b5d40a738.png)\n\nFind the perimeter of ​​the figure above.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110436 1072 #uuid "f5198bec-f68a-50c1-8d85-7a395a11092d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110436 1074 "The figure is composed of three squares, $R_1$, $R_2$, and $R_3$, and one rectangle, $R_4$.\n\n![](images/fb/fb931caab675b73e4d69d5f182b4c1171aaed537e76c1571857402a64ee6d585.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the figure is determined by the sum of the lengths of\n\n- $3$ sides of $R_1$ (each of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$),\n- $1$ side of $R_2$ (of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$),\n- $3$ sides of $R_3$ (of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$),\n- $3$ sides of $R_4$ (two of length $3\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and one of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$).\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 3 \\cdot 1 + 1 + 3 \\cdot 1 + (3 + 1 + 3) \\\\\n&= 3 + 1 + 3 + 7 \\\\\n&= 14cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110437 1069 "e-2958" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110437 1071 "What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/87/87149081680062ab08a5f2d613079284325f2b7fddf19e8b8a5ad3bd56f4894c.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110437 1072 #uuid "98005d9a-2bec-5165-a3a2-5f8249f4a8b5" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110437 1074 "To find the area of the shaded region, we calculate the areas of the outer and inner shapes and then subtract the results.\n\nThe outer shape is a trapezoid with bases of length $8\\,\\textrm{in}$ and\n\n$$\n2+2=4\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\n, and a height of $4\\,\\textrm{in}$. Therefore, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{O} = \\dfrac{(8+4) \\cdot 4}{2} = 24\\,\\textrm{in}^2\n$$\n\nThe inner shape is a triangle with a base of $8\\,\\textrm{in}$ and a height of $4\\,\\textrm{in}$. Therefore, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{I} = \\dfrac{8 \\cdot 4}{2} = 16\\,\\textrm{in}^2\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the shaded shape is the difference between the areas of the outer and inner shapes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{O} - A_{I} \\\\\n&= 24 - 16 \\\\\n&= 8in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110438 1069 "e-2960" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110438 1071 "A farmer wants to grow corn in a triangular field around his rectangular house, as shown below. What is the total area that is available for growing corn?\n\n![](images/65/6599906ea4795445f0c0a256aa25940a8a705c8df72c479a942545b0211594de.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110438 1072 #uuid "acdba433-6c9b-536b-943c-93186ccee1bb" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110438 1074 "The area of the field that the farmer will use for planting corresponds to the shaded region.\n\nTo find the area of the shaded region, we calculate the areas of the outer and inner shapes and then subtract the results.\n\nThe outer shape is a triangle with base $60\\,\\textrm{m}$ and height $40\\,\\textrm{m}$. So, the area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{O} = \\dfrac{60 \\cdot 40}{2} =1\\, 200\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nThe inner shape is the rectangle with sides $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $20\\,\\textrm{m}$. So, the area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{I} = 10 \\cdot 20= 200\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the shaded shape is the difference between the areas of the outer and inner shapes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{O} - A_{I} \\\\\n&= 1200 - 200 \\\\\n&= 1000m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total area available for growing corn is $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}^2$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110439 1069 "e-2963" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110439 1071 "What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/8e/8ee6c81011b94090fcb9361cdc05463c8600e57555212f70a620185d6b75b668.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110439 1072 #uuid "e9ca5cd1-4e6a-5a6c-964d-708f5989fdf0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110439 1074 "To find the area of the shaded region, we calculate the areas of the outer and inner shapes and then subtract the results.\n\nThe outer shape is a rectangle with sides of $8\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $7\\,\\textrm{cm}$. So, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{O} = 8 \\cdot 7 =56\\, \\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nThe inner shape consists of two parts.\n\n- The first part is the isosceles triangle (on the left) with a base of $2+2=4\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and a height of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$. So, its area is\n$\\mathcal{A}_{I_1} = \\dfrac{2 \\cdot 4}{2} =4\\, \\textrm{cm}^2$.\n- The second part is a square (on the right) with a side of length $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$. So, its the corresponding is\n$\\mathcal{A}_{I_2} = 4 \\cdot 4 = 16 \\, \\textrm{cm}^2$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain the inner area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{I} &= A_{I_{1}} + A_{I_{2}} \\\\\n&= 4 + 16 \\\\\n&= 20cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the shaded shape is the difference between the areas of the outer and inner shapes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{O} - A_{I} \\\\\n&= 56 - 20 \\\\\n&= 36cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110440 1069 "e-2966" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110440 1071 "![](images/23/23679e6f00544c82e6b423ca04db527d25a0bd9db006ecf99fbe48d10b4b0458.png)\n\nThe figure above is composed of a trapezoid and an isosceles triangle. Find the area of ​​the figure.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110440 1072 #uuid "a8e417bc-83d0-58eb-9bd7-64ce9f1d9ac8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110440 1074 "The area of ​​the trapezoid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= ((2 + 4) \\cdot 1)/(2) = 3cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of ​​the isosceles triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{2} &= \\frac{4 \\cdot 3}{2} = 6cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of ​​the entire figure is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{1} + A_{2} \\\\\n&= 3 + 6 \\\\\n&= 9cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110441 1069 "e-2967" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110441 1071 "![](images/16/160fa17ee54c7bb55ad7899f4ca4e63578f839f9d2cd4dda9d540d89b466f14c.png)\n\nThe figure above is obtained by taking a square with an area of $16\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ and cutting out an equilateral triangle. Find the perimeter of ​​the figure.\n\n*(Note: Figure not draw to scale)*" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110441 1072 #uuid "58386834-0c84-5b68-a4e7-949625dae906" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110441 1074 "Let $a$ be the length of the sides of the square. Then, the length of each side of the triangle is also $a$ since it is equilateral and the square and the triangle have a common side.\n\n![](images/60/6080873a0fc8ad69d23e5ad29882be48754333f76c1872dd089110ff81f2ac19.png)\n\nWe're given that the area of a square is $16\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} &= 16 \\\\\na &= 4cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe perimeter of the figure is the sum of the lengths of\n\n- $3$ sides of the square (each of length $a$), and\n- $2$ sides of the triangle (each of length $a$).\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\np = 3a + 2a = 5a\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\na=4\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get that the perimeter of ​​the given shape is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 5a \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 20cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110442 1069 "e-3044" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110442 1071 "Given the figure below, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/8b/8b89eec86675b2bebfda74ecf1da8e0b7980e5de6d3a52de84e93e64a4a62068.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110442 1072 #uuid "11f90b96-0b4d-5f8d-8b0d-b4cde31fc7fd" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110442 1074 "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical.\nSince vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 12^{∘} &= 54^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 42^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 21^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110443 1069 "e-3113" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110443 1071 "What is the perimeter of the square $ABCD$ with vertices $A(0,0)$, $B(2,0)$, $C(2,2)$, and $D(0,2)$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110443 1072 #uuid "37072f91-b290-5e25-aae4-012b7f69f3ff" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110443 1074 "Let's plot the square using the given coordinates.\n\n![](images/3d/3d18273e4e60148e6999fed26c3212b2315ff8627315c1d0dfb98355df2faaee.png)\n\nSince our quadrilateral is a square,\n\n$$\nAB=BC=CD=AD\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can calculate the length of $\\overline{AB}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\mid x_{B} - x_{A} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 2 - 0 \\mid \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the polygon is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + CD + DA \\\\\n&= 4AB \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110444 1069 "e-3114" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110444 1071 "What is the perimeter of the parallelogram $ABCD$ with vertices $A(-1,1)$, $B(4,1)$, $C(6,3)$, and $D(1,3)$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110444 1072 #uuid "e067cd17-27ed-5faa-ac39-efa4c5568976" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110444 1074 "Let's plot the parallelogram using the given coordinates.\n\n![](images/21/2121d680f8fa8a5b3ab94300b67fbebac7c686620db95e323b3a2271ce6d7289.png)\n\nSince the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is horizontal, we can easily compute its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\mid x_{B} - x_{A} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 4 - (-1) \\mid \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the length of $\\overline{BC}$, we use the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\sqrt{(x_{C} - x_{B})^{2} + (y_{C} - y_{B})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(6 - 4)^{2} + (3 - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 4} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + AC + BC + CD \\\\\n&= 2AB + 2BC \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= 10 + 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110445 1069 "e-3117" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110445 1071 "Given that $A(2,2)$ and $C(5,5)$ are the coordinates of two opposite vertices of a square $ABCD$, what is the perimeter of the square?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110445 1072 #uuid "57aff09b-cf9d-5528-bc90-35e95feb6444" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110445 1074 "We need to calculate the measure of the sides of the square. To do that, we first find the measure of the diagonal $\\overline{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= \\sqrt{(x_{C} - x_{A})^{2} + (y_{C} - y_{A})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(5 - 2)^{2} + (5 - 2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{18} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $a$ is the measure of a side of the square, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{AC}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 4a \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110446 1069 "e-3118" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110446 1071 "The points $A(-6,4)$, $B(0,4)$, and $C(p,q)$ are the coordinates of the vertices of an isosceles triangle $\\triangle ABC$ with base $\\overline{AB}$ and perimeter $16$. Given that $q>4$, find the coordinates of $C$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110446 1072 #uuid "9ecd53f9-0786-5f02-8fb3-cf56f80c3368" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110446 1074 "Let's sketch our triangle.\n\n![](images/70/708e22844a8115b8eecac6ecd15a667dda870df428e09fbc96f56f8aad470ebf.png)\n\nSince the vertex $C$ is equidistant from $A$ and $B$, it lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment $\\overline{AB}$. In our case, this is the line $x=-3$. So the coordinates of $C$ are $(-3,q)$, where $q$ is an unknown parameter.\n\nFrom the diagram, we can calculate the length of $\\overline{AB}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\mid x_{B} - x_{A} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 0 - (-6) \\mid \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the length of $\\overline{AC}$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= \\sqrt{(x_{C} - x_{A})^{2} + (y_{C} - y_{A})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3 - (-6))^{2} + (q - 4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + (q - 4)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AC=BC$ and the perimeter is $16$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 &= AC + BC + AB \\\\\n16 &= 2AC + AB \\\\\n16 &= 2AC + 6 \\\\\n2AC &= 10 \\\\\nAC &= 5 \\\\\nAC^{2} &= 5^{2} \\\\\n9 + (q - 4)^{2} &= 25 \\\\\n(q - 4)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\nq - 4 &= ± 4 \\\\\nq &= 0, 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since we are told that $q > 4$, the coordinates of the point $C$ are $(-3,8)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110447 1069 "e-3121" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110447 1071 "Sketch the square $ABCD$ with vertices $A(1,3)$, $B(4,4)$, $C(5,1)$, and $D(2,0)$ in the coordinate plane, and find the area of the square." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110447 1072 #uuid "a2f283fd-ee27-5e51-b498-1ed60ab9eaae" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110447 1074 "Let's plot the square $ABCD$:\n\n![](images/78/7867db9c7e3f12d2a15d851568a1a4c017220220f0deafc38edc4302bbe2ec34.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of the square, we need to find the length of its side. For example, the length of the side $\\overline{BC}$ can be found using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\sqrt{(x_{C} - x_{B})^{2} + (y_{C} - y_{B})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(5 - 4)^{2} + (1 - 4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = BC^2 = (\\sqrt{10})^2 = 10\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110448 1069 "e-3143" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110448 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110448 1072 #uuid "b34736aa-e682-5ab3-85a0-dd86f49759f1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110448 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nWe now cancel the common factor of $\\sec^2 \\theta$ in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2\\sec^{2} θ}{\\sec^{2} θ} &= \\frac{2\\sec^{2} θ}{\\sec^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 1}{1} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110449 1069 "e-3171" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110449 1071 "Consider the diagram shown below. What transformation maps the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{C'}$?\n\n![](images/44/44ff3fefc7163c74485bd26b49fa872395c7bf254bb540c7d1af71df03cd7324.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110449 1072 #uuid "35f10d91-a3f0-5788-8459-02a71d606b3e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110449 1074 "Notice that both circles have a radius of $2$. So, the two circles are congruent.\n\nSince the circles are congruent, mapping $\\mathcal C$ onto $\\mathcal C'$ can be done using only a translation, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d4/d4049a99ab40d9e001a184ddf9cccbee75da5ce20f6de27f6392158c3efdad30.png)\n\nTherefore, to map the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{C'}$, we apply a translation of $4$ units to the right and $2$ units up." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110450 1069 "e-3172" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110450 1071 "What transformation maps the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ with center at the origin and radius $2$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{C'}$ with center at the origin and radius $8$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110450 1072 #uuid "d31e1e7e-11ef-55dd-a430-3386bab0cc13" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110450 1074 "Notice that both circles have center $O$. So, mapping $\\mathcal{C}$ onto $\\mathcal{C'}$ can be done using only a dilation.\n\nThe radius of $\\mathcal C$ is $r_1 = 2$ units, and the radius of $\\mathcal C'$ is $r_2 = 8$ units. Therefore, the required dilation has a scale factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{r_2}{r_1} = \\dfrac{8}{2} = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, to map the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{C'}$, we apply a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $4$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110451 1069 "e-3173" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110451 1071 "![](images/e1/e172b8216e2954267a870a17c7095c9127f133cac7c3d88795ffb9ba161f5dd8.png)\n\nConsider the following functions:\n\n$f(x,y)=(x+p,y+q), \\qquad g(x,y)=(kx,ky)$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is applied to $\\mathcal C$, and then $g(x,y)$ is applied to the result. The image of $\\mathcal C$ under this sequence of transformations is $\\mathcal C'$. What is $p+q+k$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110451 1072 #uuid "2871ac63-ac3c-5736-8be6-17eeb1e9525b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110451 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n- the function $f(x,y) = (x+p,y+q)$ represents a translation of $p$ units right and $q$ units up, and\n- the function $g(x,y) = (kx,ky)$ is a dilation of scale factor $k$.\n\nA translation of $4$ units right, represented by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = (x+4,y)\n$$\n\nmaps the circle $\\mathcal C$ with center $A$ and radius $2$ onto the circle $\\mathcal C''$ with center $O$ and radius $2$. Therefore, $p=4$ and $q=0$.\n\n![](images/fb/fb11fc9a0d625ae0e2025c5749c808090565d2e0dc3f47a6fc2e0c032f8fb1be.png)\n\nThen, a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nmaps the circle $\\mathcal C''$ onto the circle $\\mathcal C'$ with center $O$ and radius $1$.\n\n![](images/db/dbf563bc441247f54dadfbdb6ff42595a4cb3f8a3c77b65cdb2bd677302c024f.png)\n\nThe function that performs this dilation is\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = \\left(\\dfrac12x, \\dfrac12x \\right)\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np+q+k = 4+0+\\dfrac12 = \\dfrac92\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110452 1069 "e-3174" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110452 1071 "![](images/db/db3249a61aa432765309aee24aec04700d9e681a926060508522d29507efb609.png)\n\nThe following transformation maps the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{C'}$:\n\n> *A translation of $\\textrm{_____}$ and $\\textrm{_____}$, followed by a dilation with center $B$ and scale factor $\\textrm{_____}$.*\n\nWhat should be substituted into the blank spaces?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110452 1072 #uuid "bce4c5b9-26d0-5ac4-9630-299c4c37c1a0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110452 1074 "First, we apply a translation of $3$ units to the left and $3$ units down. This maps the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ with center $A$ and radius $2$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{C''}$ with center $B$ and radius $2$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/feeaf5e97ac7767faadf4f630e2d235760b69fa4a6305879df681f9375aab412.png)\n\nThen, a dilation with center $B$ and a scale factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nmaps the circle $\\mathcal{C''}$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{C'}$.\n\n![](images/94/9472a8726e2c97ffabdc9e0855ad79a9281f4799f6999449001055d5d244f32b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct sentence that describes the mapping is as follows:\n\n> *A translation of **$\\boldsymbol{3}$ units left** and **$\\boldsymbol{3}$ units down,** followed by a dilation with center $B$ and scale factor $\\boldsymbol{\\dfrac{1}{2}}$.*" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110453 1069 "e-3349" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110453 1071 "![](images/69/69e1bae966baea90d6f2395bd4db263d3a564f53912f67d4e0812243d28550e3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta = 100^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane. What is its reference angle $\\theta_R$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110453 1072 #uuid "1a73bf50-94b6-536b-8213-eb3d0b3ea1a3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110453 1074 "The reference angle, denoted $\\theta_R$, is the angle that\n\n$$\n\\theta=100^\\circ\n$$\n\nmakes with the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/e4/e453e8a82b70d69009db59f161a798cb57c85602d79b4bcc44757cd2a192b860.png)\n\nSince $\\theta$ is in the second quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= 180^{∘} - θ \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 100^{∘} \\\\\n&= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110454 1069 "e-3350" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110454 1071 "If the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{9}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way, then what is its reference angle?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110454 1072 #uuid "36c616f3-67df-5833-90a6-81ee6966e1a3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110454 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{11\\pi} {9}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 220^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11\\pi} {9}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $220^\\circ$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n![](images/96/964e1116b6f9273321faf21f3f30c325ec81f49622eab951bc6a54823f3c7079.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted $\\theta_R$, is the angle that $\\theta$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta$ is in the third quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\theta - \\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= \\frac{11π}{9} - π = \\frac{2π}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110455 1069 "e-3352" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110455 1071 "![](images/75/75717526542bddffb22bbf8ca2593187fea84724fdd107b1327c049e6495a57b.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta$ is measured in the usual way. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $0.74$, calculate the reference angle $\\theta_R$ in degrees to the nearest integer." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110455 1072 #uuid "743ae44e-1a0c-5015-a292-f4e0f89cbfc1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110455 1074 "First, we create a right triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3cf6be5c87565683466ecf91646e3587accc41cd4d1774ec6aaed007f1e809.png)\n\nSo we have the following triangle, where $\\theta_R$ is the reference angle of $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/51/51bfc8624d27c386ceaaea38ab99ac1d96ed880e5674b90c9dd00b15579b0d6a.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle.\n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta_R = \\dfrac{0.74}{1} = 0.74\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\arccos\\left(0.74\\right) \\approx 42^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110456 1069 "e-3353" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110456 1071 "An angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 30^\\circ$. Which of the following cannot be $\\theta$?\n\n1. $30^\\circ$\n2. $150^\\circ$\n3. $240^\\circ$\n4. $330^\\circ$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110456 1072 #uuid "591bf84d-62a7-5c9b-ada2-fdb2de62462b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110456 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between $\\theta_R$ and $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/57/57e85a4bfae7edc05e1328e4c275b6271bc9324fea64fd41d757a3de07f0a8c6.png)\n\nUsing\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nlet's calculate all possible values of $\\theta$.\n\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n$θ = θ_{R} = 30^{∘}$.\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the second quadrant, then\n$θ|= 180^{∘} - θ_{R}; = 180^{∘} - 30^{∘}; = 150^{∘}$.\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the third quadrant, then\n$θ|= 180^{∘} + θ_{R}; = 180^{∘} + 30^{∘}; = 210^{∘}$.\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n$θ|= 360^{∘} - θ_{R}; = 360^{∘} - 30^{∘}; = 330^{∘}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\theta$ cannot be equal to $240^\\circ$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110457 1069 "e-3386" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110457 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\cot x$?\n\n![](images/94/947b0865b172c5b8f06618a8a934587ee82171f34ae78a16527b3388c36eaf53.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110457 1072 #uuid "9ac6f5c4-a484-52b0-8f62-f873443616f3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110457 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\cot x$:\n\n- The domain is all real numbers except at the integer multiples of $\\pi$: $x= 0,\\pm\\pi,\\pm 2\\pi,\\dots$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The period of the function is $\\pi$.\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n- The zeros occur at $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points that are not in the domain: $x=0, \\pm\\pi,\\pm 2\\pi,\\dots$, and at each asymptote, the graph tends to $-\\infty$ from the left and $\\infty$ from the right.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is II." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110458 1069 "e-3391" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110458 1071 "Which of the following is the equation of a vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\cot x$?\n$x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad x=2\\pi, \\quad x=-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, \\quad x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110458 1072 #uuid "98b33c41-0792-504c-9b79-0bbaf9ee24a1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110458 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\cot x$.\n\n![](images/e0/e01402141f79b247b05980f7f4e830c112a7dcdc24232a8a4c9beefc1de6f603.png)\n\nThe graph has vertical asymptotes at the points that are not in the domain:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nx=0, \\pm\\pi,\\pm 2\\pi,\\dots\n$$\n\nAt each asymptote, the graph tends to $-\\infty$ from the left and $\\infty$ from the right.\n\nOf the given equations, the only one that corresponds to an asymptote of the function is $x=2\\pi$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110459 1069 "e-3470" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110459 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta \\, (1 + \\cot^2 \\theta)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110459 1072 #uuid "eceb2f88-2948-5037-bdbb-60483b3a2f34" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110459 1074 "First, using the identity\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 \\theta = \\csc^2 \\theta\n$$\n\nwe rewrite the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin θ(1 + \\cot^{2} θ) = \\sin θ \\cdot \\csc^{2} θ\n$$\n\nThen, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\csc {\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nwe can further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ \\cdot \\csc^{2} θ &= \\sin θ \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\sin θ \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin θ \\cdot \\sin θ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&= \\csc θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110460 1069 "e-3472" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110460 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\csc^2{\\theta} - 1}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110460 1072 #uuid "0d77018a-b39f-57f6-b2b8-646c1ed73b9e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110460 1074 "First, let's recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\csc^2{\\theta} - 1 = \\cot^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\csc^{2} θ - 1} &= \\frac{1}{\\cot^{2} θ} = \\tan^{2} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110461 1069 "e-3473" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110461 1071 "Simplify $\\csc x + \\csc x \\cot^2 x -\\csc^3x$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110461 1072 #uuid "e3b17108-28ce-5a36-b211-05e6671c1f37" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110461 1074 "First, we factor $\\csc x$ from the first two terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc x + \\csc x\\cot^{2} x - \\csc^{3} x &= \\csc x(1 + \\cot^{2} x) - \\csc^{3} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the cotangent-cosecant identity,\n\n$$\n1+ \\cot^2 x=\\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc x(1 + \\cot^{2} x) - \\csc^{3} x &= \\csc x \\cdot (\\csc^{2} x) - \\csc^{3} x \\\\\n&= \\csc^{3} x - \\csc^{3} x \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110462 1069 "e-3480" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110462 1071 "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{5}{9}$. What is the $x$-coordinate of $A$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110462 1072 #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110462 1074 "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{5}{9}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\right)^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{25}{81}\\right)&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2&= 1-\\dfrac{25}{81} \\\\[5pt] x^2&= \\dfrac{56}{81} \\\\[5pt] x&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{56}{81}} \\\\[5pt] x&= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive. So, we select the positive answer.\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110463 1069 "e-3482" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110463 1071 "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110463 1072 #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110463 1074 "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{2}{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{4}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant.\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110464 1069 "e-3484" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110464 1071 "![](images/e5/e50848432dd18a4a5dc64d4e5a5cfb8b3e14e9fed6231774dd83ead5552cd463.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\csc\\theta$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110464 1072 #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110464 1074 "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{24}{49}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110465 1069 "e-3485" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110465 1071 "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5}$, what is $\\tan\\theta$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110465 1072 #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110465 1074 "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{4}{5})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{16}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110466 1069 "e-3497" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110466 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\csc \\theta + \\cot \\theta} + \\cot \\theta$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110466 1072 #uuid "4da93c71-9a1e-512e-9735-14d7c6221378" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110466 1074 "First, we recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n1= \\csc^2{\\theta} -\\cot^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, and then factoring it as a difference of squares, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\csc θ + \\cot θ} + \\cot θ &= \\frac{\\csc^{2} θ - \\cot^{2} θ}{\\csc θ + \\cot θ} + \\cot θ \\\\\n&= ((\\csc θ - \\cot θ)(\\csc θ + \\cot θ))/(\\csc θ + \\cot θ) + \\cot θ \\\\\n&= ((\\csc θ - \\cot θ)(\\csc θ + \\cot θ))/(\\csc θ + \\cot θ) + \\cot θ \\\\\n&= (\\csc θ - \\cot θ) + \\cot θ \\\\\n&= \\csc θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110467 1069 "e-3546" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110467 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2} \\sin{x} \\cos{x} = -\\sin{x}$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$. If the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ are written in ascending order, then what is $x_2\\cdot x_3$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110467 1072 #uuid "ef4a57fd-8ade-5b7d-9d6e-0fabd228e603" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110467 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x\\cos x &= -\\sin x \\\\\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x\\cos x + \\sin x &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin x(\\sqrt{2}\\cos x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1 =0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$ are $x = 0,\\, \\pi$.\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1= 0$, or equivalently $\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. The solution to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$ is $x =\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0,\\quad x_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},\\quad x_3= \\pi\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_2\\cdot x_3 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\cdot \\pi=\\dfrac{3\\pi^2}{4}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110468 1069 "e-3548" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110468 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\sin{\\theta}\\tan{\\theta}-\\sin{\\theta} = 0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$. If the solutions $\\theta_1, \\theta_2, \\theta_3, \\theta_4$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of $\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110468 1072 #uuid "4c5e27aa-f9a5-54c0-a15c-14b923eec893" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110468 1074 "Note that the function $\\tan{\\theta}$ is not defined at\n\n$$\n\\theta = 90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nso these values cannot be solutions to the equation.\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3}\\sin θ\\tan θ - \\sin θ &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin θ(\\sqrt{3}\\tan θ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta} - 1 =0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{\\theta} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$ are $\\theta = 0^\\circ, \\, 180^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta} - 1 =0$, or equivalently $\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$ are $\\theta=30^\\circ, \\, 210^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = 0^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_2= 30^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_3=180^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_4= 210^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3 =30 \\cdot 180 = 5\\,400\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110469 1069 "e-3550" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110469 1071 "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} \\tan{x} = \\tan{x}$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110469 1072 #uuid "362274e9-6980-5e3b-896b-db42cfc41a9e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110469 1074 "Note that the function $\\csc{x}$ is not defined at the points where\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0\n$$\n\nOn the interval\n\n$$\n0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nthe excluded values are\n\n$$\nx = 0^\\circ, 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\tan{x}$ is not defined at the points where\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = 0\n$$\n\nIn the given interval, the excluded values are\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc x\\tan x &= \\tan x \\\\\n\\csc x\\tan x - \\tan x &= 0 \\\\\n(\\csc x - 1)\\tan x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} - 1 = 0, \\qquad \\tan{x} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\csc{x} -1= 0$, or equivalently, $\\csc x=1$. The solution to this equation for $0 ^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ is $x = 90^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\tan{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ\\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ are $x = 0^\\circ,\\, 180^\\circ$.\n\nFinally, taking into account the values excluded from the interval, we conclude that the given equation has no solution." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110470 1069 "e-3551" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110470 1071 "The graph shows the curve $y = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}$ and the curve $y = \\cos{x}$ on the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$. The curves intersect at the points $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$, as shown. Find $x$-coordinates of these points.\n\n![](images/d3/d3462ebb123dcaf59391787ffae5b39dea5809eefe656cfda2f17498259dc1b6.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110470 1072 #uuid "0aff48c8-380d-504b-a044-8ba08a2617a4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110470 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives\nthe equation\n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x\\sin x - \\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\cos x(2\\sin x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = 0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\cos{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x= 90^\\circ, \\, 270^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0$, or equivalently $\\sin{x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x = 30^\\circ,\\, 150^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=30^\\circ, \\quad x_2=90^\\circ,\\quad x_3=150^\\circ, \\quad x_4 = 270^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110471 1069 "e-3708" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110471 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\arcsin x$?\n\n![](images/6e/6ec23eefe628a19ee0b188830a813676dd91afe79eab082d4f08085a6e9c7225.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110471 1072 #uuid "5e74c09c-ebb2-56f2-98f1-953e6e7e3fcc" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110471 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1]$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}2, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right]$.\n- The minimum value occurs at $x=-1$.\n- The maximum value occurs at $x=1$.\n- The zero occurs at $x=0$.\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is III." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110472 1069 "e-3714" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110472 1071 "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\arcsin x$?\n\n1. $-3$\n2. $0$\n3. $2$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110472 1072 #uuid "62d7cf82-1892-5bf2-96f8-5e2f0b76e5bf" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110472 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that the domain of the function is\n\n$$\nx=\\left[ -1, 1\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=0$ lies in the domain of the\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nwhile the values $x=-3$ and $x=2$ do not." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110473 1069 "e-3715" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110473 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\arcsin x$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac \\pi 2$\n2. $\\pi$\n3. $-2$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110473 1072 #uuid "04b531a3-9e17-58b0-b25f-e09e6ddf0bdb" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110473 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that the range of the function is\n\n$$\ny\\in\\left[-\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\dfrac\\pi 2\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nlies in the range of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nwhile the values $x=\\pi$ and $x=-2$ do not." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110474 1069 "e-3716" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110474 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\arcsin x$?\n\n1. $\\left(\\dfrac 1 2,\\dfrac {\\pi}{2}\\right)$\n2. $\\left(0,\\pi\\right)$\n3. $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110474 1072 #uuid "aec97277-ccea-523c-b84e-cce4857b6bfd" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110474 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nthe following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\sin x$.\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\arcsin x$, which is $y\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.\n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\sin x$. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arcsin x$.\n\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(0,\\pi \\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\pi,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\sin x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$The range of $y=\\arcsin x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, and $y=\\pi$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(0,\\pi\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arcsin x$.\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\sin x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ The range of $y=\\arcsin x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, and $y=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ lies within this range. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\arcsin x$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110475 1069 "e-3758" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110475 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\arccos x$?\n\n![](images/91/9118c2af79b18975d79e8f7788199e81b108242751f03b59c9131429dfb60947.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110475 1072 #uuid "f6f648e7-2543-5f0d-8556-9ed0d6ea9f14" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110475 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1]$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi\\right]$.\n- The minimum value occurs at $x=1$.\n- The maximum value occurs at $x=-1$.\n- The $y$-intercept is $y=\\dfrac \\pi 2$.\n- The function is strictly decreasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is I." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110476 1069 "e-3759" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110476 1071 "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\arccos x$?\n\n1. $-2$\n2. $0$\n3. $1$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110476 1072 #uuid "da99c8cf-8609-5a74-b5a0-586912ae88ec" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110476 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that the domain of the function is\n\n$$\nx=\\left[ -1, 1\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values $x=0$ and $x=1$ lie in the domain of the function, while the value $x=-2$ does not." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110477 1069 "e-3760" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110477 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\arccos x$?\n\n1. $-\\dfrac \\pi 2$\n2. $0$\n3. $\\pi$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110477 1072 #uuid "c2e6f348-0412-571d-9b80-0b2bed42a2ba" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110477 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that the range of the function is\n\n$$\ny=\\left[ 0, \\pi\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values $y=0$ and $y=\\pi$ lie in the range of the function, while the value\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ndoes not." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110478 1069 "e-3762" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110478 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\arccos x$?\n\n1. $\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2,\\dfrac {\\pi}{3}\\right)$\n2. $\\left(0,-\\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)$\n3. $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110478 1072 #uuid "5dcbabf0-542f-59a7-8ca1-b7461359bd35" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110478 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\nthe following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\arccos x$, which is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi \\right]$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.\n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\cos x$. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arccos x$.\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(0,-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi 2,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\cos x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$The range of $y=\\arccos x$ is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi\\right]$, and $y=-\\dfrac\\pi 2$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(0,-\\dfrac\\pi 2\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arccos x$.\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\cos x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ The range of $y=\\arccos x$ is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi\\right]$, and $y=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ lies within this range. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\arccos x$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110479 1069 "e-3868" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110479 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y = \\sin x$?\n\n![](images/d2/d2bd1a95a81f860d152b1e2832b18b4736ca1ae769859fcddb10235476ac0aeb.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110479 1072 #uuid "94c79b0f-848e-5a8f-a047-729b6266087b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110479 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sin{x}$:\n\n- The minimum value is $-1$, and the maximum value is $1$.\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi$.\n- Its zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots$.\n- Its maximum values occur at $\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\ldots$\n- Its minimum values occur at $\\ldots,-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\ldots$\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that displays all of these properties is I." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110480 1069 "e-3869" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110480 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a zero of $y=\\sin{x}$?\n\n$0, \\quad -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad 2\\pi, \\quad -\\pi$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110480 1072 #uuid "20008dac-3626-5679-a0db-7dd791cfe7af" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110480 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\sin{x}$.\n\n![](images/98/98ce491ac80dbb159c9b736baad82b047db8fc869fa17b6b9e231c6b4f5c2dd8.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots$.\n\nOf the given values, the only one that is not a zero of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110481 1069 "e-3870" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110481 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y = \\cos x$?\n\n![](images/7b/7be2fd3e755b13b216099e3d6cf77a089a6079362aec7f995d9184d3724f3cb9.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110481 1072 #uuid "7347a0dd-2abc-574a-818f-cedb0b68d16a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110481 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\cos{x}$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1]$.\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi$.\n- Its zeroes occur at $x= \\dots,\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- Its maximum values occur at $x=0,\\pm2\\pi, \\pm4\\pi,\\dots$\n- Its minimum values occur at $x=\\pm\\pi,\\pm 3\\pi,\\dots$\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is II." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110482 1069 "e-3873" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110482 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\tan(x)$?\n\n![](images/41/4163fa9882994c15527ad87419ee53c882e15c0baf61dfbc99a59c022694b3d1.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110482 1072 #uuid "568c6bf6-1db5-5771-a364-8eb61e5aafa7" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110482 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\tan x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The period of the function is $\\pi$.\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n- The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\pm2\\pi,\\dots$\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$. At each asymptote, the graph tends to $\\infty$ from the left and $-\\infty$ from the right.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is III." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110483 1069 "e-3874" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110483 1071 "Which of the following is a value of $x$ that minimizes the graph of $y=\\cos{x}$?\n$-2\\pi, \\quad 0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}2, \\quad -\\pi$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110483 1072 #uuid "06b9341c-3fd7-5641-b478-ec38901c43a4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110483 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\cos{x}\\mathbin{.}\n$$\n\n![](images/80/80fe435e47fb22b950ac9546b48eb875dbba241fd5578bcafdc75448807661ca.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the cosine function reaches its minimum value when $x=-\\pi$ and $x=\\pi$.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is $x=-\\pi$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110484 1069 "e-3878" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110484 1071 "Which of the following $x$-values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\tan x$?\n$0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110484 1072 #uuid "883ed11c-51d8-58dc-a91f-603460cf2aa7" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110484 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan x$.\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nThe domain of the function contains all real numbers except for\n\n$$\nx= \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots\n$$\n\nOf the given values, the only one that is **not** in the domain is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110485 1069 "e-3887" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110485 1071 "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^3$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\n![](images/0d/0d3ede3cfbec765228ea184c74e0cb35f1df2a9340bd1cb703a0990249fecf30.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110485 1072 #uuid "61af7bef-751d-5c35-9e5f-cdb649957e68" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110485 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}x^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n0 \\in \\left[-1,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\n, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [-1,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\nA_1 = -\\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor\n\n$$\nx \\in\\left[0,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\n, we have\n\n$$\nA_2 = \\int_{0}^{3/2}\\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThe total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 =-\\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{3/2} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFinally, we carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= -\\int_{-1}^{0}\\frac{x^{3}}{2}dx + \\int_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{x^{3}}{2}dx \\\\\n&= - [\\frac{x^{4}}{8}]_{-1}^{0} + [\\frac{x^{4}}{8}]_{0}^{3/2} \\\\\n&= - [0 - \\frac{1}{8}] + [\\frac{81}{128} - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} + \\frac{81}{128} \\\\\n&= \\frac{97}{128}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110486 1069 "e-3888" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110486 1071 "The total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=x^3-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=2$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a$?\n$A = \\displaystyle -\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{a} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x$\n\n![](images/57/57d01ea60e3cd9770f2ef978e46e2cef7b69a9fcdc2ac478d5b6c73d8f366c7b.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110486 1072 #uuid "19c30733-019b-5e03-90c8-988114cb3135" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110486 1074 "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=x^3-1$. So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{3} - 1 \\\\\n(0) &= x^{3} - 1 \\\\\nx^{3} &= 1 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1 \\in [-2,2]$, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$ to $x=2$.\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nand the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{\\color{blue}1} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{\\color{blue}1}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110487 1069 "e-3889" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110487 1071 "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=x^3-9x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110487 1072 #uuid "64490be9-a9f9-5580-9ac3-99637c273100" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110487 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - 9x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 9) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 3)(x + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$, $x=-3$, and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $0 \\in [-3,3]$, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3$.\n\n![](images/29/29a51c9892de59037e84bace2f8b5822222c3056a05af861cbd741390a437a61.png)\n\nFor $x \\in [-3,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= \\int_{a}^{b}y(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-3}^{0}(x^{3} - 9x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-3}^{0}x^{3}dx - 9\\int_{-3}^{0}xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right|_{-3}^{0} - \\left.\\frac{9x^{2}}{2}\\right|_{-3}^{0} \\\\\n&= [0 - ((-3)^{4})/(4)] - 9[0 - ((-3)^{2})/(2)] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{81}{4} + \\frac{81}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{81}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(x^3-9x\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{2} &= -\\int_{0}^{3}(x^{3} - 9x)dx \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{3}x^{3}dx + 9\\int_{0}^{3}xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.- \\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{3} + \\left.\\frac{9x^{2}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= - [((3)^{4})/(4) - 0] + 9[((3)^{2})/(2) - 0] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{81}{4} + \\frac{81}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{81}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 = \\dfrac{81}{4} + \\dfrac{81}{4} = \\dfrac{81}{2}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110488 1069 "e-3956" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110488 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\arctan x$?\n\n![](images/87/87afc418078cffde22caf596794449f501cc67949abf7537e6411d248b9eb1e5.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110488 1072 #uuid "c34bce13-5054-5da9-8794-c9c152969ab8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110488 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n- There is a zero at $x=0$.\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty, 0)$ and positive over $(0, \\infty)$.\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n- $y\\to-\\dfrac \\pi 2$ (from above) as $x\\to-\\infty$.\n- $y\\to\\dfrac \\pi 2$ (from below) as $x\\to\\infty$.\n- The lines $y=\\pm\\dfrac \\pi 2$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is IV." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110489 1069 "e-3957" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110489 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\arctan x$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac \\pi 2$\n2. $\\dfrac \\pi 4$\n3. $-\\dfrac \\pi 3$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110489 1072 #uuid "b5293390-69dc-54d5-bfde-3580a0d5cdc0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110489 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that the range of the function is\n\n$$\ny\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac \\pi 3\n$$\n\nare in the range of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\nHowever,\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nis *not* in the range of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\nSo the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110490 1069 "e-3958" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110490 1071 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y = \\arctan x$?\n\n1. $2\\pi$\n2. $-1$\n3. $0$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110490 1072 #uuid "be456bc5-fbcc-5b04-a2d4-efdd7223aefd" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110490 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that the function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110491 1069 "e-3960" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110491 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\arctan x$?\n\n1. $\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3,\\sqrt 3\\right)$\n2. $\\left(0, \\pi\\right)$\n3. $\\left(0,0\\right)$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110491 1072 #uuid "74e3884e-e32f-553a-b832-b71fdc6a9172" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110491 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\nthe following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\tan x$.\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\arctan x$, which is $y\\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.\n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3,\\sqrt 3\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\sqrt 3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\tan x$. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3,\\sqrt 3\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arctan x$.\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(0,\\pi\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\pi,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\tan x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ The range of $y=\\arctan x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, and $y=\\pi$ does not lie within this range. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\pi, 0\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arctan x$.\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\tan x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$The range of $y=\\arctan x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\arctan x$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110492 1069 "e-4014" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110492 1071 "Express $\\log_3(2)+\\log_3(5)$ as a single logarithm." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110492 1072 #uuid "4e63488a-97a0-5b21-ac91-2f0c9a1efa54" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110492 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} 2 + \\log_{3} 5 &= \\log_{3} (2 \\cdot 5) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110493 1069 "e-4015" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110493 1071 "Express $3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3}$ as a single logarithm." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110493 1072 #uuid "07d810ce-010c-5a07-8174-f01d9b807a60" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110493 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\log_{5} 7 + 3\\log_{5} 3 &= 3(\\log_{5} 7 + \\log_{5} 3) \\\\\n&= 3\\log_{5} (7 \\cdot 3) \\\\\n&= 3\\log_{5} 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110494 1069 "e-4016" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110494 1071 "Express $\\ln\\left(x^2 y\\right)$ as a sum of two logarithms." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110494 1072 #uuid "679a8e3b-ef6c-565d-ab37-613dedfdf3df" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110494 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln{a} + \\ln{b} = \\ln(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (x^{2}y) &= \\ln (x^{2} \\cdot y) \\\\\n&= \\ln (x^{2}) + \\ln y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110495 1069 "e-4018" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110495 1071 "If $\\log_2(3)=k$ and $\\log_2(x)=p$, what is the value of $\\log_2(3x)$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110495 1072 #uuid "39a1c600-8d35-5671-bcb1-62682d750cd3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110495 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{2} (3x) &= \\log_{2} 3 + \\log_{2} x \\\\\n&= k + p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110496 1069 "e-4020" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110496 1071 "Express $\\log_3\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)$ as a difference of logarithms." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110496 1072 #uuid "85379129-7b9e-507f-b70a-841c868cfa15" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110496 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} (\\frac{2}{5}) &= \\log_{3} (2) - \\log_{3} (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110497 1069 "e-4022" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110497 1071 "Write $\\log_5(16) - \\log_5(8)$ as a single logarithm." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110497 1072 #uuid "8a8c6256-216f-5516-ac07-80311c557f4b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110497 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{5} (16) - \\log_{5} (8) &= \\log_{5} (\\frac{16}{8}) \\\\\n&= \\log_{5} (2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110498 1069 "e-4023" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110498 1071 "Express $2\\log_2(25) - 2\\log_2(5)$ as a single logarithm." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110498 1072 #uuid "23ccceb8-73ee-58c7-9787-ae1eb6b3f53f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110498 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\log_{2} (25) - 2\\log_{2} (5) &= 2(\\log_{2} (25) - \\log_{2} (5)) \\\\\n&= 2(\\log_{2} (\\frac{25}{5})) \\\\\n&= 2\\log_{2} (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\log_2(25) - 2\\log_2(5) = 2\\log_2(5)\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110499 1069 "e-4025" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110499 1071 "If $\\ln (p) =6$ and $\\ln (q) = 4$, then $\\ln \\left(\\dfrac p q\\right)=$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110499 1072 #uuid "85ee2fdc-9c05-5795-bcdf-adb3a871cc5a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110499 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln(a) - \\ln(b) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for natural logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (\\frac{p}{q}) &= \\ln (p) - \\ln (q) \\\\\n&= 6 - 4 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110500 1069 "e-4067" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110500 1071 "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110500 1072 #uuid "7c937587-dc81-5ee9-aa66-a509a87433e2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110500 1074 "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{x + 1} &= 3 \\\\\n\\log_{2} (2^{x + 1}) &= \\log_{2} (3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\log_b \\left(b^c \\right) = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\log_{2} (3) \\\\\nx &= \\log_{2} (3) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx= \\log_2 \\left(3 \\right) - 1\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110501 1069 "e-4069" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110501 1071 "Given that $3^{2x+3}=5$, find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110501 1072 #uuid "45d74b50-fd9d-5d18-835d-a7e140278a34" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110501 1074 "Taking $\\log_3$ of both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3^{2x+3} &= 5 \\\\[3pt] \\log_3 \\left(3^{2x+3}\\right) &= \\log_3 (5) \\\\[3pt] 2x+3 &= \\log_3 (5). \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= (\\log (5))/(\\log (3)) \\\\\n2x &= (\\log (5))/(\\log (3)) - 3 \\\\\nx &= (\\log (5))/(2\\log (3)) - \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx -0.768\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** We used the change of base formula to rewrite the right-hand side, as many calculators can only compute base $10$ logarithms." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110502 1069 "e-4071" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110502 1071 "Given that $3\\cdot 6^{x} = 9$, find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110502 1072 #uuid "5d31a3e8-aae6-50b5-9ce1-65cef1d8395e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110502 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot 6^{x} &= 9 \\\\\n6^{x} &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take $\\log_{6}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6^x &= 3 \\\\[3pt] \\log_6 \\left(6^x\\right) &= \\log_6 (3) \\\\[3pt] x &= \\log_6 (3) \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\log(3)}{\\log(6)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.613\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110503 1069 "e-4078" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110503 1071 "By expressing $\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)$ in the form $k\\ln x$, determine the value of $k$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110503 1072 #uuid "bf0e781a-8bf7-51c8-a67b-031449fee038" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110503 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left(c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (x^{2}) &= 2\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k =2$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110504 1069 "e-4079" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110504 1071 "By expressing $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_3{x}$ in the form $\\log_3 \\left(x^k\\right)$, determine the value of $k$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110504 1072 #uuid "53a33ca5-8a37-5ec7-acb2-00e3471a1c6a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110504 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left(c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{3} x &= \\log_{3} (x^{1/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110505 1069 "e-4080" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110505 1071 "Find the value of $\\ln\\left(x^3\\right)$ if $\\ln(x) = 0.2$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110505 1072 #uuid "7e755fc4-40b5-5d64-8087-ae67baad204c" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110505 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left(c^m \\right) = m\\ln{c}\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (x^{3}) &= 3 \\cdot \\ln (x) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 0.2 \\\\\n&= 0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110506 1069 "e-4127" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110506 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x) =\\ln(x+3)+6$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110506 1072 #uuid "fc54368a-099e-5b85-a6c4-15db9be3ee8f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110506 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+3)+6\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\ln (y + 3) + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\ln (y + 3) + 6 \\\\\n\\ln (y + 3) &= x - 6 \\\\\ne^{\\ln (y + 3)} &= e^{x - 6} \\\\\ny + 3 &= e^{x - 6} \\\\\ny &= e^{x - 6} - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = e^{x-6}-3\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110507 1069 "e-4128" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110507 1071 "The function $f(x) = e^{x-k}+2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)}+4$. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110507 1072 #uuid "2b08436f-c561-59c5-bb79-621a1780b470" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110507 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= e^{x - k} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= e^{y - k} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= e^{y - k} + 2 \\\\\ne^{y - k} &= x - 2 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{y - k}) &= \\ln (x - 2) \\\\\ny - k &= \\ln (x - 2) \\\\\ny &= \\ln (x - 2) + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)} + k\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=4$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110508 1069 "e-4140" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110508 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x) = 2\\log_4(3x)-1$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110508 1072 #uuid "c6d0d3ad-111a-5db1-b521-0c9416dfb6b8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110508 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2\\log_{4} (3x) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\log_{4} (3y) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\log_{4} (3y) - 1 \\\\\n2\\log_{4} (3y) &= x + 1 \\\\\n\\log_{4} (3y) &= \\frac{x + 1}{2} \\\\\n4^{\\log_{4} (3y)} &= 4^{(x + 1)/2} \\\\\n3y &= 4^{(x + 1)/2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}4^{(x + 1)/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110509 1069 "e-4146" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110509 1071 "Solve for $x$ if $\\ln(4x+5)=3$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110509 1072 #uuid "44c87021-177d-5f5c-b1f9-47eb08f107a6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110509 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e$. So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (4x + 5) &= 3 \\\\\ne^{\\ln (4x + 5)} &= e^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln(4x+5)} = 4x+5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 5 &= e^{3} \\\\\n4x &= e^{3} - 5 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{e^{3} - 5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110510 1069 "e-4147" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110510 1071 "Find the exact solution to $3\\ln (5x+1)-2 = 13$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110510 1072 #uuid "0cfb7d21-6ca4-55a3-a46a-7a765ed1b866" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110510 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\ln (5x + 1) - 2 &= 13 \\\\\n3\\ln (5x + 1) &= 15 \\\\\n\\ln (5x + 1) &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e$, so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (5x + 1) &= 5 \\\\\ne^{\\ln (5x + 1)} &= e^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln (5x+1)} = 5x+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 1 &= e^{5} \\\\\n5x &= e^{5} - 1 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{e^{5} - 1}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110511 1069 "e-4148" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110511 1071 "Find the values of $x$ such that $\\ln(1-2x) + 5\\ln(1-2x) = 4$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110511 1072 #uuid "85f82353-2436-5e48-984a-5e671ee54024" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110511 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 - 2x) + 5\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n(1 + 5)\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n6\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n\\ln (1 - 2x) &= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e$, so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 - 2x) &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\ne^{\\ln (1 - 2x)} &= e^{2/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln(1-2x)} = 1-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2x &= e^{2/3} \\\\\n1 - 2x &= \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} \\\\\n- 2x &= \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} - 1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} - 1}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-\\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} + 1}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1 - \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110512 1069 "e-4713" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110512 1071 "Express $4^{8} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{7}{4}\\right)^5$ as the product of two exponentiated integers." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110512 1072 #uuid "17173a8a-f28b-5456-8dbb-eb80d6d3cb1c" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110512 1074 "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n4^{8} \\cdot (\\frac{7}{4})^{5} = 4^{8} \\cdot \\frac{7^{5}}{4^{5}}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n4^{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{7^5}{4^5} = \\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5 =4^{8-5}\\cdot 7^5=4^{3}\\cdot 7^5\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110513 1069 "e-4714" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110513 1071 "Express $\\left(6^4\\right)^2\\cdot 6$ as a base raised to a single power." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110513 1072 #uuid "f145655c-ed98-578d-b7ab-7b18bfbfa107" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110513 1074 "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(6^4\\right)^2=6^{4\\cdot 2}=6^8\n$$\n\nThen we use the product rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n6^8 \\cdot 6= 6^8 \\cdot 6^1=6^{8+1}=6^9\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110514 1069 "e-4752" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110514 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{5^4\\cdot 5^2}{5^3}$ as a base raised to a single number." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110514 1072 #uuid "43141ab7-5ebf-5175-a512-e04f6646978d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110514 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator using the product rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5^4\\cdot 5^2}{5^3}= \\dfrac{5^{4+2}}{5^3} = \\dfrac{5^{6}}{5^3}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5^6}{5^3} = 5^{6-3} = 5^{3}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110515 1069 "e-5056" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110515 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^2< 1$ using a graphical method." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110515 1072 #uuid "852bef65-c612-58dc-84a0-0bf0e50836ce" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110515 1074 "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} & < 1 \\\\ x^{2} - 1 & < 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y = x^2-1$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 1^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x= \\pm 1$.\n\nSolving the inequality $x^2-1 < 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2-1$ is below the $x$-axis (where ${\\color{red}y < 0}$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies between the roots (but it does not include them), as shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52328002ef1d61eeedb0799bea1eb407d04dda0f064967401cd6f33423cc553c.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in {\\color{blue}(-1, 1)}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110516 1069 "e-5058" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110516 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^2< -9$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110516 1072 #uuid "23a456fd-177a-5d15-a579-48187703ddc4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110516 1074 "We can solve this equation using either of the two methods that we've learned.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality*\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, it is impossible for the square of a real number to be smaller than $-9$.\n\nSo, the inequality $x^2 < -9$ has no solutions.\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n$x^2+9 < 0$,\nand then plot the parabola\n\n$$\ny=x^2+9\n$$\n\nThe desired inequality ($y<0$) is never satisfied, so there are no solutions.\n\n![](images/9f/9f5f8968a32bbc7ae94386aa5fa2237942ebd25ed74a3390bfa440e3c4550002.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110517 1069 "e-5059" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110517 1071 "Solve $3x^2-6 > 3+2x^2$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110517 1072 #uuid "b974e974-41fc-573e-bab3-7668ca38a79a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110517 1074 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 6 & > 3 + 2x^{2} \\\\ 3x^{2} - 2x^{2} & > 6 + 3 \\\\ x^{2} & > 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{2}} \\mid > \\sqrt{9}; \\mid x \\mid \\mid > 3\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have $x>3$ or $x<-3$.\n\nSo, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,-3)\\cup(3,+\\infty)$.\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from $x^2>9$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\nx^2-9 > 0\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y=x^2-9$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 3)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 3$.\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $x^2-9$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside of the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](images/1c/1c0d933b969094b2665e539479d5f9f715be881f9e355002c9398e0f23ba9938.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,-3)\\cup(3,+\\infty)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110518 1069 "e-5060" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110518 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^2 \\leq 2$ using the absolute value method." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110518 1072 #uuid "956690fb-3601-51c6-92a8-7187272ea358" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110518 1074 "Taking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\nx^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\le \\sqrt{2}; \\mid x \\mid \\mid \\le \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 2\\leq x \\leq \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in [-\\sqrt 2,\\sqrt 2]$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110519 1069 "e-5161" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110519 1071 "Rebecca invested $\\[math]10,000$ into an account paying a $3\\%$ annual interest, compounded monthly. How much money will be in her account after $10$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110519 1072 #uuid "6d75946e-ba1d-5f7b-b880-2f0ad83e5e4e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110519 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ months in one year, so $n=12$. We also have an initial amount of $P_0=10,000$, an annual interest of $r=0.03$, and a length of $t=10$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac {r} {n} \\right)^{(n)(t)} \\\\[5pt] &= 10,000\\left(1 + \\dfrac {0.03} {12} \\right)^{(12)(10)} \\\\[5pt] &= 10,000(1 + 0.0025)^{120} \\\\[5pt] &= 10,000(1.0025)^{120} \\\\[5pt] &= 10,000(1.349\\,353...) \\\\[5pt] &= 13,493.53... \\\\[5pt] &\\approx 13,494. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, after $10$ years Rebecca will have $\\[math]13,494$ in the account." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110520 1069 "e-5163" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110520 1071 "How much money would you need to deposit today at a $2\\%$ annual interest rate, compounded yearly, to have $\\[math]10,000$ in the account after $10$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110520 1072 #uuid "3723a7d8-f191-548c-8c2d-d647aac03a48" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110520 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the payments are compounded yearly, we have $n=1$. We also have an annual rate of $r=0.02$, and a final amount of $P=10,000$ after $t=10$ years. To find the initial deposit, $P_0$, we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $P_0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac {r} {n}\\right)^{nt} \\\\[5pt] 10,000 &=P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac {0.02} {1}\\right)^{(1)(10)} \\\\[5pt] 10,000 &= P_0(1.02)^{10} \\\\[5pt] 10,000 &= P_0(1.218,994...) \\\\[5pt] P_0 &= \\dfrac {10,000} {1.218,994...} \\\\[5pt] P_0 &= 8,203.48... \\\\[5pt] P_0 &\\approx 8,203 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, you would need to deposit $\\[math]8,203$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110521 1069 "e-5164" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110521 1071 "If you deposit $\\[math]4,000$ dollars into an account with a $6\\%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $\\[/math]8,000$ in the account?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110521 1072 #uuid "6367b497-624e-54b3-9890-0463c2c4ac7f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110521 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4$. We also have an initial amount of $P_0=4,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.06$.\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until\n\n$$\nP\\geq 8,000\n$$\n\nwe substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P = P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt} &\\geq 8,000 \\\\[5pt] 4,000\\left(1 + \\dfrac {0.06} {4} \\right)^{4t} &\\geq 8,000 \\\\[5pt] (1 + 0.015)^{4t} &\\geq \\dfrac {8,000} {4,000} \\\\[5pt] 1.015^{4t} &\\geq 2 \\\\[5pt] \\log \\left(1.015^{4t}\\right) &\\geq \\log 2 \\\\[5pt] 4t\\log 1.015 &\\geq \\log 2 \\\\[5pt] 4t &\\geq \\dfrac {\\log 2} {\\log 1.015} \\\\[5pt] 4t &\\geq 46.56... \\\\[5pt] t &\\geq \\dfrac{46.56...}{4} \\approx 11.64 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $11.5$ or $11.75$ years.\n\n- After $11.5$ years, there is still less than $\\[math]8,000$ in the account, because $11.5<11.64$.\n- After $11.75$ years, there will be more than $\\[/math]8,000$ in the account, since $11.75>11.64$.\n\nTherefore, it will take $11.75$ years ($11$ years and $3$ quarters) until there is at least $\\[math]8,000$ in the account." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110522 1069 "e-5981" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110522 1071 "How many terms are there in the expansion of $(a-b+3d)^5$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110522 1072 #uuid "29419752-68d2-5d17-9eaf-7cbdb80af4cf" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110522 1074 "The number of distinct terms in the multinomial expansion with $t$ variables raised to the $n$th power is given by\n$\\binom{n+t-1}{t-1}$.\n\nHere we have a multinomial expansion with $t=3$ variables raised to the $n=5$th power. Therefore, the number of distinct terms in the expansion of $(a-b+3d)^5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{5 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1}) &= (\\frac{7}{2}) = 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110523 1069 "e-5982" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110523 1071 "How many terms are there in the expansion of $(x+y+w+z)^7$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110523 1072 #uuid "b499ce8f-83a6-5504-adc8-485f5b58d912" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110523 1074 "The number of distinct terms in the multinomial expansion with $t$ variables raised to the $n$th power is given by\n$\\binom{n+t-1}{t-1}$.\n\nHere we have a multinomial expansion with $t=4$ variables raised to the $n=7$th power. Therefore, the number of distinct terms in the expansion of $(x+y+w+z)^7$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{7 + 4 - 1}{4 - 1}) &= (\\frac{10}{3}) = 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110524 1069 "e-6235" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110524 1071 "The triangle shown below is translated using the function $f(x,y) = (x+2,y-3)$. Plot the resulting triangle.\n\n![](images/2f/2f4834c4551263c410b8f570eb17195530a8489575c55069fc829da006245006.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110524 1072 #uuid "b04d8179-861a-5dbc-a28c-6d8961dfad43" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110524 1074 "The translation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = (x+2, y-3)\n$$\n\nis a shift by $2$ units to the right and $3$ units downward.\n\nFirst, we must translate each point $2$ unit to the right. To accomplish this, we can translate each vertex $2$ units to the right and then draw in the line segments between the new vertices.\n\n![](images/c7/c78bcb5311159d1d71ca471c2606ae239347b88f21f795c2d7cd915afa00fc71.png)\n\nNext, we must translate each point of the resulting figure $3$ units downward. Again, we translate the vertices and then draw in the line segments between the new vertices. The diagram below shows the resulting triangle.\n\n![](images/22/2284fa7909cabaeb758ca1ec77213d677f564dfdcb78a64a07ebb317178476c7.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110525 1069 "e-7177" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110525 1071 "![](images/3f/3f3ac69cc09fd70f0cbcd67e4b6b64ca1a364be471fda6ccb3a9e42bb991e44c.png)\n\nWhat is the volume of the cube?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110525 1072 #uuid "333792dd-67b0-5673-821a-304ad5011c62" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110525 1074 "The volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV = s^3\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ns=4\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = (4\\, \\textrm{cm})^3 = 64\\, \\textrm{cm}^3\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110526 1069 "e-7178" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110526 1071 "The volume of a cube is $729\\, \\textrm{cm}^3$. Find the length of the edges of the cube." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110526 1072 #uuid "0d03e2bd-c8a2-5a16-a5b2-b51dbf9e408a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110526 1074 "Since the volume of a cube is\n\n$$\nV = s^3\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of an edge, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns = \\sqrt[3]{V} = \\sqrt[3]{729} = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the edges is $9\\,\\textrm{cm}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110527 1069 "e-7566" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110527 1071 "The vertex of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is at the point $(4,2)$. Given that $\\mathcal{D}$ is the parabola's discriminant and $c \\lt 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathcal{D} > 0$\n2. $\\mathcal{D} = 0$\n3. $\\mathcal{D} < 0$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110527 1072 #uuid "2c19b421-eab2-56a7-93ce-a69b3851bbc6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110527 1074 "We know the location of the vertex and that the $y$-intercept lies below the $x$-axis. So, let's sketch our parabola based on this information.\n\n![](images/7e/7e9fcee0aeea3772c429a4651f470c49bd4edd45d24b081eec670d03c6f503c1.png)\n\nSince the vertex $(4,2)$ is above the $x$-axis and not lying on the $x$-axis, the curve has either no roots or two distinct roots. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since $c< 0$, the $y$-intercept is below the $x$-axis. So, the curve must intersect the $x$-axis in at least one place as it traverses from the $y$-intercept below the $x$-axis to the vertex above the $x$-axis. Hence, $\\mathcal{D} \\nless 0$.\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal{D} \\gt 0$ and only statement I is true." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110528 1069 "e-7627" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110528 1071 "The graph of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ (shown below) has discriminant $\\mathcal D$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a > 0$\n2. $\\mathcal D = 0$\n3. $\\mathcal D < 0$\n\n![](images/d8/d81a085a48e50840161d97c20e6a03b315ba1eeeb4da028da73025d3a7c28f8a.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110528 1072 #uuid "e10fb8ee-d6d5-5f69-8b12-004b4354b5f1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110528 1074 "Given the graph of a quadratic function:\n\n- if the graph has $2$ roots, then $\\mathcal{D} > 0$,\n- if the graph has $1$ root, then $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, and\n- if the graph has no roots, then $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFor the given graph of the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c{:}\n$$\n\n- Notice that $a$ is the leading coefficient. Since the parabola opens upward, we have that $a > 0$. Therefore, statement I is true.\n- Also, notice that the parabola intersects the $x$-axis once, so it has $1$ root. Therefore, we must have $\\mathcal{D} = 0$. Hence, statement II is true, while statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110529 1069 "e-7702" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110529 1071 "The point $(-1, 5)$ is reflected across the line $y=x$. What are the coordinates of the resulting point?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110529 1072 #uuid "28209e46-7e3c-588e-b52e-37d6109b6736" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110529 1074 "The reflection across the line $y=x$ is represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\mapsto(y,x)\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(-1,5)$, we have\n\n$$\n(-1,5) \\mapsto (5,-1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(5,-1)$.\n\n![](images/ef/ef9805fb93e38e3d0e49effa62c9eb60cde3fa9658f1699ce801baaf8a71e8c9.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110530 1069 "e-7787" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110530 1071 "![](images/33/33c0c422a873ffb8c95e044b77a979cc96b5cb18953531a7999aa16aa3d6d0d6.png)\n\nFind the area of ​​the shaded region in the figure above." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110530 1072 #uuid "8f972cfa-106b-5c97-9f1e-7d05a9e44a8d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110530 1074 "Note that the figure is formed by $7$ squares with the same dimensions. The area of ​​each square is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_s= s^2 = 3^2 = 9 \\textrm{in}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of ​​the shaded region is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 7 \\cdot A_{s} \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 63in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110531 1069 "e-7789" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110531 1071 "![](images/c2/c2fa6e3b875ba19bfd0ae82affc6c8caa7f9a823150cc0b62c9e5e055c0cf76d.png)\n\nFind the area of the shaded region shown above." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110531 1072 #uuid "0502f526-194d-55d4-92d6-7b6abd94f65e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110531 1074 "To find the area of the shaded region, we calculate the areas of the outer and inner shapes and then subtract the results.\n\nThe outer shape is a triangle with a base of $6\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and a height of $8\\,\\textrm{mm}$. Therefore, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{O} = \\dfrac{6 \\cdot 8}{2} = 24\\,\\textrm{mm}^2\n$$\n\nThe inner shape is a rectangle with sides of length $2\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $3\\,\\textrm{mm}$. Therefore, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{I} = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6\\,\\textrm{mm}^2\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the shaded shape is the difference between the areas of the outer and inner shapes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{O} - A_{I} \\\\\n&= 24 - 6 \\\\\n&= 18mm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110532 1069 "e-7869" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110532 1071 "The points $A(1,1)$ and $B(9,-5)$ are two consecutive vertices of a rectangle $ABCD$. Given that $AB = 4 BC$, what is the area of the rectangle?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110532 1072 #uuid "d335142d-2bb9-55fa-b677-1dbfa17853c4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110532 1074 "Since we are given two consecutive vertices, we find the length of the side $\\overline{AB}$ using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{(x_{B} - x_{A})^{2} + (y_{B} - y_{A})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(9 - 1)^{2} + (-5 - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{64 + 36} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $AB=4BC$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\frac{AB}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= AB \\cdot BC \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110533 1069 "e-7870" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110533 1071 "Given that $P(x) = x^4 - 8$, solve $P(x) \\gt 2x^2$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110533 1072 #uuid "7cb33230-519c-5a6f-9340-fcd214248ba8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110533 1074 "To solve this inequality, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}P(x) & > 2x^{2} \\\\ x^{4} - 8 & > 2x^{2} \\\\ x^{4} - 2x^{2} - 8 & > 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo solve the inequality $P(x) \\gt 2x^2$, we solve the equivalent problem $f(x) \\gt 0$, where\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 2x^2 - 8\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y = f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nWe factor the polynomial $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{4} - 2x^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 2x^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x^{2} - 4) + 2(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2)(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2)(x + 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 2x^2 - 8\n$$\n\nare $x = \\pm{2}$, which are all simple roots.\n\nNext, we graph the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/b6/b65299eb9e1c35485c1af5a1f30c8c6ac14e03e7566a5f562e4e420ccb417a60.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0$, we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (2,\\infty)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110534 1069 "e-7871" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110534 1071 "Given that $f(x) = -x^3 - 5x^2 + 4x + 20$, solve $f(x) > 0$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110534 1072 #uuid "71e4ae8f-130e-5fd3-aee1-0b00998ec655" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110534 1074 "To solve the inequality $f(x) > 0$, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= -x^{3} - 5x^{2} + 4x + 20 \\\\\n&= - [x^{3} + 5x^{2} - 4x - 20] \\\\\n&= - [x^{2}(x + 5) - 4(x + 5)] \\\\\n&= - [(x^{2} - 4)(x + 5)] \\\\\n&= - (x + 2)(x - 2)(x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=-5$, $x=-2$ and $x=2$, which are all simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/fb/fbd429abf6d00e84d79788832949c9244a15acddde5ed98f8a28620d78cb7269.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) > 0$, we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-\\infty, -5) \\cup (-2,2)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110535 1069 "e-7872" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110535 1071 "Let $P(x) =-2 x^3 - 7 x^2 - 2 x + 3$. Given that $P(x)$ has at least one integer root, solve $P(x) \\leq 0$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110535 1072 #uuid "b728352d-37d3-5fb4-940a-12dd8fe7adc9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110535 1074 "To solve the inequality\n\n$$\nP(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x)$. We can do this using the rational roots theorem. According to this theorem, the possible rational roots of $P(x)$ are\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\pm 1, \\pm 3\n$$\n\nWe're given that the polynomial has at least one integer root, and so the list of possible integer roots is\n\n$$\n\\pm 1, \\pm 3\n$$\n\nLet's now test each of the above options until we find a root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(1) &= -2(1)^{3} - 7(1)^{2} - 2(1) + 3 - 8\\cdot \\\\\nP(-1) &= -2(-1)^{3} - 7(-1)^{2} - 2(-1) + 3 = 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $x=-1$ is a root of $P(x)$, and so $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$ by the factor theorem.\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to factor $P(x)$. We get the following:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $-2$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $3$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $-2$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= (x + 1)(-2x^{2} - 5x + 3) \\\\\n&= - (x + 1)(2x^{2} + 5x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods. This gives\n\n$$\nP(x) =-(x+1)(2x-1)(x+3)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $3$ simple roots,\n\n$$\nx=-3,-1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/a8/a831d09dbbe0f567bfe1bc06b79649f026e334ddec857362dcf7747bcce8abf7.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\nP(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwhen the curve $y=P(x)$ is below $x$-axis or touches this axis, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in [-3,-1] \\cup \\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\infty \\right)\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110536 1069 "e-7921" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110536 1071 "Given that $A(1,2)$ and $C(-1,3)$ are the coordinates of two opposite vertices of a square $ABCD$, what is the area of the square?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110536 1072 #uuid "425690f2-643f-5a3c-a6f1-53c388dd0c9b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110536 1074 "We need to calculate the measure of the sides of the square. To do that, we first calculate the measure of the diagonal\n$\\overline{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= \\sqrt{(x_{C} - x_{A})^{2} + (y_{C} - y_{A})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1 - 1)^{2} + (3 - 2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagonal $d$ and the side length $s$ of a square are related by the formula\n\n$$\ns = \\dfrac{d}{\\sqrt 2}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\ns = \\dfrac{AC}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= s^{2} = (\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2} = \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110537 1069 "e-7937" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110537 1071 "Find the equation that generates the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10f082165ef5b901e0ec43139fac54317395e1b349b31dd491dc36980981a622.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110537 1072 #uuid "04070131-1543-5516-a7f1-509ea3f01234" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110537 1074 "To obtain the given graph, we perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Translate the graph to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/35/3561b98dcf8113dcb644b1f3c738821f74d9f453e268fb2d47f6379d41f31951.png)\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x+1}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110538 1069 "e-7938" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110538 1071 "The graph below is a translation of $y=\\sqrt{x}$. Find the equation that generated the graph.\n\n![](images/d7/d7705be833455f266e892f78ec29f0dee88a7e3e803a00f85dfdfd15ff5f6f92.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110538 1072 #uuid "0e964fdb-fa9e-516a-badf-2217a7937bc8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110538 1074 "To obtain the given graph, we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Translate the graph to the left by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+2}$.\n- Translate the result down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+2}-1$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/9a/9af181a909b6ed88cee9ab8d331a09378ed75cc85a33d0f156a5b89ac91796cf.png)\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x+2}-1\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110539 1069 "e-7939" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110539 1071 "Sketch the graph of the function $f(x) =\\sqrt{2-x}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110539 1072 #uuid "4ffc1494-2efd-581c-a58e-d1e9a8a2dfc4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110539 1074 "First, let's re-write the expression as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2-x}=\\sqrt{-(x-2)}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{-(x-2)}\n$$\n\nwe can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Reflect the graph in the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/08/0848b7ecf37824cfb1eabe5b9b100cd3daf545887535e691eea62686bc99d973.png)\n\n- Translate the result to the right by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-(x-2)}$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/22/227210ee73219614d94bff78ddf7e0a8539dabfa2bc3b8f4778fa9292359c5f6.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110540 1069 "e-7940" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110540 1071 "Find the equation that generated the graph below.\n\n![](images/16/16cbb1865575a8f1d968a065a3b9a09eb6fa3b998a24029d75713825cd5b7a2a.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110540 1072 #uuid "bc4d0d1e-4cf6-5e3a-99b8-3613593fd0d3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110540 1074 "To obtain the given graph, we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Reflect the graph in the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x}$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/78/78b19415feaf4e648e0d49a7aa3a130db5a57cf23b4aeafbdc34e5a968a3bf0e.png)\n\n- Translate the result to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x+1}$.\n- Translate the result up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x+1}+2$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4ffbc7f9dd9ec50830a671f4ce688f51f6a7a11663f0c93af567ee8cc9a4a4f4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny = -\\sqrt{x+1}+2\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110541 1069 "e-7959" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110541 1071 "![](images/c9/c9209c8978b86043b5d57d4a357a480a0209914598a0d7f2a810539cc050c473.png)\n\nWhich system of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110541 1072 #uuid "ae48dbff-25c8-55a9-a9e0-fda0b3ecfd50" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110541 1074 "Consider the line $y=2x-3$. Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n$y \\gt 2x - 3$.\n\nNow consider the line\n\n$$\ny = x + 1\n$$\n\nSince the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n$y \\lt x+1$.\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n${y > 2x - 3; y < x + 1$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110542 1069 "e-7960" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110542 1071 "![](images/69/69a4b7d160f9a3990034f7e9170cc20d90eba33bd87397f8436836fdd5520e84.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities represents the shaded region shown in the graph above?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110542 1072 #uuid "4809beec-7c4e-5bd1-989d-1dc54d78d892" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110542 1074 "Consider the line\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{3}x+3\n$$\n\nSince the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny \\leq \\dfrac{1}{3}x+3\n$$\n\nNow consider the line\n\n$$\ny = x+2\n$$\n\nSince the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n$y \\lt x+2$.\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{y \\le \\frac{1}{3}x + 3; y < x + 2\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110543 1069 "e-7964" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110543 1071 "What system of inequalities defines the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/e9/e92ea6fc292a771333cc12b9554b58bd4ba5f358195982c5ac3fd6bd2a9d06ff.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110543 1072 #uuid "88a7aab5-813a-58e2-a695-8ee72dd4efb1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110543 1074 "Consider the line $y=1$. Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n$y < 1$.\n\nNow consider the line $x - y = 2$. Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n$y =x- 2$.\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny \\geq x - 2\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx-y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{y < 1; x - y \\le 2\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110544 1069 "e-7976" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110544 1071 "The point $A$ is located on the base $\\overline{PR}$ of an isosceles triangle $\\triangle PQR$, where $\\angle QAR$ is a right angle. If $AP=24$ and $AR=2x+6$, what is the value of $x$?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110544 1072 #uuid "4fa8aef1-c42a-5092-906a-22ac791b8a3c" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110544 1074 "First, let's draw a sketch using the given information.\n\n![](images/50/50c8744e11960229d98ee0c3011fb9cdf80d1f7688468101871de590e66b48a1.png)\n\nSince $\\angle QAR$ and $\\angle QAP$ form a linear pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠QAR + m∠QAP &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n90^{∘} + m∠QAP &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠QAP &= 90^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\triangle QAR$ and $\\triangle QAP$ are congruent by HL (hypotenuse-leg) since we have the following congruent legs, hypotenuses, and right angles:\n\n- $m\\angle{QAR}=m\\angle{QAP}=90^\\circ$\n- A common leg $\\overline{AQ}$\n- $\\overline{QR}\\cong \\overline{QP}$\n\nAs a consequence, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular, $\\overline{AR} \\cong \\overline{AP}$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAR &= AP \\\\\n2x + 6 &= 24 \\\\\n2x &= 18 \\\\\nx &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110545 1069 "e-8977" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110545 1071 "Six identical plastic cubic containers have a combined volume of $48 \\: \\textrm{ft}^3$. Find the side length of a single container." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110545 1072 #uuid "f265fcf1-8d50-5908-8df4-6df66cb0673a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110545 1074 "First, we find the volume of a single container:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{48 \\: \\textrm{ft}^3}{6} = 8 \\: \\textrm{ft}^3\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the side length of one container.\n\nSince the volume of a cube is\n\n$$\nV = s^3\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns = \\sqrt[3]{V} = \\sqrt[3]{8} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of a side of a single container is $2\\,\\textrm{ft}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110546 1069 "e-8979" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110546 1071 "The side length of a cube is $25 \\, \\textrm{mm}$. What is the length of a face diagonal of the cube?" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110546 1072 #uuid "fd507d07-f305-5b23-9b5f-778c2a9c17c6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110546 1074 "The length of a face diagonal $f$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nf = s\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ns = 25\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\nf = 25\\sqrt{2} \\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110547 1069 "e-8980" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110547 1071 "Given that $BE = 15\\sqrt{2} \\, \\text{cm}$, find the side length of the cube.\n\n![](images/87/878737da1e628f5a1f642cc9daa8d6f580607f79688e57fd2f0727954d04a34e.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110547 1072 #uuid "6d656a01-7f43-5b33-9e3e-2be86c5a97a8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110547 1074 "Notice that $\\overline{BE}$ is a face diagonal of the cube.\n\nSince the length of a face diagonal $f$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nf = s \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of an edge, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns = \\dfrac{f}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} = 15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the edges is $15 \\, \\text{cm}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110548 1069 "e-9741" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110548 1071 "A marble is projected vertically upward with an initial velocity of $15.7 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$. Find the maximum height reached by the marble. Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110548 1072 #uuid "394b67b5-55c6-554d-ace0-c6dc74318f35" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110548 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the marble is $u=15.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Notice that $g$ is *negative* since the marble's motion acts *against* gravity.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n&= 15.7t + \\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)t^{2} \\\\\n&= 15.7t - 4.9t^{2} \\\\\n&=-4.9t^{2} + 15.7t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function $s(t)$ is a downward parabola, and the maximum height corresponds to the vertex of the parabola.\n\n![](images/ac/ac768c8b216dddf0fa9115f2745b83f7bb3baa5deb199e3f6b5e66142c1a9e76.png)\n\nThe $t$-coordinate of the vertex can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nt= -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -4.9$ and $b=15.7$, which gives\n\n$$\nt=-\\dfrac{15.7}{2(-4.9)}=1.602\\,040... \\approx 1.6020\n$$\n\nSo the marble attains its maximum height at $t=1.6020$ seconds. The height of the marble at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= -4.9t^{2} + 15.7t \\\\\n&=-4.9(1.6020)^{2} + 15.7(1.6020) \\\\\n&= 12.576... \\\\\n&\\approx 12.6m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marble reached a maximum height of $12.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ in $1.6\\,\\textrm{s}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110549 1069 "e-9799" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110549 1071 "Among $85$ surveyed cinema visitors,\n\n- $15$ men and $21$ women like crime movies,\n- $12$ women don't like crime movies.\n\nCopy and complete the joint frequency table below, and use this to determine the total number of men surveyed.\n\n|  | Like Crime Movies | Don't Like Crime Movies |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Men |  |  |\n| Women |  |  |" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110549 1072 #uuid "71304371-5733-529a-8da0-de6d6011f4f6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110549 1074 "From the given information, we can fill in part of the table as follows:\n\n|  | Like Crime Movies | Don't Like Crime Movies | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Men | $15$ |  |  |\n| Women | $21$ | $12$ |  |\n| Totals |  |  | $85$ |\n\nFor a given joint frequency table:\n\n- the sum of the joint frequencies in any row/column equals the marginal frequency for that row/column, and\n- the sum of the marginal frequencies in the \"Totals\" row/column equals the total $(85)$.\n\nTherefore, filling in the remaining rows/columns, including the totals, we get the following:\n\n|  | Like Crime Movies | Don't Like Crime Movies | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Men | $15$ | $37$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}52}$ |\n| Women | $21$ | $12$ | $33$ |\n| Totals | $36$ | $49$ | $85$ |\n\nTherefore, $52$ men were surveyed." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110550 1069 "e-9898" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110550 1071 "|  | German | Spanish | French | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Boys | $8$ | $x$ | $14$ | $44$ |\n| Girls | $12$ | $14$ | $y$ | $z$ |\n| Totals | $t$ | $w$ | $29$ | $85$ |\n\nThe frequency table above describes some children's preferences for learning a second language. Determine the value of $w + y + z$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110550 1072 #uuid "bc96c4b3-d42d-532d-b18b-3933067e74d8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110550 1074 "For each row/column in a joint frequency table:\n\n- the sum of the joint frequencies equals the marginal frequency (or total) for that row/column, and\n- the sum of the marginal frequencies in the totals row/column equals the total $(85)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each row/column:\n\n- Looking at the first row, there are $44$ boys in total.\nGermanSpanishFrenchTotalsBoys$8$$x$$14$$44$Girls$12$$14$$y$$z$Totals $t$ $w$ $29$$85$\nTherefore:\n$8 + x + 14|= 44; 22 + x|= 44; x|= 44 - 22; x|= 22$\nWe add this value to our table.\n- Looking at the third column, we see that there are $29$ children who prefer to learn French.\nGermanSpanishFrenchTotalsBoys$8$$22$$14$$44$Girls$12$$14$$y$$z$Totals $t$ $w$ $29$$85$\nTherefore:\n$14 + y|= 29; y|= 29 - 14; y|= 15$\nWe add this value to our table.\n\nBy similar reasoning, we can deduce the following:\n\n- Considering the number of children who prefer to learn German, we get\n$t|= 8 + 12 = 20$.\n- Considering the number of children who prefer to learn Spanish, we get\n$w|= 22 + 14 = 36$.\n- Since there are $85$ children in total, we have\n$44 + z|= 85; z|= 85 - 44; z|= 41$.\n\nOur completed frequency table is as follows:\n\n|  | German | Spanish | French | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Boys | $8$ | $22$ | $14$ | $44$ |\n| Girls | $12$ | $14$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}15}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}41}$ |\n| Totals | $20$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}36}$ | $29$ | $85$ |\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nw + y + z = 36 + 15 + 41 = 92\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110551 1069 "e-9899" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110551 1071 "The favorite activity among swimming, tanning, and playing volleyball for $100$ visitors to a public beach was surveyed. The interviewer notes the following:\n\n- $20$ of the $45$ women chose swimming.\n- $30$ visitors chose volleyball.\n- $23$ men chose swimming.\n- Of the men, 3 times as many selected to play volleyball as those who chose to tan.\n\nUse a joint frequency table to determine how many women prefer volleyball." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110551 1072 #uuid "f228beb9-094a-54d8-a98b-3d87cb174a65" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110551 1074 "From the given information, we can fill in part of the table as follows:\n\n|  | Swimming | Tanning | Volleyball | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Women | $20$ |  |  | $45$ |\n| Men | $23$ | $x$ | $3x$ |  |\n| Totals |  |  | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nNote that we have let $x$ equal the number of men who plan to prefer to tan.\n\nFor a given joint frequency table:\n\n- the sum of the joint frequencies in any row/column equals the marginal frequency for that row/column, and\n- the sum of the marginal frequencies in the \"Totals\" row/column equals the total $(100)$.\n\nFilling in some more rows/columns using this idea, we get the following:\n\n|  | Swimming | Tanning | Volleyball | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Women | $20$ |  |  | $45$ |\n| Men | $23$ | $x$ | $3x$ | $55$ |\n| Totals | $43$ | $27$ | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nFrom the second row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n23 + x + 3x &= 55 \\\\\n4x + 23 &= 55 \\\\\n4x &= 32 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{32}{4} \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing this result and filling in the rest of the table, we finally arrive at the following:\n\n|  | Swimming | Tanning | Volleyball | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Women | $20$ | $19$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}6}$ | $45$ |\n| Men | $23$ | $8$ | $24$ | $55$ |\n| Totals | $43$ | $27$ | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nFinally, we conclude that $6$ women prefer volleyball." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110552 1069 "e-9930" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110552 1071 "Sketch the square $ABCD$ with vertices $A(-2,-5)$, $B(1,-5)$, $C(1,-2)$, $D(-2,-2)$ in the coordinate plane, and calculate the area of the square." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110552 1072 #uuid "6b8586f4-0ae3-52d9-90ad-d4089039e3b2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110552 1074 "Let's plot the square using the given coordinates.\n\n![](images/e6/e627f6aa87d57933b5f6ebdd9a62da5e812de87dace91249120966f2fd0864d3.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of the square, we need to find the length of its sides.\n\nSince this square has horizontal and vertical sides, we can calculate the length of $\\overline{AB}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\mid x_{B} - x_{A} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 1 - (-2) \\mid \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = AB^2 = 3^2 = 9\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110553 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050538 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110553 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111090 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110553 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111091 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110553 1039 "Calculating an Unknown Quantity Using Vertical Angles" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110553 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110442 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110553 1042 #uuid "2f6b7437-6922-58bd-a819-de7fb02a261e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110554 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048927 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110554 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049694 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110554 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049874 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110554 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111036 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110554 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111042 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110554 1039 "Calculating the Perimeter of a Polygon With Horizontal and Vertical Sides Only" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110554 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110443 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110554 1042 #uuid "be5bf3ea-482c-54b8-a920-383157f033af" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110555 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050981 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110555 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111064 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110555 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111322 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110555 1039 "Calculating the Perimeter of a Polygon Using the Distance Formula" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110555 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110444 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110555 1042 #uuid "d66370f1-9a43-51d4-95d4-f58ad9ac2bb7" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110556 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048626 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111323 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111324 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110556 1039 "Finding the Perimeter of a Square Given Two Opposite Coordinates" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110556 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110445 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110556 1042 #uuid "5174d2e4-c973-5ba2-8055-bb4a35575d18" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110557 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049424 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110557 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111048 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110557 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111065 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110557 1039 "Finding the Coordinates of a Vertex of a Polygon Given Its Perimeter" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110557 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110446 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110557 1042 #uuid "8b4e8815-f199-55d3-9ece-b9f377466f8e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186110558 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050981 1