] [17592186111806 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111809 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111806 1061 9626 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111806 1062 #uuid "774c73ad-c621-5081-a1ce-b2d502bdc593" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111806 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110808 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111807 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111808 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111807 1061 9628 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111807 1062 #uuid "5c20a853-e5d4-527d-aa0c-b564f4b62547" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111807 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110809 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111808 1061 9630 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111808 1062 #uuid "74515ebe-941e-5d6d-9a62-6b7d45db318e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111808 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110810 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111809 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111810 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111809 1061 9633 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111809 1062 #uuid "94b6b2dd-b970-5ac2-bfc6-8d48295d967f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111809 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110811 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111810 1061 9635 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111810 1062 #uuid "3ce582a6-f757-5dd4-be64-187cf9e6c038" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111810 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110812 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111811 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111574 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111811 1061 9994 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111811 1062 #uuid "e85207e1-3657-5ed5-aed1-f4604cbff53e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111811 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111862 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111812 1064 "A **median** in a triangle is a line segment joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $M$ is the midpoint of the side $\\overline{AB}$. Therefore, $\\overline{CM}$ is a median of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\n![](images/42/42504b9e361095557f2861cdaf68d2a092584c43a8ca85b5912e000c2204eeb9.png)\n\nAny triangle has exactly three medians, one for each side. All three medians of $\\triangle ABC$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8ae0de5ee93bf5608573a39eb6003229984d99180007f4f02a2598842ed416b.png)\n\nYou'll notice that the medians intersect at a single point. This is true for *all* triangles. We'll discuss this in more detail shortly." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111812 1065 10198 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111812 1066 #uuid "568201a2-1ff0-5267-9ff3-2d483dd6ac19" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111812 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111813 1064 "We're now ready to state and prove the main result of this lesson.\n\n*Theorem:*\n\n> *All circles are similar.*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n> *Consider a circle $\\mathcal{C}_1$ of radius $R_1$ centered at $A(x_1,y_1)$ and a circle $\\mathcal{C}_2$ of radius $R_2$ centered at $B(x_2,y_2)$. Let $\\mathcal{U}$ be the unit circle centered at the origin.*\n> *First, we will show that*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_1 \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.\n> *For the circle $\\mathcal{C}_1$ of radius $R_1$ centered at $A(x_1,y_1)$, applying the translation $(x,y)\\mapsto (x - x_1, y-y_1)$ followed by a dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac{1}{R_1}$ and center $O$ maps $\\mathcal C_1$ onto $\\mathcal U$.*\n> *Next, we'll show that*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_2 \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.\n> *For the circle $\\mathcal{C}_2$ of radius $R_2$ centered at $B(x_2,y_2)$, applying the translation $(x,y)\\mapsto (x - x_2, y-y_2)$ followed by a dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac{1}{R_2}$ and center $O$ maps $\\mathcal C_2$ onto $\\mathcal U$.*\n> *Now, by the symmetric property of similarity,*\n> $\\mathcal{U} \\sim \\mathcal{C}_2$.\n> *Therefore, by the transitive property of similarity,*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_1 \\sim \\mathcal{C}_2$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111813 1065 10201 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111813 1066 #uuid "4fbec2d9-9f5b-534b-9b67-0b47cfc42cd8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111813 1067 "Proof That All Circles Are Similar" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111814 1064 "The centroid has an important property that is often called the **centroid ratio theorem**:\n\n> *The centroid of a triangle splits any median in the proportion $2:1$, starting from the corresponding vertex.*\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $G$ is the centroid, and $\\overline{CK}$ is one of the medians.\n\n![](images/8c/8cc8e92382712aceaa2320dfde9cc38471a8b3c92ea15cdfd1a5280f07e0fb97.png)\n\nAccording to the centroid theorem,\n\n$$\nCG: GK = 2: 1\n$$\n\nThe same proportion holds for the other medians of the triangle. Just remember that the longer partition always contains the vertex.\n\nWe'll prove the centroid ratio theorem in a future lesson." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111814 1065 10203 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111814 1066 #uuid "8e46e3a3-7b87-57dd-88b3-6316be792173" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111814 1067 "The Centroid Ratio Theorem" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111815 1064 "The coordinates of a triangle's centroid can be calculated by taking the average of the vertices' $x$- and $y$-coordinates.\n\nNamely, if $A(x_A,y_A)$, $B(x_B,y_B)$, and $C(x_C,y_C)$ are the coordinates of the vertices of a triangle and $G(x_G,y_G)$ is the centroid, then\n\n$$\nx_{G} = \\frac{x_{A} + x_{B} + x_{C}}{3}, y_{G} = \\frac{y_{A} + y_{B} + y_{C}}{3}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result in a future lesson. But for now, let's see some examples." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111815 1065 10204 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111815 1066 #uuid "ca8e5596-b0fb-53da-942f-6018f4b7f2ff" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111815 1067 "Coordinates of the Centroid" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111816 1064 "So far, we've proved that a circle $\\mathcal C$ is similar to the unit circle $\\mathcal U$ in cases involving just a single similarity transformation.\n\nBefore we move on to more complex cases, we note that similarity in two-dimensional figures possesses the properties of reflexivity, symmetricity, and transitivity.\n\nMore formally, suppose that $\\mathcal F_1, \\mathcal F_2$, and $\\mathcal F_3$ are two-dimensional geometric figures. Then, the following properties hold:\n\n- The **reflexive** property of similarity:\n$\\mathcal F_1 \\sim \\mathcal F_1$\nThis states that every figure is similar to itself.\n- The **symmetric** property of similarity:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: \\mathcal F_1 \\sim \\mathcal F_2, \\textrm{then}\\: \\mathcal F_2 \\sim \\mathcal F_1$\nThis states that if the first figure is similar to the second, the second must be similar to the first.\n- The **transitive** property of similarity:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: F_1 \\sim \\mathcal F_2 \\:\\textrm{and}\\: F_2 \\sim \\mathcal F_3,\\textrm{then}\\: F_1 \\sim \\mathcal F_3$\nThis states that if the first figure is similar to the second, and the second is similar to the third, the first must be similar to the third.\n\nLet's see how these properties are used to prove that a circle is similar to the unit circle in cases where two similarity transformations are needed." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111816 1065 10528 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111816 1066 #uuid "83f79018-0b31-500e-90b8-e3bcda00c423" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111816 1067 "The Reflexive, Symmetric, and Transitive Properties of Similarity" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111817 1064 "![](images/4b/4bd029a874d6e7f3c7a0dd57862ee850be6d5fad4a7bfb57e7ef25800f114df9.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a circle $\\mathcal{C}$ of radius $2$ centered at the origin $O$ and the unit circle $\\mathcal{U}$.\n\n*Claim:*\n\n$$\n\\mathcal C\\sim\\mathcal U\n$$\n\n*Proof:*\n\n> *To prove that $\\mathcal C$ is similar to $\\mathcal U$, we construct a sequence of similarity transformations that map $\\mathcal{C}$ onto $\\mathcal{U}$.*\n> *A dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac12$ and center $O$ maps the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{U}$.*\n> *Therefore, $\\mathcal{C} \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.*\n\nLet's take a look at another example." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111817 1065 10529 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111817 1066 #uuid "6d2fa894-cc33-59fa-bb31-dbf077b79cab" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111817 1067 "Stating the Full Proof" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111818 1064 "To prove that *any* two circles $\\mathcal C_1$ and $\\mathcal C_2$ are similar, we can use the following strategy:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find a similarity transformation that maps $\\mathcal C_1$ onto $\\mathcal U$. This shows that $\\mathcal C_1 \\sim \\mathcal U$.\n- **Step 2:** Find a similarity transformation that maps $\\mathcal C_2$ onto $\\mathcal U$. This shows that $\\mathcal C_2 \\sim \\mathcal U$.\n- **Step 3:** Since similarity is *symmetric*, we can write the similarity statement from step 2 as $\\mathcal U \\sim \\mathcal C_2$.\n- **Step 4:** Since $\\mathcal C_1 \\sim \\mathcal U$ and $\\mathcal U \\sim \\mathcal C_2$, by the transitive property of similarity, we have $\\mathcal C_1 \\sim\\mathcal C_2$, as required.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111818 1065 10530 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111818 1066 #uuid "421f8f97-9506-54f3-be99-8e37e93eb97b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111818 1067 "A Strategy for Proving Circle Similarity" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111819 1064 "Recall that Prim's algorithm finds a minimum spanning tree of a connected, weighted graph $G$. It follows a so-called greedy approach by starting from an arbitrary vertex and expanding the tree by adding the smallest edge that connects a visited vertex to an unvisited vertex.\n\nOne advantage of Prim's algorithm over other spanning-tree algorithms is that it can easily be applied to a distance matrix. The steps of Prim's algorithm applied to a distance matrix are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Begin by marking the column corresponding to the starting vertex.\n- **Step 2:** Cancel out the row corresponding to the starting vertex.\n- **Step 3:** While not all columns are marked, find the smallest distance $s$ among all the marked columns. Identify the corresponding row and column, denoted as $u$ and $v$. Then, mark the column $u$ to indicate that the vertex has been added to the tree. Cancel out the row $u$ corresponding to $s$ to prevent revisiting, and add the edge $v-u$ to the tree.\n- **Step 4:** Repeat this process until all vertices are included in the minimum spanning tree, ensuring that the tree grows by selecting the smallest available edge at each step.\n\nThe pseudocode for Prim's algorithm on a distance matrix is the following:\n\n> `Mark the column corresponding to the starting vertex`\n> `Cancel out the row corresponding to the starting vertex`\n> `while (not all columns are marked):`\n> `Find the smallest distance s over all the marked columns`\n> `Let u and v be the row and column corresponding to s`\n> `Mark the column u corresponding to s`\n> `Cancel out the row u corresponding to s`\n> `Add edge v-u to the tree`\n\nIt's easiest to understand the algorithm by following a concrete example, so let's do that." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111819 1065 10551 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111819 1066 #uuid "6242e2b5-5d12-56eb-bf74-65cd91f84217" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111819 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111820 1064 "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.**\n\nFor example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 5 &= x + 5 \\\\\nx + 5 - x &= x + 5 - x \\\\\n5 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x$. This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3$, into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 5 &= x + 5 \\\\\n3 + 5 &= 3 + 5 \\\\\n8 &= 8.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values\n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111820 1065 11389 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111820 1066 #uuid "edb6ae26-e191-5d7d-9ce8-ad9ee36655f2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111820 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111821 1064 "Consider the equation\n$2qx = 10x$,\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q$?\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b$. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n$2q = 10$.\n\nSolving this for $q$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2q &= 10 \\\\\n\\frac{2q}{2} &= \\frac{10}{2} \\\\\nq &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2qx &= 10x \\\\\n2(5)x &= 10x \\\\\n10x &= 10x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10x &= 10x \\\\\n10x - 10x &= 10x - 10x \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x$.\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111821 1065 11486 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111821 1066 #uuid "0d28dc5e-68de-53d0-a57c-9072a89eccbb" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111821 1067 "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111822 1064 "|  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| $v_1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $8$ | $26$ | $-$ |\n| $v_2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $13$ | $19$ | $3$ |\n| $v_3$ | $8$ | $13$ | $-$ | $11$ | $-$ |\n| $v_4$ | $26$ | $19$ | $11$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $v_5$ | $-$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's use Prim's algorithm to find the minimum spanning tree of the weighted graph G with the distance matrix given above.\n\nWe start from the vertex $v_1$, and proceed as follows:\n\n| **Step 1.** Mark column $v_1$ by placing a \"$1$\" above it, and cancel out row $v_1$. |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$13$$-$$11$$-$$v_4$$26$$19$$11$$-$$-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength |\n| **Step 2.** The smallest unselected value in the marked column is $8$, located at the intersection of row $v_3$ and column $v_1$. So, we mark column $v_3$ by placing a \"$2$\" above it, cancel out row $v_3$, and add edge $v_1-v_3$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$2$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$26$$19$$11$$-$$-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$ |\n| **Step 3.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $11$, located at the intersection of row $v_4$ and column $v_3$. So, we mark column $v_4$, cancel out row $v_4$, and add edge $v_3-v_4$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$2$$3$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$ |\n| **Step 4.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $13$, located at the intersection of row $v_2$ and column $v_3$. So, we mark column $v_2$, cancel out row $v_2$, and add edge $v_3-v_2$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$4$$2$$3$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$13$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$\\color{lightgrey}3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$$v_3 - v_2$$13$ |\n| **Step 5.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $3$, located at the intersection of row $v_5$ and column $v_2$. So, we mark column $v_5$, cancel out row $v_5$, and add edge $v_2-v_5$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$4$$2$$3$$5$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$13$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$\\color{lightgrey}3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$$v_3 - v_2$$13$$v_2 - v_5$$3$ |\n\nEvery vertex is now in our tree (every column has been marked), so the process is completed. The list of edges in our minimum spanning tree is given by the table on the right, and the total weight of the resulting minimum spanning tree is\n\n$$\n8 + 11 + 13 + 3 = 35\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111822 1065 11617 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111822 1066 #uuid "89cbe76e-6489-51ba-b816-3b4ce44c4010" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111822 1067 "A Concrete Example" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111823 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t$?\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form\n\n$$\nax+b = cx+d{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b = cx+d\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d$.\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x$, and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x$, we have\n${\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}$.\nSolving this equation for $s$, we get\n$s|= 8$.\n- Equating the constants, we get\n${\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}$.\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9$, our original equation has infinitely many solutions." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111823 1065 13403 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111823 1066 #uuid "dc5c5f3e-0731-5ab3-8afd-a2cf24228db1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111823 1067 "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters in General Linear Equations" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111824 1064 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b$, then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x = 2x, \\frac{1}{3}x = \\frac{1}{3}x - 2.16x =-2.16x\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b$, we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax &= bx \\\\\nax &= (a)x \\\\\nax &= ax\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax &= ax \\\\\nax - ax &= ax - ax \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0$, is always true, regardless of the value of $x$. Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b$, and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111824 1065 13429 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111824 1066 #uuid "8428afc7-2e9a-55d0-9f13-7a2acdf8fd7d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111824 1067 "Conditions for an Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111825 1064 "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c$, and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax + b &= cx + d \\\\\nax + b &= (a)x + (b) \\\\\nax + b &= ax + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax + b &= ax + b \\\\\nax + b - ax &= ax + b - ax \\\\\nb &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b$, is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111825 1065 13430 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111825 1066 #uuid "7498a4f8-36b5-5d1d-b953-893581bfa9aa" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111825 1067 "Conditions for a General Linear Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111826 1064 "The following data set shows the total time, in hours, that a group of students spent studying for a final exam:\n\n$$\n0, \\quad 1, \\quad 1, \\quad 1, \\quad 2, \\quad 3,\\quad 3, \\quad 3, \\quad 3\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that we can represent this data using a frequency table.\n\n| Number of Hours Studied | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $\\color{blue}1$ |\n| $1$ | $\\color{blue}3$ |\n| $2$ | $\\color{blue}1$ |\n| $3$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nWe interpret the data in this table as follows:\n\n- $\\color{blue}1$ student spent zero hours studying\n- $\\color{blue}3$ students spent one hour studying\n- $\\color{blue}1$ student spent two hours studying\n- $\\color{blue}4$ students spent three hours studying\n\nThis is an example of a **one-way frequency table** because it shows the relationship between a single *variable* (the number of hours studied) and the *frequency* of each value of the variable.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to represent data with two categorical variables." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111826 1065 13543 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111826 1066 #uuid "d9421c62-2441-5d1b-aec3-cb73b414594e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111826 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111827 1064 "![](images/b2/b27906f92121928be444d3b7f616becbcc7a849e1313ef50accd8c8bfd718bfb.png)\n\nIf we know two points on a parabola with *identical* $y$-values, we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking an average of the two $x$-values.\n\nFor example, consider the parabola $y=f(x)$ above. Notice that the values of $f(x)$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=5$ are equal.\n\n$$\nf(-3) = f(5) = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola's axis of symmetry must be equidistant from the lines $x=-3$ and $x=5$. We can find this by taking the average of these two values.\n\nIf the parabola's vertex is $(h,k)$, then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{-3 + 5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h=1$ is the $x$-coordinate of the vertex, and the axis of symmetry of the parabola is $x=1$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111827 1065 13792 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111827 1066 #uuid "23d4f6e0-c0a7-5551-bd88-80b3268a5b2b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111827 1067 "Extending the Formula" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111828 1064 "Just as we can have a system of linear equations, we can also have systems of linear inequalities. For example, the system\n\n$$\ny \\le x + 2; y > - x\n$$\n\nis solved by the points that satisfy the inequalities\n\n$$\ny \\leq x+2\n$$\n\n*and* $y>-x$ simultaneously.\n\nTo find the points that satisfy both inequalities, we first draw the graph of the inequality\n\n$$\ny \\leq x + 2{:}\n$$\n\n![](images/f9/f96de3a7a91d0bfef399dd4c5810b9c7d0388137841dff98cd4fddb954664d3a.png)\n\nNow, on top of the diagram above, we draw the graph of the second inequality, $y > -x{:}$\n\n![](images/ac/ac101ee6e29e6caac9ecad1171a14c59063e48d8a04dd81476a68308f855e517.png)\n\nThe solution to the system is the *intersection* of the two regions (i.e., the part that is shaded twice).\n\n![](images/26/26aac146d9abd3e566c9534e2e11584fb5b02dd4fed06c1b49af545d7a1ba93e.png)\n\nThe important thing to realize is that *any* point in the shaded region above satisfies *both* inequalities." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111828 1065 1473 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111828 1066 #uuid "1e005009-6033-5e6b-b50c-c802bbffe3e8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111828 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111829 1064 "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nto some power $n$, we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{x}{y})^{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x \\cdot x \\cdot x \\cdot x}{y \\cdot y \\cdot y \\cdot y} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{4}}{y^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111829 1065 1484 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111829 1066 #uuid "9aedc7af-544c-5314-a986-2cd6ce64479b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111829 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111830 1064 "Sometimes, we might need to use more than one rule of exponents to simplify an expression. First, let's remind ourselves of all of the rules of exponents.\n\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nAs an example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(u^2 v\\right)^3\n$$\n\nWe start by using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(u^{2}v)^{3} = (u^{2})^{3} \\cdot v^{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u^{2})^{3} \\cdot v^{3} &=  \\\\\nu^{(2 \\cdot 3)} \\cdot v^{3} &=  \\\\\nu^{6} \\cdot v^{3} &=  \\\\\n&= u^{6}v^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111830 1065 1485 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111830 1066 #uuid "13b1c64a-c868-5824-919e-c3a665bb4a96" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111830 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111831 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x$. First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$.\n2. The graph is increasing.\n3. For $x>0$, the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n4. For $x<0$, the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n5. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$, and the range is $y>0$.\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1$. These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111831 1065 1540 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111831 1066 #uuid "1a4d886d-d7b8-5667-b52a-2c2eb4a35b91" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111831 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111832 1064 "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve $y=2(3^x)$ represents a vertical scaling of $y=3^x$ by a factor of $2$. It takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=3^x$ and multiplies them by $2$. This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/49/49c2136298e6fbf1adcc4c24ddef6d3daf47367d4d3f15dc18c476dff086087a.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111832 1065 1541 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111832 1066 #uuid "8c1fa510-e5f3-585b-852a-c46724144ec1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111832 1067 "Vertical Stretching" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111833 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=0.5^x$. First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y = 0.5^x$ | $8$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ | $0.125$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/b1/b17b1e0f554b8da98f46b89265cbec3be2fab1a40b6c04ef03f5b4cec94ac3d8.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$.\n2. The graph is decreasing.\n3. For $x<0$, the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ decreases. Symbolically, we can write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n4. For $x>0$, the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ increases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has a horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Symbolically, we can write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n5. The domain is $x\\in\\left(-\\infty, \\infty\\right)$, and the range is $y>0$.\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $0 < a < 1$. These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/6c/6c5e94fdb76c1af54dcd1cd8d44bb532ab5c69a305465282b62c28ef2b54ab75.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111833 1065 1544 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111833 1066 #uuid "6e919c9a-e896-5b0b-8570-5a7a377146d3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111833 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111834 1064 "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve\n\n$$\ny=2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nby a factor of $2$.\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nand multiplies them by $2$. This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/36/3654463dd3914bb65ffe58be20cdf2bc4cd19e5a51ac479f4aaca50e2709a214.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\left(2^x\\right)\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n$y = 2^x$\nby a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y = 2^x$ and multiplies them by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nThis has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111834 1065 1545 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111834 1066 #uuid "4ea28190-d93f-521e-964b-cdd4aad84f69" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111834 1067 "Vertical Stretching and Compression" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111835 1064 "The **zero product rule** says that if we have the equation\n\n$$\na\\cdot b = 0\n$$\n\nthen $a=0$ or $b=0$.\n\nIn other words, if we know that the product of two numbers is zero, then at least one of them must be zero because any number multiplied by zero is zero.\n\nThe zero product rule is useful for solving quadratic equations. For example, let's take a look at the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(x-1)}{\\color{blue}(x+2)} = 0\n$$\n\nWhenever a quadratic equation is factored on the left-hand side and has zero on the right-hand side, we can apply the zero product rule to solve the equation. So for our equation, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x-1=0} \\qquad\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\qquad {\\color{blue}x+2 =0}\n$$\n\nWe solve each of the equations separately, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x - 1 &= 0 & x + 2|= 0 \\\\ x|= 1 & x|=-2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = -2,1$.\n\nAs usual, we can check our solutions by substituting them back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x &= -2 & x|= 1 \\\\ ⇓ & ⇓ \\\\ (x - 1)(x + 2)|= 0 & (x - 1)(x + 2)|= 0 \\\\ (-2 - 1)(-2 + 2)|= 0 & (1 - 1)(1 + 2)|= 0 \\\\ (-3)(-0)|= 0 & (0)(3)|= 0 \\\\ 0|= 0✓ & 0|= 0✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111835 1065 1622 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111835 1066 #uuid "cb8e0dec-43f0-5f0f-b5c5-ebc8a0f66894" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111835 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111836 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-3x+1=0\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to factor the trinomial on the left-hand side. We'll need to find two numbers such that\n\n- their product is equal to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2 \\times 1 = 2$), and\n- their sum is equal to the linear coefficient (which is $-3$).\n\nThese two numbers are $-1$ and $-2$. So, we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 3x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2}- 1x - 2x_(⏟)_(-3x) + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(2x^{2} - x) + (-2x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(2x - 1) - 1(2x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx-1=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 2x-1=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=1 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2},1\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111836 1065 1626 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111836 1066 #uuid "599a9fd9-96cf-5f1d-adbc-730bf3f121e4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111836 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111837 1064 "Remember that for a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\ntells us a lot about the number of solutions:\n\n- if $\\mathcal{D}>0$, then we get $2$ distinct real solutions,\n- if $\\mathcal{D}=0$, then we get $1$ distinct real solution, and\n- if $\\mathcal{D}<0$, then we get no real solutions.\n\nIn the same way, the number of roots of a quadratic function\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\ntells us a lot about the discriminant:\n\n- if the graph has $2$ roots, then $\\mathcal{D}>0$,\n- if the graph has $1$ root, then $\\mathcal{D}=0$, and\n- if the graph has no roots, then $\\mathcal{D}<0$.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the graph of the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny=-x^2-x+1\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/93/9348e9472f8080c572caf81c07da53892d5398ca69e6263595e27d85e56bc803.png)\n\nIn the graph, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis twice, so it has $2$ roots. Therefore, we must have $\\mathcal{D}>0$. Indeed, if we calculate $\\mathcal D$ explicitly, we get a positive result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-1)^{2} - 4 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1 - (-4) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general relation between the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of a quadratic function\n\n$$\ny= ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nand the function's graph is sketched below. Remember that the leading coefficient $a$ determines which way the parabola opens: upward if $a>0$, and downward if $a<0$.\n\n![](images/22/22f69c3bd70a732e7717d801a2e2d79eae685b6cd1ceb8c5c45788339859fc7b.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111837 1065 1645 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111837 1066 #uuid "164b5b1f-d864-5a92-9604-497b6e123cec" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111837 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111838 1064 "Suppose we toss a ball vertically upwards with initial velocity\n\n$$\nu=24.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\nas shown below. How do we determine the ball's height after $1$ second?\n\n![](images/95/95a4afeb9f5c40894d10cff245518db69b5508ccbf82789f1e3878658a5c47c5.png)\n\nThe displacement of an object after being projected vertically upward can be modeled using the equation\n\n$$\ns=ut + \\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nLet's recall what each variable represents:\n\n- $s$ is the displacement from the starting position, measured in meters.\n- $u$ is the initial velocity, measured in meters per second.\n- $t$ is time, measured in seconds.\n- $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\n**Important**: In this situation, we take the constant $g$ to be *negative* because the ball moves *against* gravity. In other words, the ball travels upward, yet gravity is trying to pull the ball back down to Earth.\n\nTo determine the height of the ball after one second, we substitute\n\n$$\nu = 24.5, t=1\n$$\n\nand $g=-9.8$ into our equation for $s$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n&= (24.5)(1) + \\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)(1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 24.5 - 4.9 \\\\\n&= 19.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after one second, the ball is $19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ above the ground." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111838 1065 1659 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111838 1066 #uuid "60e39d90-dcb1-51b4-8647-168b3fe46727" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111838 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111839 1064 "The curve\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{3}(3^x)\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of $y=3^x$ by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=3^x$ and multiplies them by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThis has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/e3/e3ae2f177dd60050dd4c4035c66ebfc46d1028eed74f1d23178ddaae35d35378.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111839 1065 1858 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111839 1066 #uuid "78c17e05-8ef3-58fd-a9ea-a2fc04f4bd83" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111839 1067 "Vertical Compression" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111840 1064 "When two line segments have the same length, they are **congruent** segments. To indicate that the line segments $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ are congruent we write\n$\\overline{AB} \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{\\cong}}\\:\\: \\overline{CD}$,\nand we use special marks like in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7fd26fce7dce67fa57769db3e96be503051be5c7d2c7714ed48927547d5165.png)\n\nWe can also write\n\n$$\nAB \\:{\\color{blue}=}\\: CD\n$$\n\nwhich means that the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ is equal to the measure of $\\overline{CD}$.\n\n**Watch out!** The equation\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} = \\overline{CD}\n$$\n\nis only true when both $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ correspond to the same exact segment. When the segments are distinct, we should use the symbol $\\cong$ to represent that the two segments are separate but that they have the same length." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111840 1065 2164 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111840 1066 #uuid "a565176c-749f-56b7-aa7c-4d200187c50f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111840 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111841 1064 "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111841 1065 2165 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111841 1066 #uuid "9e476e67-9dd4-5098-be96-fc805e9e97f9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111841 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111842 1064 "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle.\n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PST &= m∠PSR_(⏟)_(orange angle) + m∠RST_(⏟)_(blue angle) \\\\\n&= 95^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111842 1065 2177 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111842 1066 #uuid "2f387075-8fbb-5e3e-9370-8b93f5e8523e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111842 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111843 1064 "Some angles have special names.\n\n- A **right angle** has a measure of $90^\\circ$. It corresponds to one-quarter of a full turn.![](images/15/155a1d6d2abd868b05674dec47ef4b20a5147d30cd328a5ba53d00dea271d65c.png)\n- A **straight angle**, which is just a straight line, has a measure of $180^\\circ$. It corresponds to one-half of a full turn.![](images/24/2423950f80de8354aee74c4f1daa6e42cf376caa0365307c76ebc7e09f96c8cf.png)\n\n- A **null angle** has a measure of $0^\\circ$. You can imagine \"closing\" the angle until the two sides of the angle overlap. In this case, we don't have any turn, so its measure is zero!![](images/f8/f86b7c1b7ba758fb6b07b6aeabd03395d29103b45e4cd220444b675f70a6e630.png)\n- A **full angle** has a measure of $360^\\circ$. You can imagine \"opening\" the angle until one of the sides goes all the way around. So, this is the angle between a ray and itself after it has made one full turn.![](images/cb/cbc9e288f4d3905357539a221954328ef0ac799531fab62f034364d32a7c0f29.png)\n\nOn a diagram, null and full angles can look the same, so it is up to us what we choose to call it." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111843 1065 2178 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111843 1066 #uuid "74e7b6f4-d873-55dc-81f9-6bf4148828b1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111843 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111844 1064 "When two lines meet at a point, they create two pairs of opposite angles. The angles in each pair are called **vertical angles**.\n\nVertical angles are *always* congruent.\n\n![](images/0d/0d5b2895ae18beb7242ef666af08e5b8e3490880de99aec8d993b02f31a9fcf4.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 3$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent.\n\nLikewise, $\\angle 2$ and $\\angle 4$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent.\n\nSo, we have $\\angle 1 \\cong \\angle 3$ and $\\angle 2 \\cong \\angle 4$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111844 1065 2179 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111844 1066 #uuid "d4d9e056-1e55-54e4-a5e4-9aee258c4fb0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111844 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111845 1064 "We categorize angles that are not right, straight, or full into three groups.\n\n- An **acute angle** has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$:![](images/ec/ec35031af65d0ea9e08121d3df02ed136c5b81b2f90698f4a51681657e67de48.png)\n- An **obtuse angle** has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$:![](images/a9/a96bdfed918f56e7f5cc1cfe9eaad42dcd32a54e663c78fead08e68e6ef80865.png)\n\n- Angles whose measure is between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$ are called **reflex angles**.![](images/62/62323a85f8b3357628c2d221f0293c94ec4c3d9e6e8d1ca65400d2ea7349d3ce.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111845 1065 2180 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111845 1066 #uuid "e1cb4bf7-16f7-5ca9-9702-5ecd2112e696" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111845 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111846 1064 "When solving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:\n\n- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\\circ$,\n- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$,\n- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\\circ$,\n- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$,\n- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\\circ$,\n- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$, and\n- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\\circ$.\n\nIt is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111846 1065 2182 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111846 1066 #uuid "282e8601-d3f4-5c34-a96b-a071a96de244" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111846 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111847 1064 "We know that when a transversal crosses two parallel lines, a set of $8$ interesting angles are formed, some of which are congruent and others of which are supplementary.\n\n![](images/c9/c97f50b95a07ee2a0c4e45056cf70dff89daad38d5b6e3465b8b08738e2b4be9.png)\n\nThe reverse is also true: when certain angles formed by a transversal are congruent or supplementary, then the two lines that the transversal crosses must be parallel.\n\nMore precisely, two lines are parallel if they are crossed by a transversal such that:\n\n- the **corresponding angles are congruent**:\n\n![](images/40/401774d798eeb40a2d73afab6f03c3863fd55dd9c9cd694bf622f8fc9cc72f2b.png)\n\n- or, the **alternate (interior or exterior) angles are congruent**\n\n![](images/c5/c52363f3967c61f20b148bbca9f545b2d95332b470fc3afac15d58bd2571b38a.png)\n\n- or, the **consecutive (interior or exterior) angles are supplementary**.\n\n![](images/ca/ca6561632b9a08c9ea87f2d51786ef3539dac40d089621631f5ce8f7b0556cd6.png)\n\n![](images/13/1309b5bea4f93b87c2747ebd3a1b721fbaa5a6df2999c1a06936164574936ef4.png)\n\nIf at least one pair of angles satisfies the conditions above, then the two lines are parallel.\n\n**Watch out:** When two lines are parallel, we also know that the vertical angles are congruent and the adjacent are supplementary. These, however, are true conditions for any pair of crossing lines, and therefore cannot be used as criteria for parallel lines." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111847 1065 2187 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111847 1066 #uuid "66692214-8975-5e4e-a10b-12fb98aae672" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111847 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111848 1064 "An angle **bisector** is a line that divides an angle into two equal parts.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of the angle $\\angle BAD$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf65e915f4fc26b8d87199f46ea0bc514195d857890983d31aa2d73eb38a811.png)\n\nThe resulting angles $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are congruent (they have the same measure)." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111848 1065 2190 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111848 1066 #uuid "525bd6d2-3c4c-53fb-aaa5-dab5eee2cf13" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111848 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111849 1064 "Consider the following triangle, where the side $\\overline{AC}$ has been extended beyond the vertex $C$.\n\n![](images/6b/6be676cc487d17815701f3783e9eca71d7491a0e63d7ae9016fb65ad801e61d6.png)\n\nThe resulting angle $\\angle{BCD}$ is called the **exterior angle** at vertex $C$.\n\nExterior angles have the following properties:\n\n- An exterior angle and its corresponding interior angle form a linear pair and are therefore supplementary. Here, we have\n$\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{BCD}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{ACB}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} = 180^\\circ$\n- The measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. This is called the **exterior angles theorem**. To see why it is true, notice that since the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n$m∠A + m∠B + m∠C|= 180^{∘}; m∠A + m∠C|= 180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math])$.\n- At each vertex of a triangle, there are two exterior angles. They are vertical angles, and consequently they have the same measure.![](images/b4/b459ad1edd5e0fdf512d722eb55ab20ff66c281d7160077d12936a6926529aa0.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111849 1065 2223 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111849 1066 #uuid "d29b69d0-268e-57ed-9c22-289173b3c810" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111849 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111850 1064 "Yet another important fact about exterior angles is:\n\n> The sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a triangle is always equal to $360^\\circ$.\n\nSo, for the triangle below, we have\n\n$$\nx+y+z = 360^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/22/22586791e1118a38a554deffaec3ac5b73eafc47eee4be2052c0949dde584cea.png)\n\nTo see why, first recall that the measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= m∠B + m∠C \\\\\ny &= m∠A + m∠C \\\\\nz &= m∠A + m∠B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + z &= (m∠B + m∠C) + (m∠A + m∠C) + (m∠A + m∠B) \\\\\n&= 2m∠A + 2m∠B + 2m∠C \\\\\n&= 2(m∠A + m∠B + m∠C) \\\\\n&= 2(180^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111850 1065 2226 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111850 1066 #uuid "3f605c56-a279-51cc-be43-72f1b9030f58" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111850 1067 "The Sum of Exterior Angles" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111851 1064 "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly $1$ pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other.\nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png) Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has $2$ pairs of opposite congruent sides or $2$ pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png) A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111851 1065 2262 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111851 1066 #uuid "06a11fa2-5911-52f5-9a6d-1028e49b44dd" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111851 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111852 1064 "Let's consider the trapezoid $ABCD$, shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0288582ac48824cc6297b24a7a20680f12f2a27533dd5fc65f03f849c0f1915.png)\n\nNotice that the angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle D$ are on the same leg. Two angles of a trapezoid that are on the same leg are called **adjacent angles.** Adjacent angles of a trapezoid are always supplementary.\n\nNow, since $\\angle A$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary, we can solve for $\\angle D$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n65^{∘} + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠D &= 115^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo understand *why* $\\angle A$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary, notice that we have two parallel lines $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ intersected by the transversal $\\overline{AD}$. This means that the angles $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{D}$ are consecutive interior angles, and we know that when a transversal intersects two parallel lines, the consecutive interior angles are supplementary.\n\nFinally, notice that we can make the same conclusion about $\\angle B$ and $\\angle C$. So in this trapezoid, we have two pairs of supplementary angles:\n\n$$\nm∠A + m∠D = 180^{∘}, m∠B + m∠C = 180^{∘}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111852 1065 2263 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111852 1066 #uuid "cadeac0a-14d4-5e59-9961-8e799d9612e9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111852 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111853 1064 "The length of the largest base of a trapezoid is the sum of the smaller base and the projections of the legs onto the largest base.\n\n![](images/91/9112c92f31b6d6220153c8b65b2524afc7c84ed5a8f41e325ded2315bb9ceb29.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, we note the following:\n\n- The smaller base is $\\overline{CD}$.\n- The projection of the leg $\\overline{AD}$ onto the largest base is $\\overline{AH}$.\n- The projection of the leg $\\overline{BC}$ onto the largest base is $\\overline{BF}$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nAB = AH + CD + BF\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111853 1065 2266 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111853 1066 #uuid "cdc6df3b-3d0f-57ae-827a-a468cf822143" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111853 1067 "The Relationship Between the Bases and Legs of a Trapezoid" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111854 1064 "A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right. It has all the properties of parallelograms: its opposite sides are parallel and congruent.\n\n![](images/1f/1fa96b5993a48483798ff4ac05d75f1ffa271f82e5ed6e61a452eb97b3daf3e4.png)\n\nIf all $4$ sides of a rectangle are congruent, then it is a **square**.\n\n![](images/82/822788813f0e81eebef50f324e1bcaf329e7fa5552e10557a19d20b4e868063e.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111854 1065 2270 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111854 1066 #uuid "1fb26940-464f-55d2-9903-b4695c595b5f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111854 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111855 1064 "In geometry, a **translation** means moving an object to the left, right, upwards, or downwards without rotating, resizing, or anything else.\n\nLet's take a look at the two triangles $\\mathcal T$ and $\\mathcal T'$ shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ac6f34b6f24cdc44cc796ef2f6a6ee33db96550a95de549ce05046f6f1228e8.png)\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- The original shape, $\\mathcal T$, is called the **preimage**. The shape $\\mathcal T'$, the result of the translation, is called the **image**. In our case, the preimage $\\mathcal T$ has been translated $4$ units to the right and $2$ units upward.\n- It's important to realize that *every point* on $\\mathcal T$ is translated to a point on $\\mathcal T'$. The diagram shows the triangle's vertices being translated, but the same translation applies to every point on each side of the triangle as well.\n- A translation is a type of **transformation**. There are other types of transformations that we'll learn more about later." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111855 1065 2280 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111855 1066 #uuid "31321075-6c9a-5caf-b878-b64161cc9264" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111855 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111856 1064 "A **reflection** can be thought of as \"mirroring\" or \"flipping\" an object over a **line of reflection.**\n\nWhen a point is reflected in the $x$-axis, the $x$-coordinate remains the same, but the $y$-coordinate is transformed into its opposite (i.e., its sign is changed). The following function can represent this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (x,-y)\n$$\n\nFor example, reflecting the point $P(3,2)$ in the $x$-axis gives the point $Q(3,-2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7fa6735e88a237c48f9bd5d414c66d81b7e204ed106bdea49bc7a315a5b43766.png)\n\nOn the other hand, when a point is reflected in the $y$-axis, the $y$-coordinate remains the same, but the $x$-coordinate is transformed into its opposite (its sign is changed). The following function can represent this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-x,y)\n$$\n\nFor example, reflecting the point $P(3,2)$ in the $y$-axis gives the point $R(-3,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3eeaf93b2020e5d4b46cf6dccece7128f6fd37ec355dbd63229285aa9126c6b4.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111856 1065 2291 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111856 1066 #uuid "ad9c9e68-604f-5dfa-9458-495bad92500c" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111856 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111857 1064 "Two polygons are **congruent** if they have the same shape. This means that\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nConsider the two polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below. Are they congruent?\n\n![](images/b0/b0a11815bf9006ccdca0ebe71ff7ba53ac7526c71a715ece71914b103b4a24a8.png)\n\nLet's check these criteria for our polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$. We can see that both polygons have $3$ sides, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AB^(―) & ≅XY^(―), & AC^(―) & ≅XZ^(―), & BC^(―) & ≅YZ^(―), \\\\ ∠X & ≅∠A, & ∠Y & ≅∠B, & ∠Z & ≅∠C\\end{bmatrix}$.\nTherefore, the polygons are congruent, and we can write\n$ABC \\cong XYZ$.\n\nThe order in which we write the symbols is important: the corresponding vertices have to be in the same order.\n\nWe can also write $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111857 1065 2293 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111857 1066 #uuid "e6be6893-a7ca-578a-9510-8a8c2166e89b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111857 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111858 1064 "The **side-angle-side (SAS) congruence criterion** can be used as a shortcut to prove that two triangles are congruent. It states the following.\n\n> Two triangles are congruent if and only if **two sides and the included angle** of one triangle are congruent to two sides and the included angle of the other triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the triangles below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c1b8e0487771a05854724aab6ab0b6dcbc0fbe7ecabc32bcf0fa72e51ae172b.png)\n\nWe can see that two sides of $\\triangle ABC$ are congruent to two sides of $\\triangle KLM$:\n\n$\\overline{AB}\\cong \\overline{KL}, \\quad \\overline{BC}\\cong \\overline{LM}$.\nAlso, we see that the included angles are congruent:\n$\\angle{B}\\cong \\angle{L}$.\n\nTherefore, the SAS congruence criterion guarantees that these two triangles are congruent, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle KLM\n$$\n\n**Caution:** When using the SAS criterion, it's vital to make sure that the angles you compare are the *included* angles between the two pairs of congruent sides. The SAS criterion only works if the included angles are congruent." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111858 1065 2296 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111858 1066 #uuid "354daef7-0b10-5bb7-8bcb-07ef92deeb11" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111858 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111859 1064 "Recall that the sides of a right triangle have special names.\n\n- The longest side of a right triangle is called the hypotenuse. In the triangle below, the hypotenuse is $\\overline{AB}$.\n- The two other sides are called the legs. In the triangle below, the legs are $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{BC}$.\n\n![](images/9c/9c53fed46a71df4e1271b81230a8c11e09d839fb627a3af0c20abfea612eee4b.png)\n\nFor right triangles, it is straightforward to prove congruence. The **Hypotenuse-Leg (HL)** criterion states the following:\n\n> Two right triangles are congruent if and only if they have congruent hypotenuses and a pair of congruent legs.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the right triangles below.\n\n![](images/38/382dde5533a7b8f1cf35d462af7d0286c960493b07b93b54dae5a1e9b2664116.png)\n\nAs we can see, the right triangles have congruent hypotenuses,\n\n$$\n\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{KM}\n$$\n\nand a pair of congruent legs,\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{KL}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the HL congruence criterion guarantees that these two triangles are congruent:\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle KLM\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111859 1065 2299 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111859 1066 #uuid "d42ede8e-ba32-5a8b-9830-8f3949d290e1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111859 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111860 1064 "A **composite polygon** is a polygon that is made up of other polygons, such as triangles, parallelograms, rectangles, squares, and others.\n\nFor example, the polygon below is composite. How can we find its area?\n\n![](images/b6/b695538eb3d7d931191f7bd678bf9313aff7db0741ce23606449c81b978ffdc1.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the polygon consists of four squares and one rectangle in the middle.\n\n![](images/f9/f986a728f6f49367ab20d0b00d55a66044c54a6953b170690919a088d3e400bf.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of the composite polygon above, we add the areas of the four squares and the rectangle:\n\n$$\nA = 2A_{1} + 2A_{2} + A_{3}\n$$\n\nThe areas of the squares and the rectangle are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= 3^{2} = 9, \\\\\nA_{2} &= 2^{2} = 4, \\\\\nA_{3} &= 3 \\cdot 2 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the area of the entire figure is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 2 \\cdot 9 + 2 \\cdot 4 + 6 \\\\\n&= 18 + 8 + 6 \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111860 1065 2309 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111860 1066 #uuid "ad3f66b2-2ac9-5028-ab4d-99372188019a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111860 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111861 1064 "In the picture below, we have a rectangle with a smaller rectangular hole. How can we find the area of the shaded region?\n\n![](images/91/9152f36db482504e76edb386be9bd36bccc10bcbdf5a8b09df2cb34dc2c51013.png)\n\nFirst, we must work out the areas of the outer and inner rectangles. Then, we subtract the area of the inner rectangle from the area of the outer rectangle.\n\nThe outer rectangle has sides of length $7\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $5\\,\\textrm{cm}$. So, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{O} = 7 \\cdot 5 =35\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nThe inner rectangle has sides of length $4\\,\\textrm{in}$ and $3\\,\\textrm{in}$. So, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{I} = 4 \\cdot 3 =12\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the shaded region is the difference between the areas of the outer and inner rectangles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{O} - A_{I} \\\\\n&= 35 - 12 \\\\\n&= 23cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111861 1065 2310 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111861 1066 #uuid "dda9b77d-83ae-53b4-a300-1afb7db828f1" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111861 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111862 1064 "Consider the transformation that translates the point $Q(1,2)$ by $4$ units to the right and $2$ units up, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9eae75a222d1cd9eb29b091772d25326fd0957ee4b894b5322a7faaa25048f59.png)\n\nWe can express this transformation by writing it as a function, as follows:\n\n$$\nT(x,y) = (x+4,y+2)\n$$\n\nThe function above takes a point as its input and gives another point as output. Let's check it with our point $Q(1,2){:}$\n\n$$\nT(1, 2) = (1 + 4, 2 + 2) = (5, 4)✓\n$$\n\nAny translation of $a$ units to the right and $b$ units up can be written using any of the following notations:\n\n$$\n(x, y) ↦ (x + a, y + b); T(x, y) = (x + a, y + b)\n$$\n\nIf $a$ is negative, then the function translates the point $(x,y)$ by $\\mid a \\mid$ units to the left. If $b$ is negative, then the function translates the point $(x,y)$ by $\\mid b \\mid$ units downwards." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111862 1065 2345 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111862 1066 #uuid "e6b0c1a7-c210-58ff-8dd9-88f132915739" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111862 1067 "Translating Objects Using Functions" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111863 1064 "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M$, we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{3 + 7}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{2} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5$.\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111863 1065 2347 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111863 1066 #uuid "7b14d3c0-0c84-55bc-be21-3359fbc5f6ec" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111863 1067 "The Midpoint Formula" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111864 1064 "The diagonals of a rectangle are equal and bisect each other. Consequently, they divide a rectangle into four isosceles triangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f0aafdf04bb7f967122fc8f90454b1b09787e375857bac3bcf5fdf80925e32c.png)\n\nIn a square, the diagonals also bisect the angles.\n\n![](images/cd/cd61fabe993fa6cde5ea4020a659e86b3543597d70a304b3e5ada3abfa426186.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111864 1065 2357 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111864 1066 #uuid "4643230c-067e-5817-af66-88717e7b8c66" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111864 1067 "The Diagonals of Rectangles and Squares" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111865 1064 "Suppose we have a rectangle with vertices $A(0,0)$, $B(3,0)$, $C(3,2)$, and $D(0,2)$, as shown below. How can we calculate its area?\n\n![](images/3e/3e10ebebd7f317a5000d5cbdb96864c1d3fcf5161c537fa015db7ce42b1959fe.png)\n\nIf we find the lengths of the sides of the rectangle, then the area $\\mathcal A$ of a rectangle is simply the product of the sides.\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = AB\\cdot BC\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{AB}$ is horizontal, the length of this segment is simply the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB = \\begin{vmatrix}x_B - x_A & =\\end{vmatrix}3-0 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, since $\\overline{AD}$ is vertical, the length of this segment is simply the absolute value of the difference between the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\nAD = \\begin{vmatrix}y_D - y_A & = & 2-0\\end{vmatrix} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 3\\cdot 2 = 6\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at a similar example with a square." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111865 1065 2396 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111865 1066 #uuid "e39a2a5e-c72b-534e-aa2d-b05014eb8a9e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111865 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111866 1064 "Let's recall the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x = 1\n$$\n\nIf we divide both sides of this identity by ${\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}$, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos^2 x} {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} + \\dfrac {\\sin^2 x}{\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} = \\dfrac 1 {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n1+ \\left(\\dfrac {\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac 1 {\\cos x}\\right)^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}, \\qquad \\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identities into $(\\ast)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + (\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x})^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{\\cos x})^{2} \\\\\n1 + (\\tan x)^{2} &= (\\sec x)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nThe final identity is called the **secant-tangent identity**. We often use it to simplify trigonometric expressions containing both tangent and secant functions." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111866 1065 2405 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111866 1066 #uuid "315779f1-366c-5754-973b-7bd8026aff6a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111866 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111867 1064 "We can use our knowledge of calculating distances in the coordinate plane to find the perimeter of polygons.\n\nFor example, consider $\\triangle ABC$ below with vertices $A(6,4)$, $B(3,4)$, $C(3,8)$.\n\n![](images/be/befdb725ae46113070852f526e60589b50b6333e8388a88ae5679dc0bbcbe9ed.png)\n\nSince $\\overline{AB}$ is horizontal, we can calculate the length of this segment by computing the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\mid x_{B} - x_{A} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 6 - 3 \\mid \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, since $\\overline{BC}$ is vertical, we can calculate the length of this segment by computing the absolute value of the difference between the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\mid y_{C} - y_{B} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 8 - 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the length of $\\overline{AC}$, we use the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= \\sqrt{(x_{C} - x_{A})^{2} + (y_{C} - y_{A})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(3 - 6)^{2} + (8 - 4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 16} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + AC + BC \\\\\n&= 3 + 5 + 4 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111867 1065 2416 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111867 1066 #uuid "9697057c-c123-567b-95da-705dcd567da5" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111867 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111868 1064 "Any two circles are similar because any circle can be mapped to any other circle using a combination of similarity transformations.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the circles $\\mathcal C$ and $\\mathcal C'$ centered at $O$ and with radii $2$ and $4$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2e/2e4bcff4d12082a68626853544ad9bbef30e465d2c65672e32bbf81d1bb82511.png)\n\nWe can double the radius of $\\mathcal{C}$ so that it has the same radius as $\\mathcal{C}'$. To do this, we use a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac24a8a3c795ebb8f197083b07a9a4e5c0a4cab0bfbfe9510783273799be129.png)\n\nSince $\\mathcal C$ is mapped to $\\mathcal C'$ under this transformation, the two circles are similar." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111868 1065 2420 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111868 1066 #uuid "6e92c083-9256-5a4a-85ca-b057296efe55" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111868 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111869 1064 "Suppose that we have an angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane positioned in the usual way. Its **reference angle**, denoted $\\theta_R$, is the smallest positive acute angle formed with the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles\n\n$$\n\\theta=45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $315^\\circ$. These angles lie in the first, second, third, and fourth quadrants, respectively. And each of these angles has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfba74ff2c6a69b3db1c9478f5caac4c31578ed93b47302a74d843b04fc6ee85.png)\n\n**Note:** Any reference angle always lies between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$ inclusively:\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta_R \\leq 90^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111869 1065 2492 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111869 1066 #uuid "2338474e-8937-5e5e-88ae-7e7848bf2d6f" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111869 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111870 1064 "The **cotangent** is the reciprocal of the tangent:\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\n$$\n\nTo plot the graph of $y=\\cot x$, with $x$ in radians, we create a table with some known values:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 2$ | $\\dfrac{2\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ | $\\pi$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\tan x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $0$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $-1$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\text{undef.}$ |\n\nPlotting these points gives the following graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e9942a4d7e3da2a9da4a2e5be0cb00557562fe73685eb345995bc167eb570a49.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- a zero on the graph of $y=\\tan x$ corresponds to a vertical asymptote on the graph of $y=\\cot x$, and\n- a vertical asymptote on the graph of $y=\\tan x$ corresponds to a zero on the graph of $y=\\cot x$.\n\nThe cotangent inherits its periodicity from the tangent, so the period of the cotangent is $\\pi$. Using this periodicity property, we can extend our plot of the cotangent function:\n\n![](images/ee/ee663ad290db82185827d9c1735c57c4fdbe64b2729eaa797e65b5bbbd68fcbd.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the function $y=\\cot x$ are:\n\n- The domain is all real numbers except at the integer multiples of $\\pi$, namely $x= 0,\\pm\\pi,\\pm 2\\pi,\\dots$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The period of the function is $\\pi$.\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n- The zeros occur at $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points that are not in the domain: $x=0, \\pm\\pi,\\pm 2\\pi,\\dots$, and at each asymptote, the graph tends to $-\\infty$ from the left and $\\infty$ from the right." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111870 1065 2506 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111870 1066 #uuid "6602ae3f-3525-55cc-830f-507c2d49ba46" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111870 1067 "Graphing the Cotangent Function" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111871 1064 "When simplifying expressions containing $\\cot$ and $\\csc$, we can use the following **cotangent-cosecant identity**:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x= \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nThe identity above can be obtained by starting with the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^{2} x + \\cos^{2} x = 1\n$$\n\nWe divide both sides by $\\color{black}\\sin^2 x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin^{2} x}{\\sin^{2} x} + \\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{\\sin^{2} x} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above using\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x = \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111871 1065 2548 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111871 1066 #uuid "54661127-f34a-5dfb-9c1e-40f04a041ed0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111871 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111872 1064 "If we subtract $1$ from both sides of the cotangent-cosecant identity\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta - 1\n$$\n\nThis is an alternate form of the cotangent-cosecant identity.\n\nWe can also get a second alternate form if we start with\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta\n$$\n\nand subtract $\\cot^2\\theta$ from both sides. This gives\n\n$$\n\\csc^2\\theta - \\cot^2\\theta = 1\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111872 1065 2549 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111872 1066 #uuid "696f9d61-1bda-572d-8420-77de5d22ca26" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111872 1067 "Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111873 1064 "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(\\cos θ)^{2} + (\\sin θ)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta$, not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111873 1065 2550 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111873 1066 #uuid "f0d65e8f-ee17-505a-8a0c-a8b8c87f02e2" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111873 1067 "The Pythagorean Identity" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111874 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x}\\cos{x}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x\\cos x - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin x &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin x(\\cos x - \\frac{1}{2}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product rule, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x}=0$. The solutions to this equation that lie in the given domain are $x = 0^\\circ$ and $x=180^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0$, or equivalently, $\\cos{x}=\\dfrac12$. The only solution to this equation that lies in the given domain is $x = 60^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions of the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 60^\\circ,\\quad x_3 = 180^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111874 1065 2575 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111874 1066 #uuid "f48575a8-2964-5b25-9b20-92588072e29d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111874 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111875 1064 "We can solve some trigonometric equations containing reciprocal functions using the zero product rule.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n(\\sec x -1)(2\\sin x +1)= 0, \\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the function $\\sec{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x}=0$. On the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$, the excluded values are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nThe equation is already factored, so by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} -1 =0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sec{x} -1 =0$, or equivalently, $\\sec{x} =1$. The only solution to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ is $x = 0$.\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0$, or equivalently, $\\sin{x} = -\\dfrac12$. The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ are $x=-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions to the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\quad x_2 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\quad x_3 = 0\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111875 1065 2576 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111875 1066 #uuid "e82f262f-fd25-5e1a-90d3-7bdfe8e09095" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111875 1067 "Equations Containing Reciprocal Functions" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111876 1064 "How do we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nAs usual, we can use a table of known values.\n\nIn order to determine these known values, let's first look at the graph of the sine function.\n\n![](images/92/92dd5df32efd0c8413d7d7890cc21e8d4e76ad803491f0645b1c4ba98356055e.png)\n\nThe sine function is a one-to-one function on the interval\n\n$$\nx\\in\\left[-\\dfrac \\pi 2, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right]\n$$\n\nSo if we restrict the domain to this interval, we can define the inverse function\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nRemember that the inverse swaps the domain and range of the original function. So the domain of $y=\\arcsin x$ is $x\\in[-1,1]$, while the range is\n\n$$\ny \\in\\left[-\\dfrac \\pi 2, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right]\n$$\n\nIn the domain, we create a table of values:\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\arcsin x$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 2$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 2$ |\n\nUsing this table, we can now plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\n![](images/bd/bdf5638fb2a758a9cb268d7255720ec0c2b099663d118590f08949c5c7016b81.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1]$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}2, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right]$.\n- The minimum value occurs at $x=-1$.\n- The maximum value occurs at $x=1$.\n- The zero occurs at $x=0$.\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111876 1065 2657 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111876 1066 #uuid "00145597-88b7-5f44-8b67-58970b4689ed" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111876 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111877 1064 "How do we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\arccos x\n$$\n\nAs usual, we can use a table of known values.\n\nIn order to determine these known values, let's first look at the graph of the cosine function.\n\n![](images/64/64d1c8ec0c1005fa73494221d859085e31bdaf1aa885b72d432ac2530034a4fe.png)\n\nThe cosine function is a one-to-one function on the interval\n\n$$\nx\\in\\left[ 0, \\pi \\right]\n$$\n\nSo if we restrict the domain to this interval, we can define the inverse function\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\nRemember that the inverse swaps the domain and range of the original function. So the domain of $y=\\arccos x$ is $x\\in[-1,1]$, while the range is\n\n$$\ny \\in\\left[ 0, \\pi \\right]\n$$\n\nIn the domain, we create a table of values:\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\arccos x$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi} 6$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{2\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $0$ |\n\nNow we can use this table to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\n![](images/14/14a1b2786fc80b187db336c13b904b5b049907a3401efec2fe66f665c0b976b1.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1]$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi\\right]$.\n- The minimum value $y=0$ occurs at $x=1$.\n- The maximum value $y=\\pi$ occurs at $x=-1$.\n- The $y$-intercept is $y=\\dfrac \\pi 2$.\n- The function is strictly decreasing on its entire domain." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111877 1065 2678 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111877 1066 #uuid "1ee49287-fa57-558c-872c-9b54b4d25e89" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111877 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111878 1064 "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point.\n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1$. And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] y^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49} \\\\[5pt] y^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49} \\\\[5pt] y&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}} \\\\[5pt] y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer.\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111878 1065 2718 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111878 1066 #uuid "b4029eb2-0222-5d8b-a285-7f0089655736" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111878 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111879 1064 "Suppose we want to plot the graph of $y=\\sin x$, where $x$ is measured in radians.\n\nFirst, let's create a table of values and include some of the values that we know:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 4$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 2$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 2$ | $\\dfrac{7\\pi} 4$ | $2\\pi$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\sin x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve.\n\n![](images/e9/e9e6ae1bace159b05749e6749f38223de6567422e06329fcff2bbf7c0d349b1b.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we know that the function $y=\\sin x$ repeats itself every $2\\pi$ radians. So we can extend our plot using this periodicity property.\n\n![](images/1d/1dfe4444da1a8f30db08c90de8efecbe1214125b1cfbf49c2ae8681edf3d10db.png)\n\nThe key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in[-1,1]$.\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi$.\n- Its zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots$.\n- Its maximum values occur at $\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\ldots$\n- Its minimum values occur at $\\ldots,-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\ldots$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111879 1065 2729 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111879 1066 #uuid "ec0af283-6ac0-5a0e-b211-f6a65db47b01" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111879 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111880 1064 "Suppose that we wish to draw the graph of the function $y=\\cos x$, with $x$ in radians.\n\nFirst, let's write up a table of values, and include some of the specific values of $\\cos x$ that we know:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 4$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 2$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 2$ | $\\dfrac{7\\pi} 4$ | $2\\pi$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\cos x$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, plotting these points, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/dd/dd9452217e387afc804ef96f3501af27c8845ec552ac4f3ae417564f14dfcd9b.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we know that $y=\\cos x$ repeats every $2\\pi$ radians. Repeating the graph using this periodicity property, we have:\n\n![](images/66/6612d11b434ccc11090d1eaab6420493035c7264a25eb6daba62f29c1821b6e3.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1]$.\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi$.\n- Its zeros occur at $x= \\dots,\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- Its maximum values occur at $x=0,\\pm2\\pi, \\pm4\\pi,\\dots$\n- Its minimum values occur at $x=\\pm\\pi,\\pm 3\\pi,\\dots$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111880 1065 2730 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111880 1066 #uuid "5eada7ff-8341-5cf5-99e8-015cd93093be" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111880 1067 "Graphing the Cosine Function" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111881 1064 "Let's now plot the graph of $y=\\tan x$, with $x$ in radians.\n\nFirst, let's create a table for some of the values that we know:\n\n| **$x$** | $-\\dfrac\\pi 2$ | $-\\dfrac\\pi 3$ | $-\\dfrac\\pi 4$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt 3$ | $\\text{undefined}$ |\n\nNow, let's plot these points to estimate the graph of $y=\\tan x$.\n\n![](images/2f/2f99367baaa4b5fb6b49f6704b156f572aeba204f110b603844b7ce82bcb75a4.png)\n\nIf we inspect the unit circle carefully, we see that the function $y=\\tan x$ repeats itself every $\\pi$ radians. Therefore, we can extend our plot using this periodicity property:\n\n![](images/5b/5bf0b36273074a4794968ba2ad7bd7a95a4c1744fb2e67d81dc99a3f6ab90fb9.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph of $y=\\tan x$ are as follows:\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The period of the function is $\\pi$.\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n- The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\pm2\\pi,\\dots$\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$. At each asymptote, the graph tends to $\\infty$ from the left and $-\\infty$ from the right." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111881 1065 2731 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111881 1066 #uuid "a1ff265f-aff1-5092-966b-1ff658df827b" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111881 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111882 1064 "When we compute the area between a curve $y=y(x)$ and the $x$-axis using integration, we must bear the following in mind:\n\n- definite integrals that correspond to areas above the $x$-axis are positive,\n- definite integrals that correspond to areas below the $x$-axis are negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbbb9c009908be0bbafc58e68bb1468d9d99f984b3a8087d7ec02d0cbd2ff5db.png)\n\nThe facts above become crucial when we attempt to find the area bounded by a curve that crosses the $x$-axis." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111882 1065 2735 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111882 1066 #uuid "39bcf1ca-e21e-5c6f-b491-155bfa6e72ad" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111882 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111883 1064 "We can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the **product rule** for logarithms.\n\nThe **product rule** states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(ab\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the product rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{5} 4 + \\log_{5} (3) &= \\log_{5} (4 \\cdot 3) \\\\\n&= \\log_{5} (12)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111883 1065 2782 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111883 1066 #uuid "a915130a-f6b9-5fec-a625-3d6d987255ac" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111883 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111884 1064 "Suppose we want to rewrite $\\log_3(4) - \\log_3(6)$ as a single logarithm. We can do this using the **quotient rule**.\n\nThe quotient rule is very similar to the product rule, except that this time we're dealing with the difference of two logarithms rather than the sum.\n\nThe quotient rule states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n} (a) - \\log_{\\color{blue}n} (b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(\\dfrac a b\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we can use this rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}3}(4) - \\log_{\\color{blue}3}(6)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} 4 - \\log_{3} (6) &= \\log_{3} (\\frac{4}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (\\frac{2}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111884 1065 2784 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111884 1066 #uuid "3ed13d12-2b3d-5700-8a30-aa21719ebea3" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111884 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111885 1064 "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}$. So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{2} (2^{x}) &= \\log_{2} (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left({\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5)\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111885 1065 2794 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111885 1066 #uuid "bcae8943-0768-5280-ad4d-a67f04877fbe" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111885 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111886 1064 "The **power rule** allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n\n$$\n\\log_{n}\\left(c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the logarithm has the effect of \"bringing down the power.\"\n\nFor example, let's use the rule to simplify\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(125\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log (125) &= \\log (5^{3}) \\\\\n&= 3\\log (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log (125) &= \\log (5^{3}) \\\\\n&= \\log (5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5) \\\\\n&= \\log (5) + \\log (5) + \\log (5) \\\\\n&= 3\\log (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule is just a shortcut for the above procedure." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111886 1065 2795 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111886 1066 #uuid "3cb8c8f4-3573-58ab-8e08-3c60cc3a3d12" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111886 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111887 1064 "Now suppose we wish to find the inverse of the logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\nWe can find $f^{-1}(x)$ using the same three steps as before.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx = 3\\log_{2}(y-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\log_{2} (y - 1) + 1 &= x \\\\\n3\\log_{2} (y - 1) &= x - 1 \\\\\n\\log_{2} (y - 1) &= \\frac{x - 1}{3} \\\\\n2^{\\log_{2} (y - 1)} &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} \\\\\ny - 1 &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} \\\\\ny &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = {2}^{(x-1)/3}+1\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111887 1065 2818 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111887 1066 #uuid "cf6e7706-d6b7-5b75-ac3f-184a71b3330a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111887 1067 "Finding the Inverse of a Transformed Logarithmic Function" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111888 1064 "To solve logarithmic equations containing the natural logarithm $(\\ln)$, we can use the same method as usual: first isolate the logarithm, and then exponentiate both sides of the equation using the same base as the logarithm.\n\nThe key is to remember that the base of the natural logarithm is $e$.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve for $x$ in the equation $\\ln x = 2$.\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e$. So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln x &= 2 \\\\\ne^{\\ln x} &= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln x} = x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=e^2$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111888 1065 2821 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111888 1066 #uuid "7e032551-9488-5bc5-858a-2197849a42cf" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111888 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111889 1064 "How do we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\nAs usual, we can use a table of known values.\n\nIn order to determine these known values, let's first look at the graph of the tangent function.\n\n![](images/e6/e6581536f42c2d1d2c60991d6941e23304efbddbbaf95f1109cc6036a394d41c.png)\n\nWe see the tangent function is a one-to-one function on the interval\n\n$$\nx\\in\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\!\n$$\n\nSo if we restrict the domain to this interval, we can define the inverse function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\nRemember that the inverse swaps the domain and range of the original function. So the domain of $y=\\arctan x$ is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$, while the range is\n\n$$\ny \\in\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\!\n$$\n\nIn the domain, we create a table of values:\n\n| **$x$** | $-2-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 3$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 3$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2+\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\arctan x$ | $-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}$ | $-\\dfrac\\pi 3$ | $-\\dfrac\\pi 4$ | $-\\dfrac\\pi 6$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}$ |\n\nNow, we can graph a sketch of the inverse tangent function:\n\n![](images/02/02149dbdbfc4d49bb03c20eeb3ae115d98bc012742efab75eca978f8a1194559.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n- There is a zero at $x=0$.\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty, 0)$ and positive over $(0, \\infty)$.\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n- $y\\to-\\dfrac \\pi 2$ (from above) as $x\\to-\\infty$.\n- $y\\to\\dfrac \\pi 2$ (from below) as $x\\to\\infty$.\n- The lines $y=\\pm\\dfrac \\pi 2$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that the function $y=\\arctan x$ never reaches the value\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac \\pi 2\n$$\n\nas $x\\to-\\infty$. So, there is *no* minimum value. Similarly, the function never reaches the value\n\n$$\n\\dfrac \\pi 2\n$$\n\nas $x\\to\\infty$. So, there is *no* maximum value." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111889 1065 2825 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111889 1066 #uuid "03c9a53f-9e12-5837-815f-299b0c5dbac8" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111889 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111890 1064 "Let's recap some of the rules for exponents.\n\n- **Product rule**: to multiply powers of the same base, add the exponents.\n- **Quotient rule**: to divide powers of the same base, subtract the exponents.\n- **Power rule**: to raise a power to another power, multiply the exponents.\n- **Power of product rule**: to raise the product of two numbers to a power, raise each number to that power, and then multiply.\n- **Power of quotient rule**: to raise a fraction to a power, raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power.\n\nWe can use a combination of these rules to simplify expressions containing exponents. Let's see an example of this." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111890 1065 3275 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111890 1066 #uuid "7c442391-b257-5c16-9fe0-292a45e3c172" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111890 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111891 1064 "Suppose we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nx^2\\geq 4\n$$\n\nLet's start by bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, so\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 4 \\mid \\ge 0\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y = x^2-4$ and find its roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x= \\pm2$.\n\nSolving the inequality\n\n$$\nx^2-4 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2-4$ is above or on the $x$-axis (where\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y \\ge 0}\n$$\n\n).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $x^2-4$ is positive, the parabola opens upward (like $\\smile$). Therefore, the solution set lies outside the roots (and includes the roots), as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15935c6dd4d6ca124da42ec8bac83ca43a12209b71be5fed1f961e342d1039ac.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the initial inequality is $x \\in {\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -2] \\cup [2, +\\infty)}$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111891 1065 3340 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111891 1066 #uuid "3acd4da1-b901-50f7-8e48-07ea09c1af85" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111891 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111892 1064 "We can also solve quadratic inequalities by reducing them to absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality $x^2 > 1$, we can start by taking the square root of both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\nx^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}> 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}> \\sqrt{1}; \\mid x \\mid \\mid > 1\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of absolute value, we must have $x>1$ or $x<-1$.\n\nSo, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty, -1) \\cup (1,\\infty)$.\n\n**Remember:** For any number $a$ that is zero or positive,\n\n- the equation $\\mid x \\mid < a$ has the solution $-a < x < a$,\n- the equation $\\mid x \\mid \\leq a$ has the solution $-a \\leq x \\leq a$,\n- the equation $\\mid x \\mid > a$ has the solution $x > a$ or $x < -a$, and\n- the equation $\\mid x \\mid \\geq a$ has the solution $x \\geq a$ or $x \\leq -a$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111892 1065 3341 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111892 1066 #uuid "d2f937a7-23e9-5ac3-9961-587ca3f805d6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111892 1067 "Solving an Elementary Quadratic Inequality Using the Absolute Value Method" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111893 1064 "Let's consider the inequality\n\n$$\nx^2 \\geq -4\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using either of the two methods that we've learned.\n\n- **Method 1:** Solving an Absolute Value InequalityWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, the square of any real number is also greater than or equal to $-4$, since $x^2 \\geq 0 > -4$.Therefore, *any* real number will satisfy the inequality $x^2 \\geq -4$. So, we conclude that the solution to the inequality is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\n- **Method 2:** Picturing a Quadratic CurveLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n$x^2+4 \\geq 0$,\nand then plot the parabola $y = x^2+4$. The desired inequality ($y \\geq 0$) is always satisfied, so the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\n![](images/b2/b26de09c0de43abfaf7da81015fcf2b1355859dd1accace02092296bf2415fb3.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111893 1065 3369 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111893 1066 #uuid "f410f757-9e31-5903-b8ac-6e34da0ab612" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111893 1067 "Solving Elementary Quadratic Inequalities with No Solutions or All Real Solutions" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111894 1064 "When money is deposited into a bank account, the bank pays an **annual interest.** This interest can be **compounded**, or split, throughout the year.\n\nThe interest is worked out for the first period and added to the total. Then, the interest is calculated for the next period, added to the total, and so on.\n\nTo calculate how much money, $P$, we will have in an account after $t$ years, we can use the **compound interest equation**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial deposit, $r$ is the annual interest rate, and\n$n$ is the number of times payments are compounded per year.\n\nFor example, suppose you deposit\n\n$$\nP_0=\\[math]1,000\n$$\n\ninto a bank account, and a bank employee suggests three options:\n\n- An account paying $r=12\\%$ annual interest compounded yearly.\n- An account paying $r=12\\%$ annual interest compounded quarterly.\n- An account paying $r=12\\%$ annual interest compounded monthly.\n\nWhich should you choose to have the most money after $t=1$ year?\n\n- If payments are **compounded annually**, there is only $n=1$ payment in the year. In this case, at the end of the first year, we will have\n$P= \\[/math] 1,000 \\left(1+\\dfrac{0.12}{1}\\right)^{(1)(1)}=\\[math] 1,120$.\n- When payments are **compounded quarterly**, we receive a payment for each quarter of the year. There are $n=4$ quarters in the year, so at the end of the first year, we will get\n$P= \\[/math] 1,000 \\left(1+\\dfrac{0.12}{4}\\right)^{(4)(1)}\\approx \\[math] 1\\,125.51$.\n- On the other hand, when payments are **compounded monthly**, we receive a payment each month of the year. There are $n=12$ months in the year, so at the end of the first year, we will have\n$P= \\[/math] 1,000 \\left(1+\\dfrac{0.12}{12}\\right)^{(12)(1)}\\approx \\[math] 1\\,126.83$.\n\nThe accounts are not the same after all! The most profitable account is the one with the annual interest compounded monthly." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111894 1065 3395 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111894 1066 #uuid "0144634d-68d9-55a5-a206-3cfb290f1422" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111894 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111895 1064 "Let's recall the multinomial theorem:\n\n> *For any positive integer $n$, the terms of $(a+b+c)^n$ can be written as*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} a^{k_1} b^{k_2} c^{k_3}$,\n> *where $k_1 + k_2 + k_3 = n$ and the general formula for the multinomial coefficient is*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} = \\dfrac{n!}{k_1!\\, k_2! \\, k_3!}$.\n\nFor example, using the multinomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(a + b + c)^{2} &= (\\frac{2}{2, 0, 0})a^{2}b^{0}c^{0} + (\\frac{2}{0, 2, 0})a^{0}b^{2}c^{0} + (\\frac{2}{0, 0, 2})a^{0}b^{0}c^{2} \\\\\n&== + (\\frac{2}{1, 1, 0})a^{1}b^{1}c^{0} + (\\frac{2}{1, 0, 1})a^{1}b^{0}c^{1} + (\\frac{2}{0, 1, 1})a^{0}b^{1}c^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2!}{2!0!0!}a^{2} + \\frac{2!}{0!2!0!}b^{2} + \\frac{2!}{0!0!2!}c^{2} + \\frac{2!}{1!1!0!}ab + \\frac{2!}{1!0!1!}ac + \\frac{2!}{0!1!1!}bc \\\\\n&= a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + 2ab + 2ac + 2bc\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $6$ terms in the above expansion:\n\n$$\n(a + b + c)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + 2ab + 2ac + 2bc_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nIn general, the number of distinct terms in the multinomial expansion with $t$ variables raised to the $n$th power is given by\n$\\binom{n+t-1}{t-1}$.\n\nIn the case of $(a+b+c)^2$, we have a multinomial expansion with $t=3$ variables raised to the $n=2$nd power. Therefore, the number of distinct terms in the expansion of $(a+b+c)^2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{2 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1}) &= (\\frac{4}{2}) = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, this matches up with what we saw in our expansion.\n\nNote that one of the variables can actually be a nonzero constant and the number of terms will still be the same. For example, we could take $c = 1$ above and we'd still get an expansion with $6$ terms:\n\n$$\n(a + b + 1)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + 1 + 2ab + 2a + 2b_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nHowever, at most one variable can be a nonzero constant; the rest must be distinct variables." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111895 1065 3823 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111895 1066 #uuid "5d81a99e-aeb6-5054-be9c-19c64fe32d28" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111895 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111896 1064 "A **face diagonal** of a cube is any line segment that joins two opposite vertices on one of the cube's faces.\n\nThe diagram below shows a cube with side length\n\n$$\ns=3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nas well as the face diagonal $\\overline{BE}$. Let's denote the length of the face diagonal as $f$.\n\n![](images/65/657de0aba34e91579406855a632a37783736b0928bf002a82e025719dffe225a.png)\n\nThe formula that relates the length of a face diagonal and sides is given by\n\n$$\nf = s\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\nFor the cube above, substituting\n\n$$\ns = 3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get that the length of the diagonal is\n\n$$\nf = 3\\sqrt{2} \\,\\textrm{m}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111896 1065 4347 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111896 1066 #uuid "0caa694c-5ab1-54c9-9e45-6f1ca916fced" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111896 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111897 1064 "Suppose that we have a cube whose edges are $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ in length, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b974b6e782801fbe2099971afd0df9a5c6e05a10afc2ce0e4054c850088e753b.png)\n\nThe formula for the volume $V$ of a cube with sides of length $s$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = s^3\n$$\n\nTo find the volume of this cube, we substitute\n\n$$\ns=2 \\: \\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula. This gives\n\n$$\nV = (2\\, \\textrm{cm})^3 = 8 \\text{cm}^3\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the cube is $8\\,\\textrm{cm}^3$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111897 1065 4348 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111897 1066 #uuid "7ebfe2f1-6dce-5f67-bf2d-58fcd1660014" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111897 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111898 1064 "When a point is reflected across the line $y = x$, the $x$-coordinate and $y$-coordinate are swapped. This transformation can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (y,x)\n$$\n\nFor example, reflecting the point $P(4,1)$ in the line $y=x$ gives the point $P'(1,4)$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/a4/a46dc01739d85dc6eaf7e65ab2f1921166f50df08abfd61202766ddbb8e49739.png)\n\nWhen a point is reflected in the line $y = -x$, the $x$-coordinate and $y$-coordinate swap and are transformed into their opposites (their signs are changed). This transformation can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-y,-x)\n$$\n\nFor example, reflecting the point $Q(-1,4)$ in the line $y=-x$ gives the point $Q'(-4,1)$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/0c/0c59c8235ec76de8574ef42447b06ca2a31520a6c6b835c55805cc5f75825f9e.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111898 1065 4654 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111898 1066 #uuid "cdac2989-8811-5f0b-8e5a-47accf61b2a0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111898 1067 "Reflection Across Other Special Lines in the Cartesian Plane" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111899 1064 "Sometimes, when we want to solve polynomial inequalities using the graphical method, we need to apply special factoring techniques to plot the polynomial. Specifically, the techniques that we might want to apply are:\n\n- factoring by grouping\n- the biquadratic factoring method\n- the rational roots theorem\n\nFor instance, suppose we want to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3 -3x^2 -x +3\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x)$. In this case, we can factor the polynomial by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{3} - 3x^{2} - x + 3 \\\\\n&= (x^{3} - 3x^{2}) - (x - 3) \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x - 3) - (x - 3) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 1)(x - 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)(x - 1)(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=-1$, $x=1$, and $x=3$, which are all simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/17/17f8634726ab1c08ec94de7a6f47ea20b6297d8ab24f04b88d6b48f92f80e2b9.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq0\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in [-1, 1] \\cup [3, \\infty)\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111899 1065 4761 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111899 1066 #uuid "dd1024d4-036c-50d6-9648-53215a9551ef" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111899 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111900 1064 "The graph of the square root function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4d6f457f29cc6a292f23029967dc6fbb0c0f3905a04f34a430fddc8b211b0c3.png)\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to apply graph transformations to the square root function. Let's start by considering translations." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111900 1065 4775 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111900 1066 #uuid "ff943ba5-7959-599a-8f13-e5bdb1bda83a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111900 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111901 1064 "We can move the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nup or down by adding or subtracting a number. For example, to obtain the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}-3\n$$\n\nwe perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Translate the graph **down** by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}-3$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b5eadb281202323377386e6b750a304ece8f39232d0c37dc5d4885004a6b588.png)\n\nSimilarly, we can also move the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nto the left or to the right by adding or subtracting a number to the variable $x$ under the square root. For instance, to obtain the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x-3}\n$$\n\nwe perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Translate the graph to the **right** by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-3}$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d51b569648468c78445daa6b8dc658fe6e2e8cb24ca8308b60a1f40b07c7e29.png)\n\nRemember:\n\n- Shifting a function up/down/left/right does *not* change the shape of a graph.\n- If $a$ is a positive number, then a transformation of the type $y=f(x\\pm a)$ is *counter-intuitive*: $f(x+a)$ moves the graph to the *left* by $a$ units, while $f(x-a)$ moves the graph to the *right* by $a$ units." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111901 1065 4776 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111901 1066 #uuid "7bdd8c52-b035-531e-b5ba-085731e44771" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111901 1067 "Vertical and Horizontal Translations" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111902 1064 "We can reflect the graph of the square root function\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt x\n$$\n\nin both the $x$ and $y$-axis.\n\nMultiplying the $\\sqrt x$ by $-1$ reflects the curve in the $x$-axis. The graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt x\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eef724b522e133a2a537d1b6e1406bdbf17fe285ea43c278888ab4a4bc9fa58c.png)\n\nMultiplying the variable $x$ by $-1$ reflects the curve in the $y$-axis. The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{-x}\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/78/782eade24c5ab9e1af679f0a310fac2d3e20805d40ee97c695ea214ac71cf2f0.png)\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{-x}\n$$\n\nis only defined for the negative numbers and zero.\n\nNow, suppose we want to sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny= -\\sqrt{x-2}+3\n$$\n\nTo obtain the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nwe can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Reflect the graph in the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x}$.\n\n![](images/b9/b95995b8ee5595d774f02d2c03c1170cc443b8f06f6ccbc634eeeb2aebe86357.png)\n\n- Translate the result to the right by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x-2}$.\n- Translate the result up by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x-2}+3$.\n\n![](images/26/263ea3a28314da17ba084bcb512976deb73483dfaa3ad149845c35634ae1d4a4.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111902 1065 4777 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111902 1066 #uuid "55172b36-dabd-52b3-8195-cbe97b913cc0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111902 1067 "Reflections" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111903 1064 "Every cube contains precisely $12$ face diagonals (two for each of the six faces), and they are all congruent. All twelve face diagonals of a cube are shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0000e65116366105ad562437acc2b55bba8d870ae591fab22cf9c8029aa8059.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111903 1065 5414 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111903 1066 #uuid "744a58ac-aa00-5074-b1dd-aa0a1f6ea5d4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111903 1067 "Counting the Face Diagonals of a Cube" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111904 1064 "Here, we will derive the formula\n\n$$\nf=s\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nthat we have been using throughout this lesson.\n\nConsider a cube of side length $s$ and face diagonal length $f$, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5720a29a22b99bd133e0e941fb529d3ed7fb6dadb0aceadaecef3e59e70daa9.png)\n\nNotice that $\\triangle ABC$ is a right triangle with legs $AB$ and $AC$ and hypotenuse $BC$. Therefore, by the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns^{2} + s^{2} &= f^{2} \\\\\n2s^{2} &= f^{2} \\\\\nf &= \\sqrt{2s^{2}} \\\\\nf &= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{s^{2}} \\\\\nf &= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot s \\\\\nf &= s\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we only consider the positive square root since $f$ is a length and therefore must be positive." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111904 1065 5416 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111904 1066 #uuid "a1168aa1-26f6-55d0-ba67-e0ae424b581e" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111904 1067 "Deriving the Face Diagonal Length Formula" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111905 1064 "We can extend our rule relating sums of adjacent angles to more than two angles.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/32/3264d0ab1a40b02915a737dad57e9f26820193d9bdc6b42431ec1ab6508cc523.png)\n\nAccording to our rule, the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle QST$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠QST &= m∠QSP_(⏟)_(green angle) + m∠PSR_(⏟)_(orange angle) + m∠RST_(⏟)_(blue angle) \\\\\n&= 100^{∘} + 95^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 240^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use this rule to determine the size of a smaller angle when we know a larger one. Let's see an example." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111905 1065 5701 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111905 1066 #uuid "ed758074-8bf2-5fa7-a897-7d4e08bc5413" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111905 1067 "Extending the Sums of Angles Rule" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111906 1064 "We can organize all mentioned types of quadrilaterals into the following hierarchy diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nEach shape below another is a specific type of the one above it. For example, every square is a rectangle, and every rectangle is a parallelogram. This means every square is also a parallelogram." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111906 1065 5931 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111906 1066 #uuid "d9d3be2f-753b-5a4b-9f26-20b34ab8f89a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111906 1067 "Classifying Quadrilaterals" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111907 1064 "Suppose a gym surveys all of its members. They find that $25$ males and $24$ females exercise twice a week, and $15$ males and $18$ females exercise three times a week. How can we represent this information using a frequency table?\n\nNotice that we now have *two* categorical variables, namely\n\n- the gender of each member (male or female), and\n- how often they exercise per week (twice a week or three times a week).\n\nSince there are two categorical variables, we can organize this information using a **joint frequency table.**\n\nTo construct a joint frequency table, we first label the rows and columns using the possible values of our two categorical variables, as follows:\n\n|  | Twice a week | Three times a week |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Males |  |  |\n| Females |  |  |\n\nNow, we fill in the cells using the information provided.\n\n- We're told that $25$ males exercise twice a week. So, we write this number at the intersection of the \"Males\" row and the \"Twice a week\" column.\n\n|  | Twice a week | Three times a week |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Males | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25}$ |  |\n| Females |  |  |\n\n- We're told that $24$ females exercise twice a week. So, we write this number at the intersection of the \"Females\" row and the \"Twice a week\" column.\n\n|  | Twice a week | Three times a week |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Males | $25$ |  |\n| Females | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}24}$ |  |\n\n- We're also told that $15$ males and $18$ females exercise three times a week. Adding this data to our table, we get the following joint frequency table.\n\n|  | Twice a week | Three times a week |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Males | $25$ | $15$ |\n| Females | $24$ | $18$ |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The numbers $25$, $15$, $24$, and $18$ are called the **joint frequencies** because they each represent joint data from two categorical variables.\n- Joint frequency tables are sometimes called **two-way frequency tables** or **contingency tables.**" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111907 1065 6021 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111907 1066 #uuid "78c5839c-f64a-546f-a368-1bc06a77fa3d" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111907 1067 "Two-Way Frequency Tables" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111908 1064 "|  | Twice a week | Three times a week |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $25$ | $15$ |\n| Female | $24$ | $18$ |\n\nNow that we have our two-way frequency table, we can analyze the data set.\n\nOne question that we can ask is, \"How many males attend the gym?\" To answer this question, let's consider the data in the \"male\" row only:\n\n|  | Twice a week | Three times a week |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $25$ | $15$ |\n| Female | $24$ | $18$ |\n\nTo determine the total number of males who attend the gym, we sum the joint frequencies in this row.\n\n$$\n25 + 15 = {\\color{red}40}\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to extend the joint frequency table to include the totals of each row and column. So let's add a \"Totals\" column and \"Totals\" row to the right and bottom of the table, and include the total number of males in the table as follows:\n\n|  | Twice a week | Three times a week | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $25$ | $15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}40}$ |\n| Female | $24$ | $18$ |  |\n| Totals |  |  |  |\n\nSimilarly, we can fill in the rest of the cells in the \"Totals\" row and \"Totals\" column by summing the entries in each row and column.\n\n|  | Twice a week | Three times a week | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $25$ | $15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{40}$ |\n| Female | $24$ | $18$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{42}$ |\n| Totals | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{49}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{33}$ |  |\n\nThe highlighted numbers, $40$, $42$, $49$, and $33$ are called the **marginal frequencies**. These numbers tell us that\n\n- $40$ males attend the gym,\n- $42$ females attend the gym,\n- $49$ members exercise twice per week, and\n- $33$ members exercise three times per week.\n\nAnother question we can ask is, \"How many members attend the gym in total?\" We can get this information by summing either the \"Totals\" row or the \"Totals\" column. Note that we'll get the same answer either way.\n\nSumming the data in the \"Totals\" column, we have\n\n$$\n40 + 42 = 82\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gym has $82$ members. We can add this data to the bottom-right cell in the table, as follows:\n\n|  | Twice a week | Three times a week | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $25$ | $15$ | $40$ |\n| Female | $24$ | $18$ | $42$ |\n| Totals | $49$ | $33$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}82}$ |" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111908 1065 6022 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111908 1066 #uuid "68c5afb4-dcc8-5895-9928-002cc7ceb635" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111908 1067 "Marginal Frequencies" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111909 1064 "When the sides of a rectangle or square in the plane are *not* horizontal and vertical, we must use the distance formula to calculate the side lengths.\n\nRecall that the distance $d$ between two points $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(x_A, y_A)$ and $(x_B, y_B)$ respectively is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(x_B - x_A)^2 + (y_B - y_A)^2}\n$$\n\nLet's see how to apply this to compute areas of squares." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111909 1065 6179 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111909 1066 #uuid "fde521fb-efc2-58db-b33f-5ca15f62e408" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111909 1067 "Using the Distance Formula" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111910 1064 "To simplify the expression $x\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^2}$, we first rewrite the radical as a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\nx \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^2} = x \\cdot \\left(x^2 \\right)^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\nx \\cdot \\left(x^2 \\right)^{1/3} = x \\cdot x^{2(1/3)} = x \\cdot x^{2/3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\cdot x^{2/3} &=  \\\\\nx^{1 + 2/3} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{5/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\nx^{5/3} = \\sqrt[3]{x^5}\n$$\n\nNotice that, by combining the radicals as fractional exponents and power rules, we get the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111910 1065 6221 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111910 1066 #uuid "91debf57-af23-518b-b432-ea04beccc17a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111910 1067 "Rules of Exponents and Radicals" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111911 1064 "Suppose we have the transformed exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2^{5x}+1\n$$\n\nand we wish to find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$. We can do this using the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2^{5x} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2^{5y} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2^{5y} + 1 \\\\\nx - 1 &= 2^{5y} \\\\\n\\log_{2} (x - 1) &= \\log_{2} (2^{5y}) \\\\\n\\log_{2} (x - 1) &= 5y \\\\\n5y &= \\log_{2} (x - 1) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{5}\\log_{2} (x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_2{(x-1)}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111911 1065 6516 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111911 1066 #uuid "dc5609e8-50a8-59fe-af6c-4cfc16ccc454" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111911 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111912 1064 "For any two distinct points, there is always one (and only one) line that passes through them.\n\nFor example, the line passing through the points $P$ and $Q$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$, while the line that passes through $A$ and $B$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$:\n\n![](images/8c/8c79a551e7ced71de157ea292baa3d7f38afaca4ea76ee467fc4add1b28ed33f.png)\n\nHowever, given three or more points, there is not always a line that passes through all of them.\n\nFor example, for the three points $F, G$, and $H$ below, it is impossible to draw a single straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/30/30361c0bd49e571e317746f2d88848626da52e5ddbcda8fe9b6f496e4aff6b69.png)\n\nHowever, it is *sometimes* possible to draw a line through three (or more) points. In such cases, we say that the points are **collinear**.\n\nFor example, the points $M$, $N$, and $O$ shown below are collinear since they all lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/bf/bf3de55518c21de30718b2d9fee7032139c89aa3a4a963b8346b457ceafbeada.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111912 1065 6732 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111912 1066 #uuid "051ac95a-dc63-5270-a5fa-a498a36c8297" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111912 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111913 1064 "To plot the graph of a logarithmic function, it helps to remember that the inverse of a logarithmic function is an exponential function. So, we can just plot the exponential function and reflect it over the line $y=x$.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2 x\n$$\n\nThe corresponding inverse function is $y=2^x$, and since $y=2^x$ is a one-to-one function defined for all real $x$, we can obtain the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ by reflecting $y=2^x$ over the line $y=x$. The result is shown below:\n\n![](images/0c/0c614201798324262429f080d8e8a31bfefe58c4008bfe82e6da1b91dc158b7a.png)\n\nHere are some key points to note about\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2(x)\n$$\n\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty < y < \\infty$.\n- The graph has the vertical asymptote $x=0$.\n- The function is an increasing function.\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0)$.\n- For $x>1$, the graph is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x$. We say that $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ (slowly) as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n\nIn general, for any base $n>1$, the graph of $y=\\log_n{x}$ always has the same shape, and all of the above points are valid. We can verify that this is true by inspecting some possible graphs shown below:\n\n![](images/91/91567f282acbc3b8390da1e4147b445018ae649d913205e59867d58ea980ff7b.png)\n\n**Note:** The important points covered above also apply to two familiar logarithms.\n\n- $y = \\ln x = \\log_e x$ is the natural logarithm. Since the base is $n=e>1$, all of the important points covered above apply.\n- $y = \\log x = \\log_{10} x$ is the logarithm with base $10$. Since the base is $n=10>1$, all the important points covered above apply." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111913 1065 6826 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111913 1066 #uuid "018709be-13e5-5b9c-9eea-914f4766db68" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111913 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111914 1064 "Our goal for this lesson is to prove the following theorem:\n\n> *All circles are similar.*\n\nFirst, we define the **unit circle**.\n\n> *The unit circle is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin.*\n\nThroughout this lesson, we'll denote the unit circle with the symbol $\\mathcal U$ (curly-$u$).\n\nNow, consider the following diagram, which shows the unit circle and a circle $\\mathcal{C}$ of radius $2$ centered at the origin $O$.\n\n![](images/f8/f8685eb0703d8eae9fa534f9d1c167287b7ca3350ab73b948fd86165e584c905.png)\n\nWe want to prove that $\\mathcal{C}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{U}$.\n\nRecall that two geometric figures are similar if there exists a sequence of similarity transformations (translations, rotations, reflections, and dilations) that map one figure onto another.\n\nSo, we begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *To prove that $\\mathcal C$ is similar to $\\mathcal U$, we construct a sequence of similarity transformations that map $\\mathcal{C}$ onto $\\mathcal{U}$.*\n\nWe can map $\\mathcal C$ onto $\\mathcal U$ using a dilation of scale factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand center $O$.\n\n> *A dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac12$ and center $O$ maps the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{U}$.*\n\nThis proves that $\\mathcal C$ is similar to $\\mathcal U$. So, we state our conclusion:\n\n> *Therefore, $\\mathcal{C} \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's write down the full proof." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111914 1065 7075 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111914 1066 #uuid "f5f6b2b2-6424-5a3c-8969-900af21537bd" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111914 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111915 1064 "Given any triangle, all three medians intersect at a single point called the **centroid** of the triangle.\n\n![](images/cb/cbd0243742d6521cc55d11ba87fc4f30404962f9eb1a094408c45acbcfe974ba.png)\n\nThe centroid of a triangle is also its center of mass. In other words, the centroid is where we'd have to place a pencil tip to balance the triangle perfectly.\n\nMore formally, the **centroid theorem** states the following:\n\n> *For any triangle, the three medians are **concurrent**, meaning they intersect at a single point. This point of intersection is called the **centroid** of the triangle.*\n\nWe'll prove the centroid theorem in a future lesson." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111915 1065 7186 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111915 1066 #uuid "cdf18958-7ee6-5097-8076-bdebd65e8d5a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111915 1067 "The Centroid Theorem" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111916 1064 "An **equation** consists of two mathematical expressions separated by an equals $(=)$ sign. The expressions may or may not contain variables. Two examples of equations are shown below:\n\n$$\n2 + 3 = 5 \\qquad\\qquad 2 + x = 5\n$$\n\nAn equation is **true** if the expression to the left side of the equals sign is equal to the expression on the right side. Otherwise, if the left side is not equal to the right side, then the equation is **false**. For instance,\n\n- the equation $2 + 3 = 5$ is **true**, whereas\n- the equation $2+2 = 5$ is **false**.\n\nAs a result, the equation\n\n$$\n2 + x = 5\n$$\n\nis true *only if* $x=3$. We say that $x=3$ is the **solution** to the equation\n\n$$\n2+x=5\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111916 1065 8166 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111916 1066 #uuid "72db5a21-14d9-5476-a1be-f9fddc69fb06" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111916 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111917 1064 "If we have rational expressions on both sides of an equation, say,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{red}x-1}}{{\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}2x-1}}{{\\color{red}5}}\n$$\n\nthen we can eliminate the rational expressions by applying **cross-multiplication**:\n\n- we cross-multiply the left-hand side numerator $({\\color{red}x-1})$ with the right-hand side denominator $({\\color{red}5})$, and\n- we cross-multiply the left-hand side denominator $({\\color{blue}2})$ with the right-hand side numerator $({\\color{blue}2x-1})$.\n\nDoing this, we get the following equation:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}x-1}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}5}) = ({\\color{blue}2}) \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2x - 1})\n$$\n\nAfter we've cross-multiplied, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1) \\cdot 5 &= 2 \\cdot (2x - 1) \\\\\n5x - 5 &= 4x - 2 \\\\\n5x - 5 + 5 &= 4x - 2 + 5 \\\\\n5x &= 4x + 3 \\\\\n5x - 4x &= 4x + 3 - 4x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Cross-multiplication might seem magical, but it is really just a shortcut for multiplying both sides of the equation by each denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x - 1}{2} &= \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{x - 1}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{x - 1}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\\\\nx - 1 &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\\\\n(x - 1) \\cdot 5 &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n(x - 1) \\cdot 5 &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n(x - 1) \\cdot (5) &= (2) \\cdot (2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111917 1065 8174 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111917 1066 #uuid "99ff1453-e12b-518f-94b1-9baf8ee914e6" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111917 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111918 1064 "Consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{2} = -\\dfrac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nEven though one of the fractions is negative, we can still use cross-multiplication. All we have to do is transfer the negative into the numerator of the fraction, and proceed with cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x + 1}{2} &= -\\frac{x}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{x + 1}{2} &= \\frac{-x}{3} \\\\\n(x + 1) \\cdot 3 &= 2 \\cdot (-x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1) \\cdot 3 &= 2 \\cdot (-x) \\\\\n3x + 3 &= -2x \\\\\n3x + 3 - 3x &= -2x - 3x \\\\\n3 &= -5x \\\\\n\\frac{3}{-5} &= \\frac{-5x}{-5} \\\\\n- \\frac{3}{5} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111918 1065 8175 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111918 1066 #uuid "ed67f4e3-4ff1-5900-bda0-c7f4e2b5a652" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111918 1067 "Cross-Multiplying with a Negative Sign" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111919 1064 "Loosely speaking, a **rational equation** is an equation containing rational expressions.\n\nWhen a rational equation contains only one fractional term, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nwe can multiply both sides of the equation by the denominator to remove the fraction. This leaves us with a linear equation, which we can then solve using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nBefore we proceed, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because if $x=0$ then the rational term would be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we multiply both sides by the denominator ${\\color{blue} x}$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{x} &= 1 \\\\\nx \\cdot \\frac{2}{x} &= x \\cdot 1 \\\\\nx \\cdot \\frac{2}{x} &= x \\cdot 1 \\\\\n2 &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=2$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111919 1065 8778 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111919 1066 #uuid "a963a90e-094e-50e9-b621-0f4c926aa056" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111919 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111920 1064 "A biologist is experimenting with bacteria stored in a Petri dish. She observes that the number of bacteria in the dish *triples* every hour. If there were $100$ bacteria initially, can she determine how many bacteria will be present after $3$ hours?\n\nStarting with $100$ bacteria, she knows that this number *triples* after one hour. Remember that tripling means to multiply by a factor of $3$.\n\nTo get the number of bacteria after one hour, we multiply the initial amount by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}{100\\cdot 3}\n$$\n\nAfter another hour ($2$ hours in total), the number above triples again. So to get the total number of bacteria after $2$ hours, we multiply the amount above by $3$:\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{100\\cdot 3}})\\cdot 3 = {\\color{red}{100 \\cdot 3^2}}\n$$\n\nAnd after another hour ($3$ hours total), the number above triples once again:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}{100 \\cdot 3^2}})\\cdot 3 = 100 \\cdot 3^3\n$$\n\nIf we evaluate the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n100 \\cdot 3^3 = 100\\cdot 27 = 2\\,700\n$$\n\nSo after $3$ hours, there will be $2\\,700$ bacteria in the Petri dish.\n\nThis is an example of **exponential growth**. The number of bacteria triples over a time interval of $1$ hour, every hour. The total number of bacteria grows very rapidly, as we can see from the chart below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf039743a936cab0ca74708584fc3670202588d15fc63a4257ad1a737047da2d.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111920 1065 8841 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111920 1066 #uuid "60dc9833-8054-5a01-9e29-d592d2e4498a" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111920 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111921 1064 "Two lines are **perpendicular** if they intersect at a right angle. For example, the lines\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y = 2x - 1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+5}\n$$\n\nshown below are perpendicular:\n\n![](images/1a/1a6eb1344ebbc9b41f58e7e68594d6913f4ce75a60ed0f0a3bd869168d088e08.png)\n\nWe can tell whether two lines are perpendicular by looking at their slopes. In general,\n\n> *If two lines are perpendicular, then the product of their slopes is equal to $-1$.*\n\nWe see this with the two lines graphed above: the lines\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y = 2x - 1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+5}\n$$\n\nhave slopes $\\color{red}2$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nrespectively. If we take the product of the slopes, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)}} = -1\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The only exception is for horizontal and vertical lines. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular, but the slope of a vertical line is undefined, so we cannot multiply it by anything." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111921 1065 8899 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111921 1066 #uuid "db71b1d0-4715-5f77-a9f9-84aa1abe83de" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111921 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111922 1064 "We've seen how to calculate probabilities for normally distributed random variables $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ when $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are both *known.*\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the mean $\\mu$ or the variance $\\sigma^2$ of a normally distributed random variable when given a specific probability.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a normally distributed random variable $X$ with *unknown* mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}$ and *known* variance\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\sigma}}^2 = {\\color{red}2}^2\n$$\n\nWe write this as follows:\n\n$$\nX \\sim N({\\color{blue}\\mu}, {\\color{red}2}^2)\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq 2.1) = 0.7088\n$$\n\nWe can use this additional information about $X$ to calculate the mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}$.\n\nFirst, we transform to a standard normal random variable $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\n$$\n\nApplying the $z$-scoring transformation to our probability statement, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 2.1) &= 0.7088 \\\\\nP(\\frac{X - μ}{σ} \\le \\frac{2.1 - μ}{σ}) &= 0.7088 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{2.1 - μ}{σ}) &= 0.7088\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\sigma}} = {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nP\\left(Z \\leq \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{\\color{red}{2}}\\right) = 0.7088\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\nP(Z\\leq z) = \\Phi(z)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is the cumulative distribution function of $Z$, we can write the above statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\\right) = 0.7088 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7088) = \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ |\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.55) = 0.7088 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7088) = 0.55\n$$\n\nEquating our two expressions for $\\Phi^{-1}(0.7088)$, we can now solve for $\\mu$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2.1 - μ}{2} &= 0.55 \\\\\n2.1 - μ &= 1.1 \\\\\nμ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111922 1065 8989 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111922 1066 #uuid "5a61536c-c1a8-53d1-a0b6-46caba6fec29" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111922 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111923 1064 "It's often helpful to use the symmetry properties of the normal distribution to help us determine unknown parameters.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following normal random variable with unknown variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX \\sim N(1, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nSuppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(-2 \\lt X \\lt 4) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nThe interval $-2 \\lt X \\lt 4$ and the area associated with the probability that $X$ lies within this interval are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1101c2617de9f63394c7aa7da89fd7590ab401d792ba38e0c9c5fa8f8c4e9330.png)\n\nThe endpoints of the interval $(-2,4)$ both lie at a distance of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ from the mean $\\mu$. So, this interval is symmetric about the mean, and we can write the given probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(\\mu - {\\color{red}{3}} \\lt X \\lt \\mu + {\\color{red}{3}}) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the total shaded equals twice the area between the mean $\\mu$ and the right endpoint:\n\n$$\n2P(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $2$, we get\n\n$$\nP(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nLet's write this probability in terms of $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\nP(\\frac{μ - μ}{σ} < \\frac{X - μ}{σ} < \\frac{μ + 3 - μ}{σ}) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\nP(0 < Z < \\frac{3}{σ}) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of $\\Phi$, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) - \\Phi(0) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nand since we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0) = 0.5\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) - 0.5= 0.4357\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) = 0.9357\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} = \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357)\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z)$,\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9207 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9332 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9345 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9452 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9463 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9474 \\,$ |\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.52) = 0.9357 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357) = 1.52\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $\\sigma{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{σ} &= 1.52 \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{3}{1.52} \\\\\n&= σ \\mid \\approx 1.974\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111923 1065 8990 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111923 1066 #uuid "a748c912-a01f-5524-83df-8f7ffeb40112" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111923 1067 "Using Symmetry Properties" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111924 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2 = 4\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side is a perfect square and the right-hand side is a constant:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(x+1)^2}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\textrm{perfect square}} = \\overbrace{\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!4\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!}^{\\large\\color{red}\\textrm{constant}} \\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can solve the equation using the square root method. First, we take the square root of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= 2 \\\\\nx + 1 &= ± 2 \\\\\nx &= -1 ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -1 + 2 = 1 \\\\\nx &= -1 - 2 =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3$ and $x=1$." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111924 1065 9201 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111924 1066 #uuid "df0a169f-0b13-5cb1-bac5-a328aa175052" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111924 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111925 1064 "Many quadratic equations have two solutions. However, it's also possible for a quadratic equation to have only one solution or no real solutions at all.\n\nFor example, consider the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(3x-6)^2=0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x - 6)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(3x - 6)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{0} \\\\\n\\mid 3x - 6 \\mid &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormally, at this stage, we would obtain two solutions by setting the inside of the absolute value equal to positive or negative $(\\pm)$ the right-hand side. However, in this case, the right-hand side is zero, and $+0$ and $-0$ are both just $0$.\n\nSo, we have only one solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid 3x - 6 \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n3x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111925 1065 9203 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111925 1066 #uuid "3b3899a9-dbc6-524a-b493-c57a45bfeda9" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111925 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111926 1064 "Let's try and solve the equation\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2=-1\n$$\n\nusing the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= \\sqrt{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a problem here. We cannot take the square root of a negative number. For that reason, the equation above has *no real solutions*." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111926 1065 9205 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111926 1066 #uuid "414b731c-3f76-57ad-985a-3d64bda214e0" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111926 1067 "Perfect Square Quadratic Equations With No Real Solutions" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111927 1064 "If a parabola has two roots, the axis of symmetry occurs precisely halfway between the roots of the parabola. Therefore, we can find the axis of symmetry of a parabola by finding the roots and then taking their average value.\n\nSo, if the roots are $x_1$ and $x_2$, the **average of the roots formula** tells us that the axis of symmetry of the parabola is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's consider the parabola\n\n$$\ny=(x+2)(x-4)\n$$\n\nWe have the roots $x_1=-2$ and $x_2=4$. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{-2 + 4}{2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the axis of symmetry of the parabola\n\n$$\ny=(x+2)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis $x=1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec08bc1ba75082ac98bb6559b6c0ef8119fbf245f3c266710ce8c6a569fa8a02.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111927 1065 9250 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111927 1066 #uuid "cd361f4f-6e49-5ced-8872-2849078d909c" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111927 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111928 1064 "Two angles are **supplementary** if their measures add up to $180^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/88/886f1ce19ad595b470d5ad13943b780bd5a22ee737908d9554e97bd08c9ce985.png)\n\nThese angles are supplementary because the sum of their measures is $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n105^\\circ +75^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nA **linear pair** is a pair of adjacent angles formed when two lines intersect. The angles shown above form a linear pair.\n\nThe **linear pair postulate** states the following:\n\n> If two angles form a linear pair, then they are supplementary.\n\nThe converse is *not* true. That is, if two angles are supplementary, they do not necessarily form a linear pair.\n\nTo see why, consider the diagram shown below:\n\n![](images/29/29ff0dfefb0b535342f4e8060b37fb2404983fc150681bb02d18fa6e5b00f899.png)\n\nAlthough the angles are supplementary (the sum of their measures is\n\n$$\n120^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\n), they do not form a linear pair of angles since they are not adjacent." 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111928 1065 9518 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111928 1066 #uuid "ea9626ef-f56f-5668-8f47-81a9f68c9db4" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111928 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111929 1064 "We can use a protractor to **rotate** a point by a certain angle about another point.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's rotate the point $A$ below by an angle of $80^\\circ$ **counterclockwise** around the point $O$.\n\n![](images/59/59eaa91598d8c3719c68fd628d6f87122111e62b6c9556ce1dc9231b085f3f98.png)\n\n**Step 1.** We draw a ray $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{OA}$\n\n![](images/ef/ef37c4f3e1c4f18885494b9ec06def176d0f69810f111fe9ba1e32824b34b3e9.png)\n\n**Step 2.** Using a protractor, draw a **counterclockwise** angle $\\angle {BOA}$ which measures $80^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/99/99720a18dd464f766a22ca322d4ef27ed781ac9462d089cfcc280b0c24a00dc2.png)\n\n**Step 3.** Using a compass, we mark a point $A'$ on $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{OB}$ such that the segment $\\overline{OA'}$ is congruent to $\\overline{OA}$.\n\n![](images/5d/5d34da0dcb10c41d1797be02d4a1b6c8e0b95c034a8b8aa9b026ffbacf282aa9.png)\n\nThe point $A'$ is the image of $A$ under a rotation of $80^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the point $O$. We're done!" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111929 1065 9810 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111929 1066 #uuid "d1a8eb7d-4c01-5485-b07b-a333ac5bc8fb" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111929 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111930 1064 "To rotate a polygon about a point, we rotate each of its vertices and then connect the vertices with line segments.\n\nFor example, suppose the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ below is rotated by $135^\\circ$ clockwise about the point $C$. What is the resulting polygon?\n\n![](images/e3/e3f62e35b791eea932c75f0674f60d1e91b2ea0e7b556e7096efacca83d8c9ca.png)\n\nTo rotate the triangle, we rotate each vertex and connect the resulting vertices using line segments.\n\nFirst, let's rotate the point $B$. Placing a protractor on the ray $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{CB}$, we draw an angle of $135^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/db/db87f2e01aabf220b3a3384b2a9cae5e9daf0613b7f7ba7f51d9f26f8c524238.png)\n\nUsing a compass on the second side of the angle, we mark a point $B'$ such that $\\overline{CB} \\cong \\overline{CB'}$.\n\n![](images/fd/fd636cdf864ac6c328fa2e0a33238ccb5784e6e94c068c947a638db64b92453a.png)\n\nWe use the same method to rotate $A$, which gives $A'$ as the resulting point.\n\n![](images/b7/b76e1820bd1dd19768ece6ca28da42c56106c924ecbe3f705a438680770bb573.png)\n\nFinally, we note that the position of $C$ doesn't change as it's the center of the rotation.\n\nOur rotated triangle $\\triangle A'B'C$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/d3/d35a9248d01bc61f5676148d1f9a5a6f3f9b7b35820518b930d9322be38729ef.png)" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111930 1065 9811 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111930 1066 #uuid "c0d2b6b3-ec22-59db-9e19-a57f88c00cdc" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true] [17592186111930 1067 "Rotating Polygons" 13194139533355 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"__map/00000000000000000000" 17592186107189 "__map/00000000000000000001" 17592186107190 "__map/00000000000000000002" 17592186107191 "__map/00000000000000000003" 17592186107192 "__map/00000000000000000004" 17592186107193 "__map/00000000000000000005" 17592186107194 "__map/00000000000000000006" 17592186107195 "__map/00000000000000000007" 17592186107196 "__map/00000000000000000008" 17592186107197 "__map/00000000000000000009" 17592186107198 "__map/00000000000000000010" 17592186107199 "__map/00000000000000000011" 17592186107200 "__map/00000000000000000012" 17592186107201 "__map/00000000000000000013" 17592186107202 "__map/00000000000000000014" 17592186107203 "__map/00000000000000000015" 17592186107204 "__map/00000000000000000016" 17592186107205 "__map/00000000000000000017" 17592186107206 "__map/00000000000000000018" 17592186107207 "__map/00000000000000000019" 17592186107208 "__map/00000000000000000020" 17592186107209 "__map/00000000000000000021" 17592186107210 "__map/00000000000000000022" 17592186107211 "__map/00000000000000000023" 17592186107212 "__map/00000000000000000024" 17592186107213 "__map/00000000000000000025" 17592186107214 "__map/00000000000000000026" 17592186107215 "__map/00000000000000000027" 17592186107216 "__map/00000000000000000028" 17592186107217 "__map/00000000000000000029" 17592186107218 "__map/00000000000000000030" 17592186107219 "__map/00000000000000000031" 17592186107220 "__map/00000000000000000032" 17592186107221 "__map/00000000000000000033" 17592186107222 "__map/00000000000000000034" 17592186107223 "__map/00000000000000000035" 17592186107224 "__map/00000000000000000036" 17592186107225 "__map/00000000000000000037" 17592186107226 "__map/00000000000